AB  - Successful treatment of depression includes the remission of depressive symptoms and an improvement in psychosocial functioning. Objective: To compare the effects of a combination treatment with Interpersonal Psychotherapy and medication versus standard treatment on social and interpersonal adjustment. Methods: 105 depressive inpatients randomized to "Interpersonal Psychotherapy plus pharmacotherapy" or "pharmacotherapy plus clinical management" were evaluated after 5 weeks of treatment, and 3 and 12 months after discharge. Results: Combination therapy revealed a clear advantage compared with standard treatment regarding clinician-rated global psychosocial functioning which was explained by improvement of depressive symptoms. Self-rated social and interpersonal adjustment did not show significant differences between the two treatment groups. Conclusion: The addition of brief Psychotherapy for depressive inpatients did not bring any inprovements over standard psychiatric treatment regarding social and interpersonal functioning.
