AB  - Anger, irritability, depression, boredom and other indices of negative affect have all been implicated in a stress-induced pathway to relapse in alcohol dependence. Treatment programs seek to redress these affective difficulties acutely through medical stabilization and pharmacological means and through psychological interventions. Continuing care resources too, such as, Alcoholics Anonymous (AA), place central importance on successful affect regulation, especially anger, in order to prevent relapse ("Resentment is the number one offender. It destroys more alcoholics than anything else. ... If we were to live we had to be free of anger"; AA, 2001, pg 64; 66). However, anger has not been investigated as a potential mechanism through which AA facilitates improved alcohol use outcomes. Using lagged, HLM analyses, controlling for static and time-varying confounds, this study investigated whether AA participation mobilized changes in anger and whether such changes explained AA-related benefit (R21AA016762, Kelly, PI). Alcohol dependent adults (N = 1,726), receiving treatment as part of a multisite clinical trial were assessed on state-trait anger (STAXI: Spielberger, 1988), and on frequency of AA attendance and alcohol use (Form 90: Miller, 1996) at intake, 3, 6, 9, 12, and 15 month follow-ups. Findings revealed substantially elevated levels of anger compared to the general population which decreased significantly following treatment and over the ensuing 12 months, but nevertheless remained high relative to the general population. AA attendance was associated with better alcohol outcomes over time and higher anger was associated with heavier alcohol use. However, AA attendance was unrelated to anger. Although support was not found for the notion that AA may improve drinking outcomes by reducing anger there was strong convergence between AA's explicit emphasis on anger (AA, 2001) and the findings in this sample: anger appears to be a serious and enduring problem among those suffering from alcohol dependence and is consistently related to heavy alcohol consumption. Measurement, sampling, or assessment time frame, factors may have contributed to the lack of association between AA and anger, but results suggest that while AA is related to better outcomes, by itself it may be insufficient to alleviate the suffering and alcohol-related risks specifically associated with anger.
