AB  - Background: Indices of negative affect, such as depression and irritability, have been implicated in stress-induced pathways to alcohol relapse. Developmental models of therapeutic change suggest symptom remediation as being a critical mediator in the change process. Thus, addiction treatment interventions often target these symptoms directly or indirectly to facilitate abstinence and recovery. Empirically-supported continuing care resources, such as Alcoholics Anonymous (AA), also emphasize reducing negative affect to reduce relapse risk (AA, 1953), yet little research has examined whether AA helps individuals recover through remediation of such symptoms. Method(s): Using lagged, controlled, HLM and mediational analyses, this study investigated whether AA participation mobilized changes in depression and whether such changes explained AA-related benefit (R21AA016762, Kelly, PI). Alcohol dependent adults (N = 1,726), receiving treatment as part of a clinical trial were assessed at intake, 3, 6, 9, 12, and 15 months. Result(s): Findings revealed substantially elevated levels of depression compared to the general population which decreased over the ensuing 15 months. AA participation was associated with better outcomes and greater depression was associated with heavier and more frequent drinking. However, AA attendance was unrelated to reductions in depressive symptoms. Conclusion(s): Although support was not found for reduction in depressive symptoms as a mechanism of AA's effects negative affect appears to be an enduring problem that is consistently correlated with alcohol misuse. Measurement, sampling, or assessment time frame factors may have contributed to the lack of association between AA and depression, but results suggest AA benefits its members through other means.
