AB  - Objective: Despite advances in the development of efficacious treatments for adolescents with substance use disorder (SUD), relapse remains common following an index treatment episode. Community continuing care resources, such as Alcoholics Anonymous (AA) and Narcotics Anonymous (NA), have been shown to be helpful and cost-effective recovery resources among adults with SUD, but little is known about the clinical utility and effectiveness of these groups for adolescents, despite widespread treatment referrals. Method(s): Adolescents (N=127; 24% female, 87% White, M age=16.7 years) enrolled in a naturalistic, prospective, study of community outpatient treatment were assessed at intake, and 3-and 6-months later using a battery of standardized and validated measures. Result(s): Approximately one third of youth attended AA/NA meetings in the first 3-months of follow-up which was predicted by a personal goal of abstinence, prior AA/NA attendance, and prior treatment experiences. Controlled multiple regression analyses, revealed an independent effect of AA/NA participation on abstinence, in both contemporaneous and lagged models, which persisted over and above the effects of pre-treatment AA/NA attendance, prior treatment, abstinence self-efficacy, an abstinence goal, and concomitant outpatient treatment participation. Conclusion(s): Results suggest that, similar to findings comparing adult outpatients to inpatients, AA/NA participation is not as common among less severe outpatient youth. Nonetheless, youth attendance appears to potentiate and extend the benefits of typical community outpatient treatment. Given the relative dearth of abstinent individuals and recovery-specific supports among same-aged peers in the community, these resources may provide a rare, concentrated, cost effective, social recovery resource for young people.
