AB  - AA purports to aid recovery through mobilizing spiritual/religious (S/R) practices and beliefs, yet this claim remains contentious and has been only rarely investigated. This study (NIAAA R21AA016762) examined the relationship between S/R and outcome, and evaluated whether AA was associated with enhancing S/R, as AA claims, and the extent to which any increase is responsible for potential beneficial outcomes. Alcohol dependent adults (N=1,706) participating in an RCT were assessed at intake, and 3, 6, 9, 12, and 15 months. S/R was found to increase significantly in the sample over the follow-up. Also, greater AA attendance was associated with larger increases in S/R, especially for those initially low on this measure at intake after controlling for covariates. Lagged mediational analyses revealed AA was also robustly associated with better subsequent alcohol outcomes, which was partially mediated by increases in S/R. This study complements prior results suggesting S/R is associated with improved outcomes and indicates that AA attendance mobilizes such practices, which in turn lead to better outcomes. Findings add support to AA's own emphasis on spiritual practice as an active ingredient in recovery-related change.
