AB  - Alcoholmisuse increases risk for suicide attempts (Norstrom & Rossow, 2016) and death (Kaplan et al., 2014). Suicidality (e.g., suicidal ideation (SI), attempt) is higher among those accessing substance use treatment (Bakker & Vaglum, 2007). Impulsive suicide attempts predict post-treatment alcohol relapse (Wojnar et al., 2008).With relatively low base rates of suicidality, large samples are needed to understand differences between adults in substance use treatment with varying suicidality. Understanding the relationship between alcohol use and suicidality during and after treatment is clinically important, particularly for intensive outpatient settings where duration is limited and patients are at high relapse risk (McCarty et al., 2014). This study aimed to: 1) determine whether level and recency of suicidality is related to severity of alcohol use and alcohol use problems among patients in intensive outpatient treatment for substance use disorders, and 2) examine whether baseline suicidality predicts alcohol-related problems at post-treatment and at 3- and 6-months post-treatment. Data are from6 randomized controlled trials (N = 1,190) evaluating contingency management for substance use. All patients received standard intensive outpatient (IOP) treatment delivered by community clinics; roughly half also received contingency management. Participants were categorized into one of five groups: no suicidality (58%), recent SI (5.5%), past SI (9.8%), recent attempt (2.2%), and past attempt (24.5%). There were no significant suicide group differences by treatment condition at baseline, nor were there significant treatment group by suicide group interactions on outcomes of interest. ANOVAs testing baseline suicidality group differences showed that those with recent SI reported significantly higher alcohol use problems compared to all groups, except those with recent attempt (F = 3.02, p < 0.05, gp 2=0.1). Longitudinal analyses using generalized estimating equations showed decreases in alcohol problems at follow-ups (Wald v2=5.88, p = 0.05). The significant main effect of suicidality group indicated that individuals with recent suicide attempt reported higher alcohol use problems over time (OR = 1.04, p < 0.05). Time by suicidality interactions were not significant. Findings highlight the severity of alcohol use among adults with recent SI and continued alcohol use problems of those with recent suicide attempts. Adjunctive supports to IOP, specifically around alcohol, are indicated for adults with recent suicide attempt. Integrated alcohol and suicidality treatments are indicated.
