AB  - Intoxication during sex and binge drinking increase sexual risk behaviors and HIV (Baliunas et al., 2009). Intoxication lowers intent to use safer sex practices (Rehm et al., 2012). Understanding if sexual risk behaviors dampen response to IOP is critical to directing service priorities. Gender (Justman et al., 2012; SAMHSA, 2019) and age (SAMHSA, 2019; Stahler et al., 2019) likely impact these relationships. Data from a 12-week randomized trial (n = 114) comparing standard IOP and IOP with contingency management reinforcing attendance (IOP+CM) among adults with alcohol use disorder were examined to assess if gender, age, and treatment group moderate relationships between baseline sexual risk and alcohol treatment response. All participants wore the secure continuous remote alcohol monitor (SCRAMx-). Treatment response outcomes were time in treatment, longest duration attended (LDAtt), self-reported binge drinking days, and SCRAMx-based longest duration abstinent (LDA) and percent drinking days. In gender analyses, sexual risk was associated with shorter LDA (b=-0.28, p < 0.01), and more percent drinking days (b=0.23, p < 0.05), binge drinking days (b=0.20, p < 0.05), and time in treatment (b=0.94, p < 0.01). There was a sexual risk by group interaction on time in treatment (b=-0.83, p = 0.01); sexual risk was associated withmore time in treatment in IOP+CM only (b=0.40, p < 0.05; IOP: b=-0.09, p = 0.60). In age analyses, IOP+CM showed fewer percent drinking days (b=-0.08, p = 0.05). Sexual risk was associated with percent drinking days (b=0.26, p < 0.05). Treatment group interacted with age on LDAtt (b=1.23), LDA (b=1.41), and time in treatment (b=1.30, p's<0.05). Older age was associated with increased LDAtt (b=0.35, p < 0.01) in IOP+CM, but not IOP (b=-0.02, p = 0.90). Younger age was associated with shorter LDA in IOP (b=-0.27, p < 0.05), but not IOP+CM (b=0.20, p = 0.11). In IOP, younger age was associated with time in treatment (b=-0.24, p = 0.09); in IOP+CM, older age was associated with treatment length (b=0.24, p = 0.08). There were main effects of sexual risk (b=0.97, p = 0.05) and sexual risk by age interactions (b=-0.83, p < 0.05) on binge drinking days. Sexual risk was positively associated with binge drinking in older (b=0.57, p = 0.23), but not younger adults (b=-0.14, p = 0.77). Results indicate that sexual risk behaviors are associated with worse alcohol treatment response, depending on age. Treatment enhanced to address sexual risk behaviors may improve treatment response.
