AB  - An investigation was conducted concerning the mental health of unemployed disabled people attending an Employment Rehabilitation Centre (ERC) in the UK. The study was done to: 1. confirm previous research on the negative effect of unemployment on mental health and to better understand the relationship by determining the degree to which it is affected by levels of disability and the length of time previously unemployed, and 2. examine the degree of success of ERCs in improving individual mental health and the extent to which any improvement is associated with prior disability and performance and time spent on the ERC course. The sample for the study was based on all clients entering an ERC in the Midlands during a 6-month period in 1981. Data were based on normal intake information on clients. The results show that unemployed people at the start of an ERC course report low levels of mental health. This was particularly true for severely physically and psychologically disabled people and for older clients. Results showed a significant improvement in mental health for all categories of individuals who completed the course. These improvements were associated with rated performance and with length of time spent on the course.
