AB  - The purpose of this study was to evaluate an AIDS education program at a day treatment center for persons with chronic mental illness. Forty-three clients were randomly assigned to four groups, two experimental groups and two control groups, and taught an AIDS prevention program. Risk Assessments were administered before the intervention, immediately after the intervention, and one month after treatment had concluded. The treatment consisted of eight training sessions, two sessions per week, in which clients viewed slides illustrating risk reduction behaviors, learned how to use condoms, clean needles, and practiced communication and negotiation skills using role plays. The program used lecture and interactive small discussion groups. The Risk Assessment contained seven sets of questions used to determine a client's risk factors for acquiring AIDS. These were: (a) Medical History, (b) Sexual History, (c) Sexual Behavior, (d) Trauma, (e) Substance Abuse, (f) Skills, and (g) AIDS Knowledge. The scores on the Skills and AIDS Knowledge variables were analyzed using t-tests to determine differences in the groups' skills before and after the intervention. One-way analyses of variance (ANOVAs) were performed to analyze possible relationships between the demographic variables gender, ethnicity, age, and education, and each of the seven subscores on the Risk Assessment. Results of the t-tests showed significant differences in the scores among the pre-Risk Assessment, the post-Risk Assessment, and the follow-up Risk Assessment. Training improved client's knowledge and skills. The ANOVAs revealed no significant differences in risk factors as a function of age, education, or ethnicity. There were significant differences in the risk factors on Sexual Behavior and Trauma as a function of gender, with women having higher risk factors. There were also trainer changes in attitudes about the efficacy and practice of HIV/AIDS training. The major conclusion of the study is that an educational intervention to teach AIDS prevention to the chronically mentally ill can be effective in changing knowledge and behavior. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2020 APA, all rights reserved)
