AB  - Introduction: The purpose of this study was to evaluate the drinking history and cognitive function of patients visiting two different institutions, to design an effective treatment approach. The therapeutic approach for a patient with alcohol dependence should match the patient's characteristics, determined before the treatment initiation. However, most services provided for these patients by hospitals and community counseling centers do not usually differ. Method(s): Alcohol-dependent patients visiting the Pusan Community Addiction Management Center (PCAM) and the Department of Psychiatry, Pusan National University Yangsan Hospital (PNUYH) were included as study subjects and investigated for demographics and drinking history. Additionally, we performed neuropsychological testing to evaluate IQ, memory, and executive function such as the Planning Ability score and percentage of those who show impaired executive function using the IQ-EQ >= 1SD. Result(s): Subjects of our study were 19 clients from the PNUYH and 28 clients from the PCAM. All subjects were male. Significant differences were found in terms of duration of drinking and blackouts between the subjects visiting the PCAM and the PNUYH (drinking duration (years), 33.14 +/- 13.75 vs. 22.74 +/- 13.47, p<0.05 and blackout duration (years), 18.43 +/- 13.21 vs. 9.79 +/- 9.41, p<0.05). Subjects visiting the PCAM tended to start drinking at an earlier age than those visiting the PNUYH (16.93 +/- 4.50 vs. 20.95 +/- 9.67, p<0.10). The Planning Ability was significantly reduced in subjects visiting the PCAM compared with those who visited the PNUYH (2.68 +/- 1.06 vs. 3.37 +/- 0.76, p<0.05). The higher percentage of clients visiting PCAM tended to show impaired executive function in terms of IQ-EQ >= 1SD (32% vs 11%, p<0.10) Conclusion(s): These findings suggest that the patients visiting a community counseling center started drinking earlier, had been drinking longer, had experienced blackouts longer, and had poorer cognitive function than patients visiting the university hospital. Thus, different therapeutic services should be offered based on these findings.
