AB  - Introduction: Caretaker exhaustion present in multiple forms: physical, mental, and emotional. As physicians, we often focus on caring for the patient and forget about the wellbeing of those around them. This case illustrates caretaker burnout and provides ways to address the problem. Case Report: 71yo male with alcoholic liver disease, GI bleed, esophagitis, neuropathy, falls, dementia, and homebound, is seen at home for a follow up. The Patient drinks significant amounts of alcohol without plans for discontinuation. When priorities and goals of care were addressed in the past, he was aware of his progressive deterioration from alcohol use. He stopped participating physical therapy, and, as a result, became more deconditioned. He eventually stopped bathing due to fear of falling on a hard tile. Ironically, when asked what he would want in the immediate future, he reported that he would like to see improvement in his health. He wants to walk again, stop the progression of his alcohol induced diseases, and develop a better appetite. Yet, he is stubborn and unwilling to compromise his current lifestyle, which leaves him with a poor prognosis. In the setting of the patient's multi-complexities, his primary caretaker, his wife, reports frustration. The wife has been with the patient for many decades and knows his condition very well. The family used to have a home health aide which was extremely helpful but was eventually fired, thus, at this point, the wife is the sole caretaker at home. She appeared hopeless and overwhelmed. Discussion(s): In the United States, there are approximately 15 million caregivers. It is reported that 56.1% of them experienced caregiver burden. Depression, anxiety, and self-reported poor health were common result of burnout. Fortunately, there are many resources available today to alleviate the problem. The most common way to help the caregiver is to obtain home health aide. Adult day care, caregiver support group programs, and local agency of aging are other available resources. For our case, it was crucial to realize that patient could not be helped with interventions targeted at him. Instead, it was the wife that we needed to help to optimize patient's health. We offered spiritual counseling, support groups, and helped reinstate HHA to offset the caregiver burden. The best way to help the patient was to help his wife because patient was unwilling to help himself. This case illustrates the fact that we need to always remember that those around the patient are important pillars to patient wellness.
