AB  - Background: Most studies on the predictors and effectiveness of community treatment orders (CTOs) are restricted to health-related variables and do not consider predictors from birth or forensic contacts using criminal justice databases. Objective(s): To use linked administrative health and criminal justice data for a birth cohort in Queensland to investigate the characteristics and outcomes of people placed on CTOs. Method(s): CTOs were identified from administrative data for hospital admissions and community mental health (MH) service contacts for a population birth cohort from 1990 (n = 45,141). These were linked with administrative court records and followed up to age 23-24 years. Prevalence rates of psychiatric admissions, community MH service contacts and finalised court appearances were examined by Indigenous status and sex. We used logistic regression analyses to examine predictors of CTO placement and Tobit regression analyses for predictors of health and criminal justice outcomes at 1 year follow-up. Finding(s): There were 211 cases (0.5% of the cohort) identified as having been subject to a CTO by age 23- 24 years. Indigenous Australians were more likely to be subject to a CTO but this became non-significant after adjustment. Serious mental illnesses were the strongest predictors of CTO placement (adjusted odds ratio [ORadj] = 4.07, 2.77-5.99) followed by a court appearance (ORadj = 1.99, 1.28-3.09). The CTO placement was associated with greater, not lower, subsequent psychiatric hospital admissions, inpatient bed-days and community MH service contacts, and more court appearances. Conclusion(s): Both clinical and forensic variables can determine CTO placement with no subsequent reductions in subsequent health service or criminal justice contacts.
