AB  - Introduction The various changes following a stroke threaten the psychosocial well-being of stroke patients. Therefore, a psychosocial intervention has been developed for stroke patients living in the community. It consists of eight individ-ual sessions, and is delivered by specially trained nurses or occupational therapists using work sheets to address significant issues after stroke. The intervention has been tested in an RCT study with 166 participants receiving the intervention. Developing and evaluating complex health interventions call for both quantitative and qualitative methods. Objectives To gain an in depth understanding of the participants lived experience of the interventions influence on the adjustment process after stroke. Methods Upon completion of their RCT-study participation, 19 persons with stroke in the RCT's intervention group took part in the qualitative interviews. A phenomenological hermeneutic framework inspired by Ricoeur's philosophy was chosen to interpret the meaning of the participants lived experience of the intervention. Results The interviews revealed that long-term professional support in the communities health service systems was inadequate. The empathetic relationship with the experienced intervention personnel filled inn this gap and influenced positively on the adjustment process. The systematic engagement with the work sheets together with the reflections on their illness experiences released participants potentials to resume every-day life within their limitations and lessened their loneliness, anxiety and depressive reactions. The participants welcomed the intervention personnels overall guidance and their support to seek health and social care services. When including the partner, the intervention had opened up for greater openness within the relationship. Although some of the participants with minor disabilities expressed no need for the intervention, they recommended it to future stroke survivors. Conclusion The intervention's salutogenic approach combined with guided self-determination and a narrative approach seem to be convenient to support the adjustment process of the persons with stroke. The empathetic attitude of the intervention personnel is significant to release their potentials and to fight loneliness, anxiety and depressive reactions.
