AB  - Background: The prevention of postoperative complications is of prime importance for patients' postoperative outcome and for saving health‐care costs. Preoperative patient education may prevent postoperative complications and improve patients' well‐being, but evidence for its efficacy in patients undergoing advanced and extended visceral surgery is poor. The aim of the PEDUCAT pilot trial was to evaluate the feasibility of cluster randomization and the impact of a preoperative patient education on the morbidity, mortality and patient reported outcomes in patients scheduled for elective major procedures in visceral surgery. Materials and Methods: This was a cluster randomized controlled, open‐label clinical trial with an exploratory study design. Adult patients (≥18 years) who were scheduled for elective major visceral surgery for any reason were eligible for inclusion and were randomly assigned in clusters to intervention group (preoperative, standardized, one‐hour‐long seminar to learn health‐related exercises and preventive measures after surgery in addition to a standard information brochure), or to control group receiving the center's standard patient care (information brochure only). Outcome measures were postoperative morbidity defined as pneumonia, deep vein thrombosis, pulmonary embolism, burst abdomen and in‐hospital fall as well as postoperative pain (Brief Pain Inventory, BPI), anxiety and depression (Hospital Anxiety and Depression Scale, HADS), patient satisfaction (unvalidated questionnaire), quality of life (12‐item Short Form Survey, SF‐12), length of hospital stay, and postoperative mortality within 30 days after the index operation. Statistical analysis was primarily by intention to treat. This trial is registered with the German Clinical Trials Register (DRKS00004226). Funding was granted by the B. Braun Stiftung. Results: 248 patients were randomized in 66 clusters to intervention (142 patients) or control group (106 patients). In the intervention group, 4 patients not undergoing surgery were excluded from analysis. Allocation of hospital wards instead of individual patients facilitated study conduct and reduced confusions regarding group allocation. Postoperative outcomes were similar with low frequencies of complications in both study groups (intervention vs. control group: postoperative morbidity 10.8% vs. 12.5%, p = 0.704; 30‐day mortality 1.4% vs. 1.9%, p = 0.790) and also highly satisfied patients represented by good and comparable results of patient reported outcomes in both intervention and control group (intervention vs. control group: mean physical health score 32.2 (±7.3) vs. 33.9 (±7.5), p = 0.179; mean mental health score: 43.4 (±11.7) vs. 43.8 (±12.4), p = 0.848). Conclusion: Cluster randomization was feasible in the setting of preoperative patient education and reduced the risk of contamination effects. The results of this study indicate good postoperative outcomes in patients undergoing major visceral surgery without superiority of a preoperative patient education compared to the standard patient care in a highvolume center. This may be explained by a high‐quality standard patient care provided by the professional teamwork between the expertized nursing staff and the medical doctors. However, to prove equivalence of both groups or non‐inferiority of the preoperative patient education a confirmatory trial would be necessary.
