AB  - This study investigated the potential increased vulnerability of women in their psychological adjustment to breast cancer who also reported a history of child abuse. Hypotheses explored factors known and suspected to affect psychological adjustment to breast cancer. These factors included age, income, education, marital status, cancer treatment, stage of disease, history of child abuse, and sexual orientation. The primary hypotheses were that a history of child abuse and identification as lesbian or bisexual would predict an adverse adjustment style, poorer quality of life, greater distress, and increased post-traumatic stress symptomatology. Data were gathered from women with breast cancer who participated in a psychosocial intervention offered by the University of California, San Francisco and California Pacific Medical Center. The sample included 95 women with Stage II breast cancer, who were primarily Caucasian, middle class, and had an average age of 49 years. These 95 women were divided into two groups based on whether they reported child abuse. Forty-three disclosed that they were victims of childhood abuse. Measures to assess participants' psychological adjustment to breast cancer included the Mini-Mental Adjustment to Cancer Scale (Mini-MAC), the Functional Assessment of Chronic Illness Therapy-Breast Cancer Version (FACIT-B), the Profile of Mood States (POMS), and the Post-Traumatic Stress Disorder Checklist-Civilian Version (PCL-C). Contrary to expectation, neither history of child abuse nor lesbian or bisexual identification accounted for a statistically significant amount of variance in participants' adjustment to breast cancer. The sole factor that demonstrated a significant positive association was age, with younger age predicting more distress and PTSD symptomatology. Specifically, younger age accounted for 11% of the variance in Depression/Dejection and Tension/Anxiety scores on the POMS and 11% of the variance in Post-Traumatic Stress Symptomatology. Furthermore, age was the only factor that accounted for a statistically significant amount of variance in participants' positive changes in psychological adjustment immediately following the psychosocial intervention. Thus, younger women suffer more in the face of breast cancer, and they also benefit more than older women from psychosocial interventions. Future research is indicated to determine how demographic, psychological, and cancer-related variables impact adjustment to breast cancer and how those variables might be most effectively addressed in treatment. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
