AB  - Stress among university students continues to be a concern for academic programs, as it is can negatively impact students' physical and mental well-being and lead to functional impairment. Thus, it has become increasingly important to investigate ways in which students can cope with stress. Much of the research shows that personality is associated with level of perceived stress and with use of adaptive coping strategies. Studies show stress management interventions can effectively reduce perceived stress and increasing adaptive coping skills. Furthermore, group-based interventions provide the additional benefit of social support. The purpose of this paper was to conduct a small exploratory study reviewing a group-based stress management intervention (SMART). Participants were 11 students who participated in SMART at the Roosevelt University Counseling Center. Measures were the Perceived Stress Scale, Ways of Coping (Revised), and the NEO-Five Factor Inventory-3. Contrary to expectations, results showed there was no significant association between coping strategies and neurotic personality traits prior to the intervention. There was also no significant increase in use of active coping strategies in participants post-intervention, and the effectiveness of the intervention did not vary based on neurotic personality traits. However, results showed a decrease in self-reported perceived stress following the intervention, though the difference was not statistically significant. These findings suggest that the results are inconclusive and may have been impacted by the small sample size. Future research should explore how stress impacts academic performance, psychiatric diagnoses, and physical health problems in college students. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
