AB  - Australia has fallen behind overseas counterparts in the area of geropsychology, particularly clinical geropsychology. Older Australians are not getting the level of psychological services needed either (1) on the basis of their status as a large and growing proportion of the population or (2) in terms of the level of psychological problems they experience. Research showing the efficacy of psychological interventions in this age group is briefly reviewed. Given the established need and demonstrated efficacy of psychological interventions in this age group, reasons for the paucity of psychological services to the Australian aged are explored. Finally, possible solutions to this problem are presented, with an emphasis on the need for increased attention to geropsychology in clinical psychology training. The latter includes the need for students to be exposed to older adults in their practical placements. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
