AB  - Rural veterans represent an underserved and hard-to-reach population facing many barriers to receiving timely, culturally-relevant mental health care. These include psychosocial, logistical and structural barriers (e.g., limited availability of well-trained service providers, long wait times, and lack of transportation). Due to such barriers, rural veterans with mental health disorders are significantly less likely receive individual or group psychotherapy than urban veterans. Commonly presenting problems of rural veterans include affective disorders, PTSD, substance use disorders, suicidal ideation, anger management and aggression difficulties, poor interpersonal functioning, and chronic health complaints. Deficits in emotion regulation and distress tolerance play etiological and maintaining roles in these presenting problems. While treatment interventions have been developed for veterans, to date none have been developed specifically for rural veterans nor their common barriers to care. The primary aim of this dissertation was to examine the feasibility of adapting extant, empirically-supported treatments to create a single, transdiagnostic protocol for rural veterans which targets emotion regulation and maximizes access to culturally-sensitive care. This dissertation demonstrates that development of such a group is feasible. The protocol, Stop Your Emotions from Controlling Your Life (the STOP Group), uses a transdiagnostic approach to target deficits in emotion regulation, offers drop-in sessions, and is delivered via telemental health. The STOP Group is the first group therapy protocol developed specifically for this population. Its transdiagnostic approach and focus on emotion regulation is consistent with the likely direction the field is moving and facilitates access to care while minimizing costs. The therapist manual is designed to facilitate dissemination. This dissertation does not address the feasibility of implementing the protocol, the acceptability of the group as rated by the treatment providers or veterans, or the group's ability to effectively target veterans' presenting problems and improve their capacity to effectively regulate their emotions. All of these variables will need to be evaluated in the future. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
