AB  - This chapter reports on the components of an educational support group supported by an active case management strategy used successfully with grandparent caregivers of children with developmental disabilities. Participants were 97 grandparents (aged 40-82 yrs) caring for at least 1 child (aged 2-25 yrs) with a developmental disability or delay. The project used local community-based multicultural agencies to deliver the intervention to a largely African-American and Latino group of grandparent caregivers. After 6 support group sessions, compared to controls, symptoms of depression were significantly reduced and grandparents' self-appraisal of empowerment and master over their caregiving situation significantly increased. The results also illustrate that the provision of transportation and respite, choice among session topics, and the flexible scheduling of sessions will not only lead to steady support group attendance, but will also attract and retain the most needy caregivers. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2020 APA, all rights reserved)
