AB  - This study reflects regional psychiatric epidemiological data, as recorded by admissions/dismissals from the regional psychiatric hospital, the Landesnervenkrankenhaus Valduna. With 420 beds it is the only psychiatric unit for the Austrian province of Vorarlberg, a catchment-area of approxomately 330000 inhabitants. The data concern hospitalized patients only and are collected at discharge on standardized forms. This paper covers the period 1984-1988 and the diagnoses ICD-9 295.x and 296.x. We report on interrelationships of 7267 patients' main diagnoses and demographic, social, educational, vocational and other data. Contrary to the catchment area's sex-distribution, more male than female patients were admitted (55.3% m, 44.7% f), overall 48% were first admissions. Diagnose-specific sex distribution patterns are apparent: In schizophrenia the ratio is nearly equal, affective diagnoses show a preponderance of female patients. As the hospital is concerned only with adults, the lowest age bracket ranges from 21-30 years, predominant in the endogenous psychoses. A high number is also attained by affectively ill patients 56-65 years old. The highest number of singles (708 = 69%) was registered for schizophrenics, as it was for divorced persons in the alcoholics' group. General practitioners seem to be the most important source of referrals and focus for discharged patients, although the area has a per capita density of psychiatrists of 1/20000. Austria itself has one of the world's best developed social-security systems and the province is reknown for its dense social network. General hospitals as main referrers of suicide-attempts (of various causes) indicate a deficit of some basic services. This is also stressed by the high number of unemployed and invalid pensioners among psychotic patients. Rehabilitation and social measures, social care and divers forms of living-arrangements are needed in various degrees. Low-grade workers make up 54% of our patients and are frequent in schizophrenics. As expected houswifes predominate the affective psychoses (44% of the group), which also registers a high rate of (invalid) pensioners.
