AB  - Introduction/Objective: Home healthcare is post‐acute care services provided to homebound patients in home settings, serving mostly older adults. It is important to ensure and improve home healthcare quality everywhere in the United States. This study leveraged data analytics to understand the similarities and differences between the urban and non‐urban home health agencies in terms of their quality measures. Design/Methodology: The quality measures were obtained from the Medicare Home Health Compare Database from 2014 to 2018 for each agency within the following 3 categories: (1) 6 rates indicating adherence with clinical processes; (2) 7 rates indicating attainment of desirable clinical outcomes; and (3) 2 rates indicating utilization outcomes. Each rate is represented as a number between 0 and 100. Each agency was placed into either urban or non‐urban group based on the classification of zip code in the Rural‐Urban Commuting Area (RUCA) Codes database. For each measure, a Mann‐Whitney U‐test was performed to detect differences because it is a non‐parametric test that is robust to outliers and non‐normal distributions. The null hypothesis states that it is equally likely that a randomly selected rate from 1 group will be less than or greater than the randomly selected rate from the second group. Only the p‐values below 0.05 were considered to reject the null hypothesis and thus represent a difference for the purposes of this study. Below, a difference is denoted with the number of the rate difference and the p‐values obtained from the statistical test, separated by a semicolon in parentheses. Results: Urban agencies were better at teaching foot care to patients with diabetes (0.26; 0.01), teaching patients about drugs (0.53; 1.66E‐07), and checking for flu shot (0.45; 1.10E‐56). Non‐urban agencies were better at starting care timely (0.57; 4.54E‐21) and checking for falls (0.1; 1.99E‐30), depression (0.12; 6.18E‐22), and pneumococcus (1.82; 1.40E‐20). In Category 2, urban agencies were better in improvements in bathing (1.26; 1.34E‐78), pain (2.94; 1.89E‐225), breathing (2.44;1.17E‐150), walking (0.34; 4.31E‐08), and in using drugs (1.56; 7.61E‐77). Non‐urban agencies were better in improvements in healing (0.24; 0.01). There was no difference in improvement in getting in and out of bed (p = 0.35). In Category 3, urban agencies were better in hospital admission (‐1.44; 0) and unplanned emergency room admission (‐1.07; 3.79E‐305). Conclusion/Discussion: The results show that although non‐urban agencies did better in some of the process measures, the clinical outcome measures and utilization measures were much better in urban agencies. These differences can be caused by various factors including the social determinants of health. When it comes to assessing agency performance for value‐based reimbursement purposes, it seems that rurality still remains an important variable for consideration. Furthermore, perhaps, an argument can be made for additional investments to achieve better quality of home care in non‐urban areas. Disclosures: All authors have stated there are no financial disclosures to be made that are pertinent to this abstract.
