AB  - Background: In our recent studies pregabalin was effective in treating opioid withdrawal syndrome [1]. Gabapentin demonstrated some efficacy in treating dependence from alcohol [2] and cannabinoids [3]. Aim(s): To study the efficacy of pregabalin for smoking reduction in patients with alcohol dependence. Method(s): 100 recently detoxified out-patients with alcohol and tobacco dependence were randomly assigned to one of two treatment groups. Patients of the first group (50 subjects; 38 males, 12 females, age (M+/-SE) = 43.3+/-1.7) received pregabalin (150 mg once a day at night time) for 3 months while patients of the second group (50 subjects; 45 males, 5 females, age = 45.9+/-1.4) received identically looking placebo. All patients received standardized manualized weekly counseling (medical management). To assess effect of pregabalin on smoking we measured number of cigarettes per day weekly with Time Line Follow Back (TLFB). Drinking was measured on the weekly basis with TLFB and GGT enzyme activity. Also, craving for alcohol, depression, and anxiety were measured weekly with the number of scales. The study design was double blind placebo controlled and randomized. Result(s): The mean number of cigarettes per day was significantly lower in pregabalin group compared to placebo (14.0+/-0.5 vs 12.0+/-0.3, p<0,01). Kaplan-Meier survival analysis demonstrated significantly higher retention in treatment in pregabalin group (Median (CI) = 12 (10.4-13.6) weeks in pregabalin group vs. 6 (4.5-7.5) in placebo group, Log Rank Mantel-Cox test = 0.005). Proportion of patients who completed treatment in pregabalin group was significantly higher compared to placebo group: 50% vs. 24% (p = 0.01, Fisher exact test). Mean duration of participation in the treatment program was also higher in pregabalin group: 9.1+/- 0.5 weeks vs. 7.1+/-0.5 in placebo (p = 0.006). General linear model demonstrated significant group effect on (1)Total alcohol consumption (TAC) (mean grams of alcohol per day) (F = 10.2; p = 0.01) with lower TAC in pregabalin group on weeks 3, 4, 5 and 7, and (2)On the Number of heavy drinking days (NHDD) (F = 7.9; p = 0.05) with lower NHDD in pregabalin group on the weeks 3-7 and 9. Mean NHDD per patient for the period of participation in the study was lower in pregabalin group (3.6+/-0.7 vs. 6.4+/-0.8; p = 0.009), while the mean number of abstinent (sober) days was higher (55.9+/-3.6 vs. 40.0+/-3.3; p = 0.001). No significant differences between two groups were found on the craving for alcohol, depression, anxiety, and GGT enzyme activity. The rate of adverse events (AE) was 22% in pregabalin group vs. 6.1% in placebo group (p = 0.023). The most common AE were sleepiness, headache, and asthenia. All AE were mild, gradually disappeared, and did not require any medication. Conclusion(s): Results of this study provide evidence that pregabalin in a low does of 150 mg per day is an effective and safe medication for both reduction of smoking and reduction of drinking in patients with alcohol and tobacco dependence.Copyright © 2018
