AB  - Examined the impact of 8 chronic diseases on the health-related quality of life (HRQOL) of 760 Chinese patients (aged 18–94 yrs) of a family medicine clinic in Hong Kong. Each S answered the COOP/WONCA charts and a standard questionnaire on demographic and morbidity data. The likelihood of sub-optimal COOP/WONCA scores of each disease group was compared with that of patients without any of the surveyed diseases. The independent effects of each disease on the COOP/WONCA scores were analyzed by multivariate logistic regression, controlling for demographic variables and comorbidity. Depression increased the risks for sub-optimal scores in all but one COOP/WONCA chart. Hypertension increased the risk of a sub-optimal physical fitness score. Increased risk of limitations in daily activities was associated with stroke, osteoarthritis of the knee, diseases of joints other than the knees and asthma/COPD (chronic obstructive pulmonary disease). Osteoarthritis of the knees also increased the risk of sub-optimal overall health. Depression was the most disabling disease, and osteoarthritis of the knee had more impact on the HRQOL than many other chronic diseases. The lack of adverse effects of diabetes mellitus and heart disease on the HRQOL of Chinese patients deserves further studies. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2019 APA, all rights reserved)
