AB  - Residential substance abuse treatment programs rely on group therapy as the predominant source of treatment and information dissemination for the populations they serve. These groups are typically highly structured and systematically organized throughout the day. This method can become problematic when the needs of the individual differs from the needs of the group; for example, when members of these programs have the presence of a mental health disorder and a substance use disorder, otherwise labelled co-occurring diagnoses. This study interviewed ten participants who met the criteria for co-occurring diagnoses in a residential substance abuse treatment program. Participant responses were coded for themes across the phenomenological dimensions of investment, engagement, utility, and meaning. The central theme that emerged in participants responses was the lack of group utility because of the minimal focus on mental health issues. This lack of utility lead to increased burn-out and disengagement from the group. The other factor most associated to participant disengagement from groups was mismatching levels of investment. There are frequent disruptions and interpersonal disputes when the group is not mutually invested. Given this, all of the participants were preoccupied on group engagement and utility, and these dimensions heavily influenced their investment and meaning in the groups. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
