AB  - Background: Treatment involving agonist medication has been proven to be an effective strategy for treating opioid use disorder (OUD), yet however, waiting for patients to present for treatment is problematic and reactive, partially because patients themselves often delay seeking treatment or are not aware of treatment programs. In this research, we utilize a novel approach combining assertive outreach to identify high-risk individuals and engage them in a comprehensive medical, behavioral, and peer support program. Objective(s): The development of this research study is a result of growing opioid-related overdoses and mortality in Houston, Texas. Psychological theories involving critical time intervention and human motivation suggests that there are certain instances when individuals have higher levels of readiness and commitment to change, especially following critical events, such as an overdose. In this study, we sought to assess if assertive outreach, rapid initiation to medical treatment, behavioral counseling, and peer recovery support can improve patient engagement and retention in treatment. Method(s): The study utilized routine data and referrals from multiple sources, including emergency medical services, courts, police, and social service agencies) to identify high-risk individuals with OUD for potential enrollment. If they met study criteria (eg, acute withdrawal, history of opioid dependence, adult >18 years), they were enrolled in a single-arm clinical trial. Protocol involved initial meetingwith an emergency department physician for same-day induction with buprenorphine, to serve as a temporary bridge until outpatient treatment appointment was confirmed. Each patent were provided with individual and group recovery support with a certified peer recovery coach and scheduled for individual and group behavioral counseling with a professional chemical dependency counselor. The primary dependent variable was the percentage of patients who retained in treatment, drug-free for 30 or more days. Result(s): Of approximately 350 individuals with reported overdoses, the program successfully engaged 75 patients to be enrolled in the study during the initial 9 months (April-December 2018). The majority of enrolled patients were women (66%) with an average age of 35 years. Of these, 81% (61) retained in treatment and recovery, drug-free for >30 days (mean=98 days). There were 4 (4.5%) of patients who had subsequent relapses and no reported subsequent overdoses. Conclusion(s): This study outlines new and innovative ways to identify and connect active opioid users to immediate, evidence-based treatment. Assertive outreach with real-time data on overdoses or high-risk individuals can guide patient identification and create opportunities to engage individuals earlier in the cycle. This program requires partnerships with community agencies, clinics, and recovery centers. We found that assertive outreach followed by comprehensive medical and behavioral treatment is effective at short-term retention in treatment and reducing the high rates of recidivism with OUD. These findings and our lessons learned during the first year can serve as a model to encourage other communities to explore similar strategies to better combat the growing opioid epidemic nationally.
