AB  - A case management model for people living with HIV, utilizing both health facility-based "case managers" and community-based volunteers, has been implemented in Nyagatare District in Rwanda since November 2007. To address the psychosocial needs of clients, case managers make referrals to available counseling services, and community volunteers are trained on active listening and coping counseling. This evaluation explored psychosocial outcomes associated with program exposure by comparing PLHIV with and without contact to case management personnel. Method(s): A household survey among 247 adult "clients" on ARTs assessed basic demographics, exposure to the program; and psychosocial well-being as measured by stigma, marginalization, and depression scales. Multivariate analysis controlled for gender, community volunteer status, whether the client had a case manager or community volunteer, food security, HIV symptom status, and number of household assets. Result(s): About 20% reported lacking a community volunteer, and one-fifth reported not having a case manager. Though stigma and marginalization were generally low among the sample, particularly compared to other studies of similar populations assessed with the same scales, 75% of respondents met the criteria for depression. There were no differences on any psychosocial outcomes among those with and without exposure to case management personnel. Depression was significantly associated with gender (P < .01), with women having considerably higher levels of depression than men. Discussion(s): These results suggest that efforts to mitigate stigma and marginalization are having an effect in Rwanda more generally. However, the current case management model is not fully addressing clients' psychosocial needs. The source of high levels of depression is unknown-perhaps in part due to HIV status and in part to the Rwandan genocide. While case managers and community volunteers may benefit from additional training in this domain, broader efforts to increase the availability of professional counselors may be warranted.
