AB  - The abstracts within this symposium will address the challenging issues of how to implement and evaluate interventions for supporting 'recovery' in mental health services. The recovery approach is the most influential development in mental health services internationally in the last decade, and has been adopted at the policy level in the UK, the USA and many other countries. Although the ideology of recovery has been adopted widely, the empirical evidence base lags behind. Three of the papers within this symposium report early findings from a large, 5 year research project called “REFOCUS: Developing a recovery focus in mental health services in England” led by Dr. Mike Slade which is addressing this knowledge gap. The fourth paper reports upon another project called “Implementing Recovery ‐ Organisational Change (ImROC)” which has developed 10 key organisational challenges that face mental health services that wish to transform their organisation and delivery of services to become 'recovery‐orientated'. The ImROC team is working with mental health providers to produce a methodology to assist organisational change to 'co‐produce' system development, and improve process and outcomes. All presenters will describe how these projects are currently attempting to implement and evaluate recovery‐oriented interventions within NHS mental health services in England. The REFOCUS project is conducting a randomised controlled trial which aims: (1) to establish the effectiveness of the intervention described in the REFOCUS manual, (2) to validate the REFOCUS intervention model, (3) to establish and optimise trial parameters, and (4) to understand the relationship between clinical outcomes and recovery outcomes. It includes a secondary study which is specifically testing the effectiveness of the intervention on a sub‐sample of service users from black backgrounds. The pre‐trial preparation work has involved systematically reviewing the literature and undertaking a narrative synthesis of personal recovery models and theories to produce a conceptual framework of personal recovery. This has been used to provide a conceptual underpinning to the research programme. Specifically, it has been used (i) to inform the development of the intervention model, (ii) to inform the intervention manual contents, (iii) to inform the 'INSPIRE' measure of recovery‐orientation in services, (iv) to guide the selection of outcome measures to be used at baseline and follow up stages of the trial, and (v) to identify the areas the process evaluation should address. The intervention manual also contains an implementation strategy which has been developed to support individual practitioners to introduce and maintain the practice changes. The implementation strategy was partly informed by the findings from a series of focus groups and individual interviews with staff and team managers, held in both the RCT sites, to identify local, contextual blocks and enabling factors to implementing a recovery intervention. The ImROC project aims to assist up to 6 demonstration sites to improve the quality of their local services to support people more effectively to lead meaningful and productive lives ('recovery'). It will use a methodology for promoting recovery through organisational change developed by the Centre for Mental Health. This is based on structured self‐assessment, goal setting, implementation and review and takes a 'whole system' approach. Each organisations will receive an individually‐tailored support package outlined above which includes expert consultancy, access to specialist, user‐led training programmes and materials, and participation in a learning network. Presenters will encourage discussion and debate within session around conceptualisation, methodological and implementation issues around recovery.
