AB  - This study analyzed the relationship between day program participation and quality of life for persons with mental retardation who relocated into the community from institutional living. The study incorporated a secondary analysis of data collected during the closure one of New Jersey's institutions for persons with developmental disabilities. The study also involved the organization of qualitative data into case studies for ten individuals who were in the cohort that left the institution. The study reviewed the concept of quality of life for persons with mental retardation against the historical background of institutional environments, the influence of litigation and legislation, and the deinstitutionalization movement. The study provided a review of applicable literature related to issues that provided the backdrop of social change, including the theory of normalization, social role valorization, role theory, social support, the strengths perspective, quality of life, and the community service system. The study assumed that the service system was established to increase the quality of life of system participants. Various statistical techniques were used to analyze the quantitative data to determine if there were relationships between quality of life variables and day program participation, and between several predictor variables and day program participation. The quantitative methodology findings indicated there were some significant relationships between type of day program (especially employment type) and quality of life. The ten case studies revealed the dynamics of day programs and routines. The study utilized a triangulated approach of data collection. Each subject's day program/routine was reviewed in light of quality of life domains. The qualitative findings suggest that once in the community, the person's quality of life is influenced by the living arrangement, day program participation, and family and friends' interactions. Day programs have a significant effect on one's autonomy, community inclusion, the development of relationships, employment, and learning adaptive skills. Day programs were generally group oriented, offering a pre-determined curriculum, lacking in employment focus, lacking in focus on socially valued roles, and lacking sufficient funds, resources, and space. The study also presents implications for public policy, future research, and for social work practice.
