AB  - A prospective descriptive correlational study was conducted to describe recovery from substance abuse in African American males. The intent was to broaden understanding of recovery from substance abuse in African American males, an understudied population. Relationships among the variables addiction severity, readiness to change, self-efficacy, perception of racism, and sociodemographic variables were examined within a self-care nursing framework within one week of admission to the treatment program and at 30 and 90 days post admission. The sample consisted of African American males who met inclusion criteria, agreed to participate and who entered treatment during the time frame established for data collection, June 2001-December 2001. Of the 55 African American males initially enrolled in the study at baseline, 53 completed the study protocol. Study hypotheses were as follows: (1) There will be relationships among addiction severity, readiness to change, self-efficacy, perception of racism, sociodemographic variables and recovery in African American male substance abusers; and (2) readiness to change and self-efficacy will characterize those patients who maintain abstinence at 90 days. Descriptive statistics were generated for all variables, followed by a correlation matrix and regression analysis. Findings were as follows: The first hypothesis was partially supported. There were significant relationships between self-efficacy, sobriety status, history of relapses and recovery. Support for the second hypothesis was accomplished by dividing the sample into two groups at 90 days. In Group I those who maintained abstinence (abstainers) at 90 days and in Group II were those who relapsed (relapsers). Those in group I (abstainers) were middle class, had a mean age of 54 years, were married, unskilled workers, and attended Alcoholics Anonymous meetings. Those in Group II (relapsers) were also middle class, were younger with a mean age of 45 years, were skilled workers, and did not attend Alcoholics Anonymous meetings. Patients in group I (abstainers) exhibited higher confidence to maintain sobriety in high risk situations scores. Self-efficacy was the sole, predictor of successful recovery, thus partially supporting the second hypothesis.
