AB  - Evaluated clinically significant outcome and consumer satisfaction related to an eight-week cognitive behavioral social training and resolution skills (STARS) therapy group for 7- to 12-year-old boys and girls. The group, developed from a cognitive behavioral developmental model, addressed seven social skill areas. The participants consisted of 30 children ages 7 to 12 years old referred for outpatient services through a hospital's mental health program, and their 20- to 50-year-old caregivers. Sixty percent were Caucasian and 40% were African-American. The children were diagnosed with internalizing (17%), externalizing (60%), or comorbid (23%) disorders. To assure that the participant's initial levels of disturbance represented the general treatment population of the medical center's mental health program, a comparison sample was also collected, which consisted of 23 males and nine females ages 5- to 12-years-old diagnosed with internalizing (3%), externalizing (63%), or comorbid (34%) disorders. Levels of disturbance were measured using the Oppositional, Social Problems, and DSM-IV Hyperactive/Impulsive subscales of the Conners' Parent Rating Scale-Revised: Long (CPRS-R: L). Caregivers of children in the treatment group completed the CPRS-R: L at three times: pre- and post-treatment, and two-year follow-up. Caregivers in the comparison group only completed CPRS-R: L at pre-treatment. Clinically significant outcomes were measured by evaluating the amount of change in the treatment group from pre-treatment to post-treatment, and at two-year follow-up on the three CPRS-R: L subscales, as well as by readmission to mental health services within the two years following the group treatment. Additionally, to evaluate clinically significant outcomes, the researcher used a normative comparison approach. To measure consumer satisfaction with the STARS program at the two-year follow-up period, the researcher analyzed caregivers responses to a satisfaction questionnaire. The results indicate that the STARS intervention improved levels of social functioning, although not all areas of behavioral functioning moved to within the normative range. However, following the STARS intervention, a large percentage of participants did not return for mental health treatment. Further, caregiver satisfaction was significantly related to their perceptions of the quality of the intervention. Regarding attrition, participants who completed STARS were not statistically different from those who terminated treatment. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
