AB  - This article presents encouraging findings from existing preventive interventions in pharmacotherapy, psychotherapy, and psychosocial efforts. These studies highlight the opportunity, yet also the paucity and lack of development, in prevention studies. Many of the described prevention studies only discussed improvement in symptoms or a comparison between an intervention and control group over time. The studies also provide an overview of the potential for prevention and challenges and limitations of this work. The greatest challenge with selected and indicative prevention trials is the need for an adequate follow-up period. It is important to take into account the disabilities, mobility, and cognitive capabilities, in addition to the resiliencies, wisdom, and strengths of the older population in preventive designs. In conclusion, the prevention of geriatric mental disorders is a field laden with challenges, yet ripe for growth, holding potential for great health and economic payoffs. Adapted from the source document.
