AB  - Investigated the degree of psychopathic personality disorder (PPD) in 72 male and 43 female residents (aged 17–54 yrs) in a therapeutic community for the treatment of drug addiction. Ss were in 1 of 5 treatment levels and assessed using the Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory-168 (J. E. Overall et al, 1973), the Neuroticism Scale Questionnaire, and the Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale (WAIS). Findings suggest that treatment does have general effects on presentation of PPD symptoms by drug-dependent persons. This general effect seems to interact significantly with the sex of the person, producing a more favorable outcome for males. However, treatment does not seem related to change in PPD with drug addicts. It is argued that successful treatment outcomes can occur regardless of the personality disorder classification of the person. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2019 APA, all rights reserved)
