AB  - This information packet on self-help programs for persons who are homeless and mentally ill provides a overview of the content of the most relevant literature and an annotated bibliography. The overview of the literature indicates that the philosophy and goals of self-help programs for homeless mentally ill consumers center on self-determination and the offering of services that meet consumers' stated preferences. Multi-service programs have core services that typically include advocacy, peer counseling and support groups, and assistance in meeting basic needs. Other self-help programs may focus on a single service such as housing or employment. A key feature of most self-help programs for homeless mentally ill persons is that they are directed and staffed primarily by currently or formerly homeless mentally ill consumers. The overview discusses staff experience and educational requirements, staff training, organizational structure, funding, recognition and acceptance, community opposition, and program effectiveness. Descriptions of 12 self-help programs represent the range and scope of such programs. The annotated bibliography contains 19 entries of books and journal articles. Information on the cost of materials and how to order them is included.
