AB  - Objective: To explore the effects of WeChat mutual help group on treatment compliance and quality of life during 131I treatment of patients with thyroid cancer. Methods: 103 patients with thyroid cancer who were treated with 131I in our hospital from August 2016 to December 2017 were enrolled in the observation group (52 cases) and the control group (51 cases) according to the random number table method. The control group was treated with standard treatment and routine care. On the basis of this, a WeChat group was established for thyroid cancer patients in the observation group to conduct daily communication and mutual assistance. Treatment compliance, quality of life and self‐rating depression scale (SDS) were compared between the two groups on the day of discharge, and radionuclide emissions were compared between the two groups after six days of admission. Results: The individual quality scores of the quality of life of the observation group were higher than those of the control group on the day of discharge with a statistically significant difference (P<0.05). At the same time, the depression score of the control group was lower than that of the control group (P<0.05). The lifestyle and medication habit compliance of the observation group were better than those of the control group (P<0.05). The compliance rate of radionuclide discharge in the observation group was 96.15% at 6 days after hospitalization, which was higher than that of the control group (88.23%), and the difference was statistically significant (P<0.05). Conclusion: The application of WeChat mutual help group can enhance the patient's treatment compliance, improve the patient's quality of life, and increase the rate of radio nuclide emission. It is worthy of popularization and application.
