AB  - This study examines eight ecological variables that predict cognitive and social competence in two-year-old children of 50 impoverished, unmarried, first-time adolescent mothers. Family Intervention, a covariate in the analyses, was determined by assignment to either the Intervention or Control group: Thirty mother-toddler dyads received social support services; 20 demographically matched control group dyads did not receive services. Other variables included Maternal Race, Maternal Age (at delivery), Maternal Educational Progress, Additional Pregnancy, Maternal Depression (Beck Depression Inventory; Beck & Beamesderfer, 1974), Child Difficultness (Infant Characteristics Questionnaire; Bates, Freeland, & Lounsbury, 1979), and Supportive Home Environment (HOME Inventory; Caldwell & Bradley, 1984). The latter five variables were assessed during the 24-month home visit with each mother and toddler dyad. The study proposed to determine whether a Multiple Risk Score (MRS), computed by summing dichotomized ecological variables (as 'present' or 'absent' risks), was a better predictor of cognitive and social competence than the best subset of non-dichotomized variables. Cognitive competence (Mental Development Index; Bayley, 1969) was best predicted by simultaneous entry of all predictor variables (35%) compared to the best subset (including Family Intervention, Maternal Depression, and Supportive Home Environment at 20%) or to the MRS (8.5%). Social competence, a factor derived from the Infant Behavior Record (Bayley, 1969; Matheny, 1980), was not predicted from any combination of variables, including the MRS. Several interesting associations were found. Intervention group mothers reported more educational progress. Minority race mothers made more educational progress and experienced fewer repeat pregnancies than non-minority mothers. Mothers who reported educational progress experienced fewer pregnancies; conversely, not having a second pregnancy might have fostered educational progress. A weak but significant association was noted between minority race and the amount of stimulation in the home environment. Family Intervention did not account for a substantial amount of variance in toddler cognitive and social competence, although it seemed to improve maternal outcomes. It can be hoped that the benefits of improved maternal functioning, due in part to intervention services, will accrue for the child over time. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
