AB  - Women who gave birth during the COVID-19 pandemic ('covid mums') are a new vulnerable population at increased risk of poor physical and mental health outcomes for both mother and baby. Amongst social distancing requirements and online service delivery during 2020, 'covid mums', especially first-time mothers, missed out on trustworthy resources and supports vital to improving mental health and coping with the stressors of new motherhood. Literature suggests that music therapy parenting interventions, such as Sing&Grow, offer an excellent opportunity to support the health and functioning of vulnerable mothers by providing trustworthy social support and a therapeutic context in which to develop parenting tools and skills. This practice paper offers a reflective description of how an 8-week Sing&Grow group program was adapted to cater to the specific needs of first-time 'covid mums' with babies under 12 months old. Discussion focuses on the therapeutic approach, describing the interactions and content within sessions, strategies employed, and the participant experience of the program. Descriptive examples from the sessions are included to provide insight into the needs of the mothers and how therapeutic principles and strategies were utilised. Observations by the music therapist, combined with self-reported parent feedback, portray that Sing&Grow provided a unique, supportive and therapeutic context for the attending 'covid mums' to receive the social support, skill development, parenting tools and information they clearly craved and needed.
