AB  - BACKGROUND Catheter ablation of accessory pathways (APs) is a successful therapy for symptomatic atrio-ventricular re-entry tachycardia, but manual manipulation of the relatively stiff catheter can lead to complications. Furthermore, both investigator and patient are exposed to fluoroscopic radiation during the procedure. OBJECTIVE To assess the use of remote-controlled catheters in the ablation of APs, using the novel Niobe® (Stereotaxis, Inc., St Louis, MO) magnetic navigation system (MNS). DESIGN The study population was recruited between May 2003 and August 2005. AP conduction was determined by conventional electrophysiology. Exclusion criteria for the ablation procedure were contraindications for the MNS, such as claustrophobia, metallic implants, or the presence of a pacemaker. Follow-up was planned for 3 months after treatment. INTERVENTION Three successive types of remote-controlled catheter were used. The first group of patients was treated with a first-generation single-magnet catheter, the second group with a second-generation bipolar three-magnet catheter, and the third group with a third-generation three-magnet quadripolar catheter. Right-sided APs were reached by retrograde aortic valve passage, while left-sided APs were reached either by retrograde passage or via anterograde trans-septal puncture. Catheter operation was conducted from a separate control room, markedly reducing the investigators' exposure to radiation. The maximum procedural ablation time was 120s, and the maximum energy was 40W. OUTCOME MEASURE The success of AP ablation was determined with conventional electrophysiological criteria. RESULTS A total of 59 patients (67% male, mean age 36-.14 years) were enrolled in the study. No significant differences in baseline characteristics were observed between the three treatment groups. There were 40 patients with left-sided APs (one of whom had two APs) and 19 patients with right-sided APs. Remote-controlled ablation was successful in 67% (12 out of 18) of patients in group one, 85% (23 out of 27) of patients in group two, and 92% (13 out of 14) of patients in group three. Ablation success rates for left-sided APs were low with retrograde access (3 out of 7, 43%) and, consequently, anterograde access with standard trans-septal puncture was adopted in the majority of procedures, with better success rates (29 out of 33, 88%). MNS failures (11 patients in total) were also associated with use of the first-generation single-magnet catheter and an inferoparaseptal insertion site. Compared with group one, median fluoroscopy dosage was significantly lower in groups two (290 vs 1,100 µGym2; P<0.001) and three (129 vs 1,100µGym2; P<0.001). Mean procedure time was significantly lower in group three than in group one (172±90min vs 217 ± 67 min; P= 0.038). There were no complications or recurrences of arrhythmia during follow-up (median 351 days). CONCLUSION The MNS is feasible and safe for remote-controlled catheter ablation of APs. The third-generation three-magnet quadripolar catheter seems to be the most effective catheter for use with the MNS, and involves the shortest procedural radiation exposure time. [ABSTRACT FROM AUTHOR] Copyright of Nature Clinical Practice Cardiovascular Medicine is the property of Springer Nature and its content may not be copied or emailed to multiple sites or posted to a listserv without the copyright holder's express written permission. However, users may print, download, or email articles for individual use. This abstract may be abridged. No warranty is given about the accuracy of the copy. Users should refer to the original published version of the material for the full abstract. (Copyright applies to all Abstracts.)
