AB  - It was hypothesized that official labeling gives personal relevance to an individual's beliefs about how others respond to mental patients; the belief that others label & discriminate against mental patients becomes personally applicable & leads to self-devaluation &/or fear of rejection. Such reactions may have negative effects on both psychological & social functioning. This hypothesis was tested by comparing samples of community residents & psychiatric patients (N = 429 & 164, respectively) in New York City. Five groups -- (1) first-treatment contact patients, (2) repeat-treatment contact patients, (3) formerly treated community residents, (4) untreated community cases, & (5) community residents with no evidence of severe psychopathology -- were administered a scale that measured beliefs about mental patients; scores were associated with demoralization, income, loss, & unemployment in labeled groups but not in unlabeled groups. The results suggest that labeling may produce negative outcomes like those specified by the classic concept of secondary deviance. 7 Tables, 1 Appendix, 38 References. Modified HA
