AB  - Studied the effects of age and other interacting factors on attrition in an inpatient alcoholic treatment unit. 44 male Ss over 55 yrs (mean age 59.2) and 117 male Ss under age 50 (mean age 36) completed a 90-item questionnaire on their background and drinking habits, the Hopkins Symptom Checklist, a 60-item mood scale, 6 semantic differentials measuring attitudes, and the 130-item Ward Atmosphere Scale. Data were analyzed with several 2 × 2 factorial ANOVAs. Results indicate that older Ss were more likely than younger to remain in treatment; being married was associated with completion of the program for both groups; and the younger Ss were more likely to have had a mother that drank excessively. Introversion and depression were significantly related to attrition for all Ss; depression and anxiety scores were higher for younger Ss. Older (but not younger) Ss who dropped out perceived less spontaneity among patients and staff, perceived less involvement of patients in the program, and felt less encouraged to be self-sufficient, suggesting that personal relationships on the ward between staff and patients may be particularly important in preventing older alcoholics from dropping out. (20 ref) (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2019 APA, all rights reserved)
