AB  - Much has been written about working with dying patients. To evaluate counseling, 120 terminally ill cancer patients were randomly assigned to counseling or no counseling and studied before random assignment and at 1, 3, 6, 9, and 12 months on quality of life variables (alienation, depression, locus of control, life satisfaction, self-esteem) as well as functional status and survival. Counseled patients changed significantly in comparison to controls and in a favorable direction by 3 months. The purpose here was to see if older and younger cancer patients differed at baseline and if outcomes of counseling differed by age. Patients under age 60 were compared with those 60 and over. There was no multivariate difference at baseline but univariate differences of more disability and less life satisfaction in the older group. Overall, response to therapy was similar in old and young, with both improving.
