AB  - This study had 2 goals. The first was to determine whether the empirically derived, multidimensional typology developed by T. F. Babor et al (see record [rid]1993-01882-001[/rid]) could be replicated with a different patient sample of alcoholic patients and, thus, provide evidence that the typology is generalizable. The 2nd goal was to evaluate the clinical utility of this typology by determining whether alcoholic subtypes have different outcomes with different treatments. Ss were 79 men who were randomly assigned to 1 of 2 types of aftercare groups: coping skills training or interactional therapy. Results lend credence to the idea that alcoholic patients can be reliably classified using multiple indicators of vulnerability, natural history, chronicity, and severity of consequences. Clinical differences between the 2 aftercare groups persisted 2 yrs after follow-up, demonstrating the clinical usefulness of this typology. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
