Y1  - 2019///
AB  - BACKGROUND: Depression is a highly prevalent mental disorder. It is also a leading cause of ill-health and disability worldwide. Not only does it incur human suffering, it also has a considerable impact on public health burden. Empirical research findings have established that Cognitive Behavioral Therapy (CBT) is an effective and efficacious psychological treatment for depression. However, the delivery and uptake of CBT in frontline clinics is less than desirable. Part of the problem resides in the shortage of resources and help-seekers' practical concerns over financial and time cost. Development of less resource intensive CBT and more effective treatment delivery method may help address the service gap. OBJECTIVES: The present study had four main objectives: (1) to develop an original brief group CBT for depression (BGCBT-D) for local Hong Kong Chinese adults with major depressive disorder, (2) to evaluate the impact of BGCBT-D, (3) to examine the factors related to treatment outcome, and (4) to explore treatment responders' experience in the treatment. METHOD: Mixed methods design involving both quantitative and qualitative studies was used. A community sample of treatment-seeking adults, who had a principal diagnosis of major depressive disorder but were not receiving psychiatric medication treatment, was recruited into an open trial. Consented and eligible participants received six group CBT sessions plus two review sessions. To assess treatment efficacy, outcome measures were administered before, during and after treatment, as well as at 2-month and 6-month post-treatment. A subsample of treatment responders was interviewed two months post-treatment to understand their subjective experience in BGCBT-D. The qualitative data gathered from the interviews were analyzed using Interpretative Phenomenological Analysis (IPA). RESULTS: A total of 24 participants were recruited, in which 18 participants completed at least five group treatment sessions. Overall, results obtained from the as-treated sample showed marked improvement in depression, anxiety, cognitive process, functional impairment, and positive emotion after treatment. The positive gain could be maintained up to six months post-treatment. Participants also reported a high degree of satisfaction and acceptance towards BGCBT-D. In the qualitative study, six treatment responders were recruited and interviewed. Five superordinate themes emerged from the analysis of interviews: (1) self in the group, (2) a commitment, (3) building a toolbox, (4) a flexible way of being and (5) can I really be my own therapist!?. CONCLUSION: The open trial demonstrated the preliminary efficacy of BGCBT-D. The analysis of participants' subjective experience suggested that the benefit occurred through relational factors in the group as well as the therapeutic mechanisms postulated in CBT. In addition, participants' change went beyond symptom reduction and had a wider positive impact on their life. The results of this study have implications for research and practice. BGCBT-D holds promise as a cost-efficient and effective treatment for depression in the context of limited resources. It is worthy of further examination. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2019 APA, all rights reserved)
