AB  - Studied the efficacy of outpatient pharmacotherapy and a short follow-up for the treatment of abstinence syndromes in abusers of opiates and cocaine. Ss were 28 Spanish drug abusers (aged 18–33 yrs). Ss, who requested treatment for speed-ball or other heroin–cocaine combinations, were randomly assigned to 2 treatment groups, which received either trazodone (100 mg/day at night) or amitriptyline (25 mg/day at night) plus perfenazine (2 mg/day at night). Ss were also treated with ketazolam, zolpidem, and guanfacine during initiation of detoxification and with naltrexone during the follow-up period. Ss were followed on an outpatient basis for 2 mo. Treatment efficacy was evaluated using a symptom chart and the Anxiety–Depression Rating Scale by M. Hamilton (1960). Statistical tests were used. (English abstract) (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2019 APA, all rights reserved)
