AB  - Background: As Houses of Faith are often trusted centerpieces in African American (AA) life, there is a well-recognized potential for health interventions that partner with faith-based communities. The Dementia Resource Champion (DRC) Program is a train-the-trainer style health initiative-piloted in 2018 to educate and empower leaders in faith communities to serve as resource and develop Caregiver Support Groups (CSGs) for caregivers of persons living with Alzheimer's Disease and Related Dementia's (ADRD) in underserved communities. The study examined DRC program results at 6 months follow up. Method(s): The DRC pilot program was a 5-session, train-the-trainer intervention aimed at establishing dementia resource champions within faith communities. The participants were asked to develop a plan for a CSG and received ongoing mentorship and resources to develop and implement their CSGs within local faith communities. After the pilot program, two CSGs were created. Surveys were administered to DRC participants 6 months after the training series completion as a follow-up to assess challenges and successes of creating faith-based CSGs. Result(s): Since the end of the pilot program with13 participants from 5 houses of faith, two new caregiver support groups were developed in communities on Chicago's South side with potential support to surrounding neighborhoods. These CSGs meet monthly at respective churches with 8-10 participants consistently. Qualitative assessment of CSGs revealed the following Barriers and Lessons learned: Barriers: Initially: appropriate timing of sessions; lack of knowledge of community resources and understanding of self-care routines was a barrier. Presently: the size and timing of the groups is prompting the need to create additional groups to meet the needs of the working population. Lessons: Need for ongoing mentorship, providing education and resources for CSG meetings and networking will meet the needs of CSGs. Conclusion(s): The early success of the DRC pilot CSGs showcases program feasibility and replicability as an innovative way to empower and train leaders at houses of faith to become ADRD resources for their church and community at large.
