AB  - Background: Inflammatory bowel disease (IBD) imposes a significant psychosocial burden on people and a high economic cost on the community. In 2012 in Australia, hospital costs associated with IBD were estimated to be over AU$100 million; productivity losses at $380 million; and indirect costs at over $2.7 billion. A significant proportion of these costs are thought to result from the consequences of associated co-morbidity, including mental health issues (MHI) in 20-60% of IBD patients. Integrating psychological care with routine screening and intervention may therefore reduce healthcare costs and improve patient wellbeing. Thus, we explored whether changes in healthcare utilisation were seen as part of a 2 year study of integrated psychological care. Method(s): In a prospective observational study, 335 adult patients were recruited from an established hospital IBD Service between September 2015 and September 2016. Patients who opted to participate in psychological screening completed questionnaires measuring mental health and quality of life. Psychological intervention was subsequently offered on the basis of need and willingness. Demographic and IBD-specific information was recorded, and healthcare utilisation data (including emergency department [ED] presentations, admissions, outpatient appointments, and contacts to the IBD nurse support line) were collected via state-wide electronic databases. Follow-up assessment took place twelve months after intake. Result(s): A greater proportion of participants with likely mental health issues (MHI+) presented to ED for IBD-related reasons over twelve months (N=51, 28%), compared to participants without mental health issues (MHI-) [N=28, 18%; X2(1)=4.23, p=0.040]. At follow-up, the proportion of participants who presented to ED over the twelve months decreased significantly more for those who accepted psychological intervention (p=.045) than for those who declined (p=.118). The group that accepted intervention had a total of 51 ED presentations in the first year and only 24 in the following year; this reduction represents a potential cost saving of $36,990. Additionally, an average annual cost per IBD patient was calculated by totalling inpatient and outpatient care for study participants: the average annual cost was AU$1,500 more per MHI+ patient compared to MHI-. Conclusions Integrating mental health screening and support into an established hospital IBD service might decrease utilisation of certain healthcare services. Our data show that poor mental health is associated with higher costs in routine IBD care. Further, integrated psychological support offers an opportunity for cost-savings, particularly via reduced ED presentations. With longer follow-up, greater benefits from more constructive engagement in care may become more apparent.Copyright © 2019 AGA Institute. All rights reserved.
