AB  - Introduction: Caring for a relative with dementia is associated with negative health consequences for caregivers, including depressive symptomatology and anxiety. The differential efficacy of two structured therapies, cognitive behavioural therapy (CBT) and acceptance and commitment therapy (ACT), compared with a minimal support group (MSG), is analyzed. Methods: Participants were 137 dementia family caregivers from Madrid (Spain) with depressive symptomatology (scores above 16 in the CES‐D) were randomly assigned to treatment conditions. Follow‐up data (six months after post‐intervention assessment) are available for 66 caregivers (25 ACT, 19 CBT, and 22 MSG). In addition to depressive symptomatology and anxiety, dysfunctional thoughts about caregiving and experiential avoidance in caregiving were measured. Results: Significant time X intervention effects were found for depression (F = 6.33; p < 0.01; partial‐eta‐squared = 0.17), anxiety (F = 5.01; p < 0.01; partial‐eta‐squared = 0.14), experiential avoidance (F = 3.12; p < 0.05; partial‐eta‐squared = 0.09), and dysfunctional thoughts (F = 5.24; p < 0.01; partial‐eta‐squared = 0.15). Significant follow‐up reductions were found for ACT participants in depressive symptomatology (p < 0.05), experiential avoidance (p < 0.01) and dysfunctional thoughts (p < 0.05). For CBT participants, significant follow‐up reductions were found for depressive symptomatology and anxiety (p < 0.01) and dysfunctional thoughts (p < 0.05). No significant follow‐up changes were found in the MSG group. Conclusions: The results of this study provide additional support for the use of CBT with dementia caregivers, and suggest that ACT may be also useful for reducing distress in this population.
