AB  - This paper is part of a larger project on the consumption of a locally made alcohol, akpeteshie, & its impact on the health & well-being of the people in the Upper West Region of Ghana. The paper reports the findings of a qualitative study using focus group discussions about community perceptions & impacts of akpeteshie. Focus groups were undertaken differently for men (n = 25) & women (n = 20), & the contents were analyzed using grounded theory & from a social learning perspective. Participants indicated that both the elderly & the young were engaged in the use & abuse of akpeteshie. Men drink mainly for coping responses, such as, increased self-confidence, adult status, & to cope with the various social demands. Women seem to drink for socializing with peers. Akpeteshie drinking by both men & women is on the rise, & is increasingly used for sexual abuse & rape. The findings reveal strong perceptions of the health & economic damage that alcohol is having on the people of the area; & the need for policy intervention that not only target health promotion, but an improvement of the socioeconomic conditions of the people & the akpeteshie vendors in the region. 1 Table, 1 Figure, 56 References. Adapted from the source document.
