AB  - Examined the efficacy and safety of sertraline hydrochloride for the treatment of major depression in Alzheimer disease, and evaluated the effect of depression reduction on activities of daily living, cognition, and nonmood behavioral disturbance. 44 outpatients who have probable Alzheimer disease and major depressive episodes participated. Intervention was sertraline hydrochloride, mean dosage of 95 mg/d, or placebo, randomly assigned. Outcome measures were response rate, Cornell Scale for Depression in Dementia, Hamilton Depression Rating Scale (HDRS), Mini-Mental State Examination (MMSE), Psychogeriatric Depression Rating Scale-activities of daily living subscale, and Neuropsychiatric Inventory (NI), to quantify patient behavior disturbance and caregiver distress. In the sertraline-treated group 9 Ss were full responders and 11 were partial responders compared with 3 and 4, respectively, in the placebo-treated group. The sertraline-treated Ss had greater improvements in the scores for the Cornell Scale for Depression in Dementia and HDRS, and a statistical trend toward less decline in activities of daily living on the Psychogeriatric Depression Rating Scale-activities of daily living subscale. There was no difference between treatment groups in MMSE or NI ratings over time. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
