AB  - With the rapid aging of the world's population, health services everywhere have a responsibility to promote independence while treating and rehabilitating older people who encounter illness. Many problems associated with old age can be largely attributed to myths surrounding the aging process and not just personal psychological or emotional distress. This practical and accessible text shows that many of these difficulties can be effectively tackled through changing lifestyles, appropriate care, and an adjustment in treatment emphasis and expectations by mental health professionals particularly. The Evergreen Programme is a psycho-educational, group-based, holistic rehabilitation programme for older adults with acute mental illness. It has been delivered by a multidisciplinary team at St. Patrick's University Hospital in Dublin, Ireland, for the last decade. Themes of the programme include positive or successful aging, a wellness emphasis rather than a focus on illness and disability, and group sharing and peer support. These principles permeate the broad range of topics relevant to the health and morale of older people that are covered in the programme and reflected in this book. Key features of this publication include a format this book as a manual to commence group discussion also impart key information and learning to ensure that older people take advantage of their recovery to live longer and healthier lives. This book is written from a mental health perspective but is also relevant to a range of professionals who work with older people in groups including mental health and general nurses, psychiatrists, social workers, occupational therapists, clinical psychologists, and psychotherapists. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2020 APA, all rights reserved)
