AB  - The purpose of this study was to ascertain the experiences of both landlords and clinical and housing service teams in a supported housing intervention between head lease and rent subsidy programs for homeless adults with a serious mental illness. The research questions were: (a) What are the points of tension / trouble spots with regard to landlord-tenant relationships? Do these tension points differ by rent subsidy / head lease conditions? And, (b) In what ways do landlords / caretakers support program tenancies? Does this differ by head lease / rent subsidy condition? In this study 16 landlords were interviewed, and 24 housing and clinical team members from the At Home program and CMHA housing participated in focus groups. Research findings revealed that there was less contact between landlords and tenants in the head lease condition where housing teams tended to negotiate troubles spots. Head lease programs tended to be marked by a congruence with therapeutic values and a partial as opposed to full separation of housing and services marked by surveillance. The rent subsidy program saw more interaction between landlords and tenants and subsequently more trouble spots centering around visitors and damages. The rent subsidy program was more congruent with citizenship values and afforded participants more opportunities to learn about tenant roles. Copies of dissertations may be obtained by addressing your request to ProQuest, 789 E. Eisenhower Parkway, P.O. Box 1346, Ann Arbor, MI 48106-1346. Telephone 1-800-521-3042; e-mail: disspub@umi.com
