AB  - Background/Purpose: Individuals with serious mental illness (SMI) experience increased cancer mortality due to inequities in cancer prevention, delays in diagnosis and inequities in treatment. People with SMI face unique barriers to cancer care, including uncontrolled psychiatric symptoms, disproportionate poverty, and the fragmentation of mental health and cancer care. Method(s): To increase access to high-quality cancer care, we developed a partnership between a local community mental health organization and an academic medical center to identify individuals at high risk for cancer and individuals with a recent/previous cancer diagnosis, and address barriers to care. We conducted educational sessions and outreach with community mental health staff including mental health clinicians, administrators, and residential staff. We developed a population-based system to track referrals and triage urgency. Medical center faculty developed streamlined processes for screening (e.g. same day mammography) and appointments. Result(s): From June 2017 through June 2018, we received 53 referrals of patients with SMI at high risk for cancer or previously/recently diagnosed with cancer. The patients who were referred had a mean age of 57 years, 71% were male, and 72% were current smokers. We partnered with health center staff to access screening and oncology care. Reasons for referral included screening/prevention (24), prior cancer diagnosis (11) and new cancer diagnosis (n= 6). Intervention strategies included collaborating with group home staff to facilitate same-day diagnostic mammograms and conducting joint meetings with oncology providers to prevent delays to surgery and guide end of life care. Conclusions and Implications: Person-centered approaches, collaboration between academic medical centers and community organizations, community engagement with a diverse team, and population- based tracking are promising strategies to promote equitable cancer care for patients with SMI from underserved communities.
