AB  - Presenter: Vashisht Madabhushi MD University of Kentucky Background: For patients who experience failure of medical management, liver transplantation is the ultimate therapeutic modality for end-stage liver disease (ESLD). In rural areas, the road to obtaining a liver transplant starts at the primary care providers (PCPs) office. There is significant variability in patient selection, timing, and discussion of referral to the transplant center. Patients with cirrhosis secondary to alcohol abuse are far less likely to be referred to a transplant center. In rural communities, patients with ESLD have the lowest rates of wait-listing for liver transplantation. Patients in rural communities also experience higher rates of mortality while waiting for liver transplantation compared to those in urban areas. Data regarding the practice patterns around liver transplantation referral, particularly in rural populations, are limited. Our institution serves the state of Kentucky, where approximately 70% of the counties are classified as rural. This qualitative study aims to identify issues related to knowledge and perceptions that are potential barriers to liver transplant referral among PCPs. Method(s): After obtaining IRB approval, data were collected through semi-structured focus groups with 4 different PCP groups around the state (n=14). Each focus group was audio-recorded and transcribed. Each of the transcripts were analyzed, individually first, then as a group, by the authors and a codebook was developed. Analysis focused on identifying themes associated with barriers to liver transplantation referral and perceptions of patients with ESLD. After our team completed an initial coding of focus group transcripts, the codebook was refined and consolidated via an iterative process. Finally, transcripts were re-coded with the updated focus group and themes were extracted. Result(s): Three major thematic areas were identified that contributed to the barriers and perceptions of PCPs towards liver transplant referral. Medical culture was a significant barrier, including beliefs regarding referrals "not [being] my job [as a PCP]." Furthermore, all groups identified gaps in their knowledge related to diagnosis criteria and understanding of the transplant process as significant barriers. The final theme was the predisposition against referring patients with "self-induced causes of cirrhosis", such as alcoholism or hepatitis B/C. PCPs expressed a belief that these patients, prior to obtaining to a liver transplant referral, had to prove to the PCP that the patient was "worth that liver," by meeting arbitrarily defined targets including attending regular PCP appointments and varying durations of abstinence from substance use. Conclusion(s): This study identifies various areas where perceptions of PCPs can serve as barriers to liver transplant access. Gaps in knowledge regarding the liver transplant evaluation process were important barriers to referral. The results identify significant bias amongst PCPs and provides a foundation for future quantitative studies and interventions to improve this healthcare disparity. Considering the increasing prevalence of liver disease, interventions may prove beneficial in early referral of patients with ESLD to hepatologists and liver transplant specialists.Copyright © 2020
