AB  - Investigated the long-term psychological effects of political imprisonment in the former German Democratic Republic. 146 non-treatment-seeking former political prisoners (aged 27–82 yrs) were compared with 75 age- and sex-matched controls. Assessments included the structured Diagnostic Interview for Psychiatric Disorders for Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders-III-Revised (DSM-III-R) and DSM-IV diagnoses, a checklist of persecution and maltreatment, and other self-rated measures of posttraumatic stress disorder (PTSD), anxiety, depression, and dissociation. Results show a frequency of 30% current and 60% lifetime PTSD in the former prisoners. Other anxiety disorders (e.g., claustrophobia, social phobia) outnumbered comorbid affective disorders. The level of dissociation was elevated in the former prisoners. Intrusive recollections and hyperarousal were more common than avoidance/numbing symptoms. Despite differences in imprisonment duration between 3 historically defined eras of persecution, no differences appeared in the level of symptomatology. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2019 APA, all rights reserved)
