AB  - A number of studies have demonstrated that substance use self-efficacy is strongly related to relapse. The goal of the current study was to evaluate the effectiveness of a combined substance use treatment program as a function of change in substance use self-efficacy. The program was modeled after treatments evaluated in Project MATCH and adapted for delivery in an outpatient substance use clinic. Intensive Outpatient Program (IOP): IOP is a 4-week program that consists of 3 hours of daily group counseling. Three treatment modalities that have been tested and found equivalent in the MATCH study were utilized: Cognitive-Behavioral Therapy (CBT), Twelve Step Facilitation (TSF), and Motivation Enhancement Therapy (MET). Population and measures: Ten adults (ages 22-60; 60% females) who were evaluated at the outpatient clinic and who met diagnostic criteria for substance abuse or dependence for any type of substance were subsequently referred to the IOP. Substance-related self-efficacy was assessed at pre-and post-treatment using the Drug Avoidance Self-Efficacy Scale (DASES). A client satisfaction survey was administered at post-treatment. Result(s): Results demonstrated a significant increase in self-efficacy from pre-treatment to post-treatment (t(9)=-2.44, p <.05; Cohen's d=.80). Gender, substance use severity, substance comorbidity, and previous treatment were not significantly related to self-efficacy at post-treatment. Overall, treatment satisfaction was high. The open-ended questions about the program revealed that participants consistently reported their counselors' empathy, encouragement, and knowledge to be the most helpful feature of the program. The most common limitation was the inflexible schedule of the program (i.e., 9am-12pm daily) and the need for evening groups. Conclusion(s): The current pilot study demonstrates substance use self-efficacy improvements subsequent to a community treatment program using a combination of CBT, TSF, and MET in a clinical setting with a non-restricted representative sample. Although the patient sample was small, the results of the current study may suggest the importance of combining efficacious treatment techniques to maximize the impact on client self-efficacy, as was suggested by Ilgen et al (2005). In order to establish the relationship between self-efficacy and behavior change, it will be necessary to examine whether increased levels of post-treatment selfefficacy predict future abstinence.
