AB  - Results are reported of an in-jail methadone maintenance program that services 3,000 New York City inmates annually, most of whom are injectors of both heroin & cocaine. Methadone program participants' (N = 308) postrelease outcomes are compared with outcomes for similar addicts (N = 138) who received only a 7-day detoxification while in jail. Multivariate analyses of interview data suggest that program participants are more likely than the control group to pursue drug treatment after release & to remain in treatment longer. Although overall rate of retention in community treatment programs after release was modest, drug use & crime were lower among the program paticipants who remained in treatment after 6 months. The in-jail methadone maintenance program proved most effective among inmates who had already been enrolled in similar programs before arrest. Recommendations are made to help improve outcomes for this difficult-to-treat population of criminally involved addicts. 9 Tables, 27 References. Adapted from the source document.
