AB  - Aim. This study purposes to assess anger and aggressiveness perception in a group of residual schizophrenia (SR) outpatients compared with a control group (N). Methods. We administrated the Aggression Questionnaire to two group of patients: the first (SR) were constituted by 27 (14 M; 13 F) residual schizophrenia outpatients; the second (N) by 30 subjects (15 M; 15 F) without psychiatric diagnosis recruited in the Policlinic staff in accordance with matched pairs criterion for gender and age. The significance of the differences in the answers to every item, in the SR group and in the N control group, were evaluated, item by item, through the Chi-square test. Results. A significant difference in two groups regarding the dimensions examined by the questionnaire has been found. Subjects with residual schizophrenia (SR) were inclined to consider them unexpectedly as sensitive, irritable persons often able to act more aggressively than subjects of control group (N). Conclusions. In our study patients with residual schizophrenia seem to be characterized by unexpressed and unsuspected aggressiveness and anger, elements that must be considered in the therapeutic relationship. So it is necessary to insert these patients in resocialising activities in order to facilitate the expression of aggressive feelings in a mediation context as rehabilitation. This offers the possibility to face the mental illness and to avoid the occurrence of acting out.
