AB  - Individuals with a severe mental illness (SMI) & substance use disorder tend to have medical & social problems & to make slower progress in treatment than those who have either disorder alone. Reported here is an attempt to identify techniques that might help to change patterns of substance uses in the SMI, drawing on qualitative focus group data from 21 psychiatric clinic outpatients (primarily male Caucasians) with a current schizophrenia spectrum diagnosis. A total of 18 participants had a lifetime diagnosis of alcohol abuse or dependence, & 21 lifetime other drug diagnoses were recorded. Participants identified several therapeutic & extratherapeutic factors that helped them to initiate & maintain changes in their substance use, as well as factors that hindered change. The findings are related to knowledge about the effectiveness of substance use disorder treatment techniques in general, & implications of the data are discussed for the conduct of integrated treatment of individuals with SMI & a substance use disorder. 21 References. Adapted from the source document.
