AB  - Administered questionnaires to 37 female and 34 male staff members (59 of whom were aged 26–54 yrs) in 4 alcoholism treatment facilities regarding the appropriateness for treatment of 2 prospective patients described by case summaries, a 38-yr-old teacher and a 59-yr-old indigent homeless person. To assess whether female applicants would be less desirably received than males, the teacher summary was male and the indigent female for half the Ss, while the sex assignments were reversed for the other half. Results show that female teachers were considered less appropriate for individual therapy/counseling and evening outpatient clinics, while inpatient rehabilitation units and daytime outpatient clinics were deemed less appropriate for the female indigents. Contrary to formulated hypotheses, female indigents were considered more appropriate for admission than male indigents and were preferred for the modalities of group therapy, individual therapy, and marital and family therapy. Female staff were less likely than male staff to consider female teachers appropriate for treatment and more likely to refer male teachers for vocational counseling. Findings support the contention that differential attitudes toward women exist among alcohol treatment staff personnel and suggest that, in certain circumstances, women may judge other women less favorably than men do. (13 ref) (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2019 APA, all rights reserved)
