AB  - Background: Up to 20% of older adults become home bound at some point in their lives. Homebound older adults suffer from depression (29%), comorbidities (33%), polypharmacy (48%) and recurrent hospitalizations (32%). The Dementia Palliative Care Program (DPCP) is a collaboration between Montefiore's Center for the Aging Brain (CAB) and Westchester Jewish Community Services (WJCS). The CAB provides a consultative multispecialty team assessment of patients with cognitive complaints. WJCS is a multi-disciplinary agency that provides coordinated and effective care for Westchester residents. The DPCP was created to meet the needs of older adults with Alzheimer's dementia and related diseases (ADRD) who are homebound. Method(s): The initiative was led by a fellowship trained geriatrician, who delivered home-based care to frail older adults in Westchester County from August 1, 2018 - present. The physician worked with WJCS to deliver home-based community services, including referrals for concrete services, advance care planning, counseling, specialized home care and respite services. Entry criteria: community dwelling older adults aged >=65 years, home bound, with cognitive complaints, not hospice eligible. Data reviewed after one year of the program. Result(s): 39 patients were recruited: 38% were residents in assisted living, and the rest were home bound. Average age 86 years (range 71 - 106 years), 74% female. Most (85%) already had a dementia diagnosis, advanced stages, requiring help with most activities of daily living or bedbound. 43% had hired help or aides in the home, 38% needed help with their independent activities of daily living. Medical needs identified: 26% required medication reviews and adjustments, 31% required a primary care doctor, and 2 needed specific medical equipment. Ten percent of the patients expired. 16 clients had social work (SW) services provided by WJCS. The SW identified increased depression and isolation on the part of caregivers. Conclusion(s): This unique collaboration highlights the vulnerable population of homebound adults with ADRD and the urgent need to develop innovative programs. The initial families who sought the DPCP appeared to have resources but still needed additional assistance. The high mortality in this small cohort underscores the need for a palliative approach.
