AB  - As managed care becomes more prevalent in psychiatric rehabilitation, services are becoming more limited. This can be attributed to managed care providers using more narrow definitions of medical necessity (Sharfstein, 1997). As a result, psychiatric rehabilitation practitioners are faced with the challenge of broadening the definition of psychiatric rebabilitation to include skills training and environmental support as legitimate points of intervention. A group of 92 (N = 92) people with severe and persistent psychiatric disabilities identified perceived barriers to community integration in three areas: skills, support systems, and resources. People with psychiatric disabilities reported that resources were greater barriers than support systems or skills. When rating barriers to successful community integration, people with psychiatric disabilities and practitioners do not perceive barriers similarly. Implications for service delivery in a managed care environment and future research were discussed.
