AB  - For centuries, exclusion from the community - ostracism, exile, and sometimes incarceration - has been a fact of life for adults with severe and disabling mental illnesses. Nineteenth-century asylums quickly became 20th-century warehouses of exclusion. Too often, deinstitutionalization simply created little institutions in the community - run-down board-and-care homes and skilled nursing facilities that sometimes were more degrading and disabling than their predecessors. The reaction to these failures led to program models that depend on staff to provide long-term support: a particularly insidious form of exclusion. Today people with severe mental illness commonly are in but not of the community. The mental health field has addressed these shortcomings by improving treatment and rehabilitation technologies, and through policy initiatives. Some technological advances have dramatically reduced symptoms and improved skills. These advances have been less successful in promoting social inclusion and participation, however. At the policy level, the general public is blamed for its refusal to accept people with mental illness. Social policies aim at forcing inclusion. Anti-stigma campaigns exhort communities to change their beliefs and practices. Neither has been very successful. Behind these efforts are assumptions that are begging to be questioned. Mental health consumer-survivors have begun to do this by advocating that recovery guide how we design and implement services. Perhaps it is time to look beyond how we can improve intervention technologies or create policies that reduce community resistance to inclusion. It is time to challenge some of the prevailing assumptions about how people with psychiatric disabilities can successfully achieve community life. [Copyright American Psychological Association]
