AB  - Purpose: In 2019, 62% of HIV diagnoses in the UK were among people born abroad. Data suggests over half acquired HIV in the UK. Despite this, there is no shared understanding of the policies and interventions needed to combat HIV in the context of migration. National AIDS Trust (NAT) conducted a project to understand the barriers migrants face accessing HIV testing, treatment and care and make recommendations to overcome them. Method(s): This project utilised a peer-led research design model. NAT employed three migrants living with HIV to be a part of our research team who developed a semi-structured interview guide. The Peer Experts conducted 22 in-depth interviews and two focus groups. The inclusion criteria for this project for both Peer Experts and participants was that the person had to be living with HIV, born outside the UK and have received HIV care in London. Result(s): Participants were pleased with the care received through HIV clinics and from support services. There were barriers reported to accessing primary care services and concerns about understanding of HIV among GPs. Participants reported delaying accessing HIV healthcare services due to fears and confusion around charges for NHS services and NHS and Home Office data sharing. Nearly half of the participants first diagnosed in the UK were diagnosed late. Participants reported being unable to access information about healthcare entitlements. Other factors were language barriers, poverty, mental health problems and the impact of stigma and discrimination. Conclusion(s): People born abroad are not always testing proactively. Hostile environment policies have a devastating impact on access to healthcare. Migrants are at risk of being left behind in the UK's response to HIV without urgent action. There needs to be more proactive information about healthcare entitlements and prevention campaigns targeted at this population. Wider barriers to accessing healthcare must be considered and removed.
