AB  - In recent years, cognitive-behavioural interventions have proven to be effective in the treatment of Panic Disorder with Agoraphobia (FDA). However, there is controversy concerning treatment efficacy of PDA for patients with a comorbid diagnosis of Personality Disorder (PD). This study evaluates the impact of a FD on PDA treatment response. Patients (70 women, mean age: 36; 11 men, mean age: 39) were recruited from a cognitive-behavioural group treatment program for PDA. Diagnoses on axis I (N = 81) and axis 2 (N = 36) were made at treatment onset according to DSM-III-R criteria, Validated questionnaires were used to assess agoraphobia at treatment onset (pretest) and following treatment (post-test, 3 month follow-up). AU patients improved markedly from pretest to post-test. and at the 3 month follow-up. Grouping of patients according to the presence or absence of a personality disorder reveals significant differences between the groups on pre and post-treatment scores on agoraphobia. The results also reveal that patients with a personality disorder improve more slowly than subjects without a personality disorder. The discussion provides suggestions for future studies.
