AB  - Contemplative practices are strategies and activities that foster an open awareness of oneself, one's surroundings, and thoughts, feelings, and experiences that arise through the practice. Mindfulness, namely the awareness gained through intentional focus on the present moment without judgment, is often a byproduct of contemplative practices. The use and research of mindfulness has increased over the past several decades. More recently, mindfulness-based practices have been increasingly used in educational settings, both with students and educators. Many K-12 schools use mindfulness-based interventions to foster social-emotional well-being in students and educators, improve academic performance and mitigate unwanted behaviors in students, and reduce occupational stress for educators. Research has supported its use in school contexts for both populations, with small to medium effect sizes. Specific attention has been directed to the use and acceptability of mindfulness-based interventions in urban schools with educators and ethnic and racial minority students. However, the extent to which mindfulness practices have been investigated in rural schools has been minimal. Rural communities and schools face unique circumstances, including geographical and professional isolation, lack of resources for schools, high levels of poverty among students, and multiple roles filled by educators, that place school staff at higher risk of chronic stress, mental health concerns, and other negative health outcomes. This study employed a mixed-method design comprised of focus groups and a small-scale study to refine and determine the acceptability and usability of a mindfulness-based training for rural educators meant to reduce occupational stress and improve emotional well-being, educator self-efficacy, self-compassion, and mindfulness skills. Results from focus groups indicated that the rural educators in this study have a need for this type of training and offered recommended modifications. Findings from the usability study demonstrated that participants experienced improvements in their emotions, self-compassion, emotional manifestations of stress, and mindful observation skills. After participating in the training, participants shared their experiences and benefits gained. These results and implications for future research were discussed. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
