AB  - The 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development, adopted at the United Nations by world leaders in 2015, promotes just, peaceful and inclusive societies (Goal 16) as an important component of sustainable development. Peaceful societies not only have an absence of violent conflict, but also promote effective social inclusion and integration of women and men. This briefing will provide findings and recommendations on post-war reintegration and community engagement of ex-combatants based on empirical research conducted by the author on the reconstruction of gendered identities of former Tamil soldiers. Through psychosocial programmes and mentoring, women's identities were reconstructed and refeminised to fit into contemporary Tamil / Sri Lankan society to facilitate ex-combatant reintegration. Previous primary research by the author examines Sri Lanka's process for rehabilitating Tamil ex-combatants (Bureau of the Commissioner General of Rehabilitation 2011), particularly through vocational training and psychosocial components. The research was conducted by interviewing professionals working with ex-combatants, and found that a process of 're-feminisation' occurred for females (Martin 2016a, 2016b, 2016c). In 2015, semi-structured interviews were conducted with seven professionals on their experience working with ex-combatants in rehabilitation centres in Sri Lanka. Participants worked in the fields of mental health, psychology, teaching, the military and academia. The interviews were qualitatively analysed for main themes. Judith Butler's theories on gender performativity (Butler 1988; 1990) and Steven Lukes' (1974) theories on three-dimensional power were used as a foundation for the research. The research revealed that through the rehabilitation programme, men and women were acculturated to behave in ways conducive to traditional, patriarchal Tamil society. The aim was not to criticise the Government of Sri Lanka (GoSL) but to provide an analysis of the rehabilitation programme. The current briefing highlights the programme's effects on women beneficiaries and the challenges they faced when reintegrating into conservative Tamil society in the north and east of Sri Lanka. Recommendations for policy and decision-makers will also be provided.
