AB  - Assessed whether joint hypermobility syndrome is more frequent in patients with panic disorder, agoraphobia, or both than in control Ss and, if so, whether mitral valve prolapse modifies or accounts in part for the association. A case-control study was conducted in a general teaching hospital outpatient clinic. Ss were 99 patients (mean age 38.5 yrs), newly diagnosed and untreated, with panic disorder, agoraphobia, or both and 2 groups of age- and sex-matched control Ss: 99 psychiatric patients and 64 medical patients who had never suffered from any anxiety disorder. Measures consisted of the Structured Clinical Interview for DSM-III—R, Beighton's criteria for joint hypermobility syndrome, and 2-dimensional and M-mode echocardiogram. Joint hypermobility syndrome was found in 67.7% of Ss with anxiety disorder but in only 10.1% of psychiatric and 12.5% of medical control Ss. Ss with anxiety disorder were over 16 times more likely than control Ss to have joint laxity. These findings were not altered after the presence of mitral valve prolapse was taken into account. Of the Ss with anxiety disorder, those who had joint hypermobility syndrome were younger and more often women and had an earlier onset of the disorder than Ss without joint hypermobility syndrome. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
