PY  - 2014
DA  - 2014/02//
Y1  - 2014/02//
AB  - Despite a wide array of effective treatments for mental health conditions, services are often not used. Research argues that the stigma pervading mental health categories is a significant barrier to seeking needed care (Corrigan, 2004; Link & Phelan, 2006). The current work investigates a psychological determinant (ascribed humanity) of people's willingness to seek treatment for mental health conditions. Three studies converged on a specific process model: as people ascribe humanity--that is, attribute uniquely human characteristics--to mental illness, a self-representation emerges that incorporates these stigmatized others and there-by influences compassion toward them. These compassionate feelings, in turn, can redirect toward the self and increase peoples' own willingness to seek treatment (should the self become vulnerable). This research complements previous work--which has examined the inverse process of denied humanity (dehumanization) in interpersonal and intergroup contexts--by illustrating how extending the category of human to include those with mental illness influences perceivers' treatment-seeking motivations. [PUBLICATION ABSTRACT]
