AB  - Discusses the effectiveness of the conjoint systemic family therapy within the context of Italian government psychiatric services offering mental health assistance to patients from comparatively underprivileged socioeconomic backgrounds. Observations of 58 families are reported that were treated by a psychotherapy team over a 12-mo period. 75% of the designated patients (32 males and 26 females) were under 30 and 40% under 18 yrs of age. 34 families had already received some form of psychiatric assistance, and 13 designated patients had histories of institutionalization. Data indicate a high percentage of successful family therapy outcomes, with only 4 cases showing no improvement. Conjoint family therapy is cost-effective because of the fewer therapy sessions required and better utilization of psychiatric personnel. Conjoint family therapy, depending on the therapist's observations rather than on the educated introspection and articulate verbalization of problems by individual patients, is viewed as eminently suitable for the clientele of free public facilities. (English abstract) (10 ref) (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
