AB  - Rationale: Support during smoking cessation is essential for the achievement of continuous smoking cessation. Recently, the number of people who use smartphones as tools for self-management or for collecting information has been increasing because of the rapid increase in the use of smartphones and their applications. Objective(s): We developed a nicotine addiction treatment application for smartphone "CureApp" jointly with CureApp Research. A multicenter single group intervention study was conducted to verify its effect. This application has a smoking diary, automatic response chat and video education program, behavior therapy support, and counseling functions. Method(s): In addition to the usual treatment including prescription of drugs that help patients to stop smoking on an outpatient basis, we enrolled patients to use this application. Then, the baseline information of each case, and smoking status at 12, 24 and 52 weeks was collected. Result(s): Of the 55 cases enrolled in this study, the proportions of those who quit smoking tobacco in the data collected as of the end of October 2017 were 78% (40/51), 64% (28/44) and 46% (11/24), respectively. According to the nationwide smoking cessation rate survey conducted by the Ministry of Health, Labor and Welfare in 2007, the rates of continuous smoking cessation in outpatient clinics were 54%, 41% and 33% at 12, 24 and 52 weeks, respectively. The results of our study were better than this. Conclusion(s): The smartphone application "CureApp" may be useful in helping individuals quit smoking in outpatient settings.
