AB  - Objective: In women, chronic energy deficiency, acting mainly through a dysfunction of the hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis, is a well-known cause of hypogonadotropic hypogonadism, leading to hypothalamic amenorrhea. Although similar alterations in gonadotropin secretion can also occur in males in response to severe energy restriction, psychiatric causes of undernutrition are often underestimated in male patients. Orthorexia nervosa is an Avoidant/Restrictive Food Intake Disorder characterized by a fixation on proper nutrition, often associated with strenuous exercise. Orthorexia may lead to significant weight loss, nutritional deficiencies, endocrine complications and poor quality of life. Estimated prevalence ranges from 6.9% to 57.6% and it is still unclear whether it is more common among women or men. Method(s): A 33-years-old Caucasian man was referred to our outpatient Andrology and Sexual Medicine clinic for hypogonadism. He was working as a quality control technician and denied smoking, alcohol or drug habit. When he was 6 years old he underwent right orchiectomy for "severe hypoplasia of the testis". He identified himself as heterosexual and reported that he had never had any relationship or sexual activity, including autoerotism; however, he did not complain for low sexual desire or for reduction of spontaneous erections. The patient's psychiatric history revealed an obsessive behaviour in pursuit of a healthy diet. At 4 years old he started avoiding eating meat and fish for a feeling of disgust. Since adolescence, he progressively developed an obsession with healthy food, focusing not on the quantity but on the quality, associated with an avoidance of situations possibly exposing to food bought or prepared by others, leading to social isolation. He also showed an irrational concern over the relationship between diet choices and his symptoms, such as digestive problems, allergies and joint pain. The patient also reported an uncomfortable feeling of upper abdominal fullness and belching after meals; further evaluation revealed a selective body image distortion related to the abdomen. Self-imposed "healthy" rules included fasts, excessive exercise and avoiding talking to minimize abdominal distension. On admission the patient appeared pale, with normal androgenisation; his weight and height were 58 kg and 1.77 m, resulting in a BMI of 18.5 kg/m2. Left testis had normal size (25 ml) and sonographic features. General and hormonal laboratory tests revealed alterations typically associated with restrictive food intake, in particular iron deficiency anaemia, ipotrygliceridemia, euthyroid sick syndrome (TSH, thyroid stimulating hormone, at lower limits - 0.97 U/L; fT3, free triiodothyronine, below normal - 1.94 pmol/L), low IGF-1 (insulin-like growth factor-1; 93.9 ng/mL), and hypogonadotropic hypogonadism (LH, luteinizing hormone 1.9 U/L; FSH, follicular stimulating hormone 2.0 U/L; total testosterone 7.5 nmol/L). Result(s): A multidisciplinary team, including psychiatrist, endocrinologist, psychotherapist and dietician, took charge of the case, initially in in-patient, and afterwards in out-patient setting, applying a combination of medication (paroxetine, amisulpride and S-Adenosyl methionin) and cognitive-behavioral therapy. Following a brief hospitalization, the patient's BMI increased from 18.5 to 18.8 kg/m2. At that time thyroid function was completely restored, with normal levels of TSH (2.09 U/L) and fT3 (3.59 pmol/L); even if both LH and total testosterone levels were still below normal (2.0 U/L and 10.4 nmol/L, respectively), gonadal axis showed initial signs of recovery. Therefore, it was decided to closely monitor laboratory tests, without administering testosterone replacement therapy. Conclusion(s): With weight gain, LH and testosterone gradually increased up to normalization. 11 months after hospitalization, the patient's BMI was 21.5 kg/m2 and total testosterone reached optimal levels, more than 3-times higher than the first evaluation (25.3 nmol/L; LH 7.5 U/L). Despite taking psychotropic drugs with potentially negative effects on libido, he reported an improvement of his overall sexual functioning, but had not engaged in sexual activity yet. The case will require further assessment to evaluate other crucial aspects connected with undernutrition and hypogonadism, i.e. changes in bone mass density. Policy of full disclosure: Eating disorders in men are often underdiagnosed and overlooked. In particular, Avoidant/Restrictive Food Intake Disorder is a common condition with unfavourable endocrine consequences, including hypogonadotropic hypogonadism. Weight restoration and psychiatric recovery can promptly lead to full resolution of gonadal axis dysregulation. There is no financial conflict of interest.Copyright © 2016
