AB  - Health and Cultures is the culmination of years of effort by many individuals and groups, including the Canadian Council on Multicultural Health/Conseil Canadien de la Sante Multiculturelle and the Canadian Public Health Association. Several chapters in both volumes were originally presented at national conferences sponsored by these groups on multicultural health care, ethnicity and aging, immigrant and refugee adjustment and health needs, and substance abuse in cultural communities. Participating authors are from a wide variety of areas, including the social sciences, health sciences, education, rehabilitation, medicine, nursing and social work. The chapters in each volume are organized around general topic areas, such as theory, policies, education, care delivery, programs and services, and the specific needs of newcomer Canadians. In the two volumes some forty authors, over thirty - six chapters, detail the development and implementation of programs, services and care for racially and culturally diverse populations. The over - all purpose is to generate awareness among students and practitioners of the complex ethnic and cultural issues pertaining to health status and health care. This review outlines what some of these issues are. A number of psychological, sociological, anthropological, nursing and interactive - communication models are offered in recognition of the fact that because health (as well as illness) behavior is culturally determined and shaped, culturally sensitive health care delivery must draw on an understanding of human behavior in its many cultural and situational contexts. Another important and laudable goal of the two volumes is to examine the effects of bigotry, prejudice and stereotyping on the physical and mental well - being of people who experience these in their everyday lives. Collectively, the chapters in Health and Cultures represent a kind of metaparadigm for multicultural/transcultural care - giving that is both client centered and research focused. This metaparadigm includes taking into consideration the following: recognizing the uniqueness of individuals with respect to ethnic origin (taking into account age, gender, personal beliefs, values, customs and traditions), culturally sensitive environments (settings such as home, clinic, hospital), and health status based on culturally specific illness and wellness behaviors (folk beliefs of illness causation, pain management, remedies, dietary practices). Every individual is also seen as culturally unique and as a "product" of past experiences, beliefs and values that have been learned and passed down from one generation to the next. Professional care - givers are themselves no exception to this idea, as several chapters point out. Health care, as many of the authors explain, is not culturefree but is itself culturally determined. Care - givers regardless of their own cultural origins must therefore avoid projecting onto clients their own world views and cultural uniqueness if appropriate care is to be provided. Culturally diverse health care can and should also be rendered in a variety of settings, clinical or otherwise, where the client is the most comfortable. Since there is a contingent relationship between cultural determinism and the delivery of culturally sensitive care, the care provider must be guided by specific knowledge of assessment, diagnosis, planning, implementation and evaluation of the client's needs based on culturally relevant information derived from and conducted in appropriate social and/or community contexts. Multicultural care is essentially a two - way process, as several authors have noted. The chapters are particularly effective in exploring certain core concepts absolutely essential in Canadian medical and health practice, namely that of: the cultural construction of illness, population - specific diseases, individual definitions of ethnic or racial identity, folk illnesses and alternative diagnoses and treatments, individual/group experiences of distress, intercultural variations in verbal/nonverbal dimensions of clinical communication, interactions between gender, class and ethnicity/race, ethnic identity of health professionals and their efficacy, the role of informal networks within ethnocultural communities, intercultural variations in approaches to dying, grieving and palliative care, cross - cultural variations in the interpretation of ethical and medico - legal principles governing medical practice, impact of interpreters and cultural advocates on health communication and representation of the client in the system, and cultural and social political factors in the health and service experience of refugees -- to name a few. Potential barriers to effective multicultural health care teaching and delivery, such as financial restraints, limited human resources, conflicting policies and regulations, and overcrowded curricula were also identified in various chapters.
