AB  - Addressed the question of which job-search training programs work best with which types of clients. Of the 210 disabled or socially disadvantaged job-seekers (aged 20–62 yrs) in the sample, 35% were psychiatrically disabled. Ss were randomly assigned to 1 of 3 self-directed, group job-search treatments that varied in structure and in focus on behavior. Employment status was assessed at 1-mo, 6-mo, and 12-mo follow-ups. Hierarchical regression analysis of variance (ANOVA) was used to assess the main effects of the treatment and their interactions with 9 client attributes. A significant main effect for treatment was observed at the 1-mo follow-up only, with the most structured, behavior-oriented program being found superior. Significant attribute–treatment interactions involving gender and marital status (1-mo follow-up) and age (12-mo follow-up) were found. (French abstract) (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
