AB  - This study was designed to examine psychopathology in carpal tunnel syndrome (CTS), and to evaluate whether there are psychological variables that predict treatment outcome and the progression of acute to chronic CTS. Although, to date, no research has been conducted specifically on psychopathology and CTS, studies have indicated that individuals with other musculoskeletal pain syndromes have greater psychopathology than the general population. Based on these findings, it was hypothesized that individuals with CTS, a pain/disability syndrome of the hand and wrist, would have significantly greater psychopathology than normal, and that this psychopathology would be related to treatment outcome. Additionally, a comparison of CTS patients with two other groups of pain patients (low back pain, or LBP, and temporomandibular joint disorder, or TMD) was made, in order to determine if there are any differences in terms of psychopathology among the groups. Results indicated that, in general, patients in all three groups exhibited greater psychopathology than normal. The CTS and TMD patients exhibited greater psychopathology than did the LBP patients, with more CTS and TMD patients reporting at least one psychiatric disorder than LBP patients. Regarding prediction with the psychological variables, CTS patients with psychopathology were significantly more likely to report greater pain and disability at 6 months than those without psychopathology. For these patients, anxiety and personality disorders were the most important factors. In summary, greater psychopathology than normal in these three pain groups was found, with greatest psychopathology in the CTS and TMD patients. These results also reveal the presence of certain psychological predictors of pain and disability after 6 months, indicating that psychopathology can predict chronicity of pain for some patients. Finally, these results suggest important implications for treatment of individuals with CTS and indicate the impo (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
