AB  - Social influence theorists suggest that the initial power of a counselor to influence may be a pre-counseling phenomenon based on a potential client's acceptance of the counselor's legitimate cultural and institutional role as a source of professional help. The relation of ethnicity, gender, and level of acculturation to cognitive and behavioral expectations concerning help-seeking were investigated. The Expectations About Counseling-Brief Form, an acculturation scale, and a willingness to seek help problem checklist were completed by 122 Hispanic and 120 Asian American college students, and by 122 white, American students. Differences among ethnic groups, levels of acculturation, and gender were investigated with regard to two expectations about counseling factors (expectations about counselor expertise and facilitative conditions) and five willingness to seek help scales (depression, organization/motivation, self concept, substance abuse, and anxiety). Multivariate analyses of variance were performed. Significant main effects were found for ethnicity, acculturation, and gender. The white, American group were most willing to seek help for depression and the Hispanic American group were least willing. The Asian American group indicated greater willingness to seek help for self concept issues than white, Americans or Hispanic Americans. Least acculturated students indicated the highest expectations about counselor expertise. Females, regardless of ethnicity and acculturation, reported significantly greater willingness to seek help on four of the willingness scales as well as higher expectations about facilitative conditions, than males. Canonical correlations were performed in order to investigate the relation between expectations about counseling and willingness to seek help among the Hispanic and Asian American groups, and to examine whether those relationships changed as level of acculturation varied. There was a significant positive correlation between willingn (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
