AB  - Reports on State Vocational Rehabilitation (SVR) outcomes indicate that many SVR eligible clients who have severe psychiatric disabilities leave SVR prior to developing a rehabilitation plan, prior to completing their program, or prior to attaining an employment outcome. One variable which may contribute to this early departure is the individual's readiness to participate in rehabilitation activities. The purpose of this study was to ascertain if the Rehabilitation Introduction Group (RIG) promoted readiness that, in turn, resulted in differential outcomes and processes for individuals with severe psychiatric disabilities who applied for State Vocational Rehabilitation (SVR) services with or without participation in the RIG. The Rehabilitation Introduction Group was a classroom-based, six session, instructional intervention designed to heighten personal awareness of readiness for rehabilitation so that participants could make an informed choice about whether to participate in vocational rehabilitation. Follow up data, 24 months after SVR application date, indicate that the 39 clients who participated in the RIG did not have a significantly different experience with SVR processes after referral to SVR nor did they achieve significantly different employment outcomes than a matched sample of 39 SVR clients who did not participate in the RIG. Sample size and elements of the study design may have diminished the capacity to measure the actual effect of the RIG. Therefore, changes in the study methodology should be incorporated into new studies. Changes that are recommended include recruiting a larger sample, recruiting subjects who have a felt need for the RIG and who self select, adding a qualitative measure of client and counselor perceptions of readiness, employing an experimental research design rather than an ex post facto design, utilizing a revised RIG curriculum with a specific focus on SVR process and utilizing a longer follow up period to adequately assess the impact of the RIG over time. Although the results of this study were not able to demonstrate the effect of the RIG, the study did suggest that there is much more to learn about rehabilitation readiness, its assessment and its relationship to SVR outcomes. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
