AB  - Purpose. The purpose of this study was to investigate the effects of the addition of self-relevant mastery imagery to an inpatient substance abuse program based on Marlatt's model (Marlatt, 1985). The research questions investigated were (1) Does the use of self-relevant mastery imagery result in an increase in a patient's level of perceived self-efficacy for coping with high risk drinking situations? (2) Does the use of self-relevant mastery imagery result in a decrease in the number of drinking incidents or an increase in duration of the period of abstinence? Procedures. The subjects were 50 male veterans who were voluntarily recruited as they were admitted to an inpatient substance abuse treatment program. Participants completed the following instruments at time of admission: Beck Depression Inventory (BDI), State-Trait Anxiety Inventory (STAI), the Situational Confidence Questionnaire (SCQ), and the Inventory of Drinking Situations (IDS). The subjects completed the SCQ again on day 10 of treatment. Finally, at time of discharge each subject completed the BDI, STAI, and the SCQ. Subjects were randomly assigned to one of two groups. Treatment of the two groups was identical except that subjects in the treatment group engaged in self-relevant mastery imagery four times a week while in the program. Findings. Analysis of the data using 2 factor ANOVAs suggested no significant difference in level of self-efficacy between the two groups at time of discharge. Chi-square tests and a survival analysis indicated no significant differences between the groups at follow-up regarding number of drinking incidents or duration of abstinence. Conclusions. The following conclusions were drawn based on the findings of this study. (1) The addition of self-relevant mastery imagery did not result in significantly higher levels of self-efficacy for high risk drinking situations. (2) Treatment subjects did not report fewer incidents of drinking, or longer periods of abstinence than control subjects at follow-up. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
