AB  - The study proposes to provide insight into the ability of the Rorschach Inkblot Test to detect vicarious traumatization in law enforcement officers, in order to assist in the understanding of a law enforcement officer's psychological well being. Vicarious trauma may develop when an individual is exposed to indirect danger and is rendered powerless. Therefore, these experiences may then cause the individual impairment in daily functioning. It is this writer's hypothesis that the Rorschach Inkblot Test is an effective measure in identifying the presence of vicarious trauma in law enforcement officers when administered with the Briere's Trauma questionnaire. Current research is limited to primary trauma in the Rorschach Inkblot Test, indicating a need for future research. The research is intended to lend new insight into the ability of the Rorschach Inkblot Test. This writer has chosen the Rorschach specifically as the primary testing instrument in an attempt to support the writer's hypothesis: that the Rorschach is a beneficial psychological testing instrument in detecting vicarious trauma in law enforcement officers. The study's main focus was on detecting the presence of vicarious trauma in law enforcement officers. The N=25 Illinois State Certified Police Officers of which 21=Male and 4=Female with a mean age of 40 (SD=9.2). Subjects reported years of service M=11 years (SD=7.8). A few subjects reported a history of prior military experience (12%). Most subjects reported that they have feared for their life (76%) and they have been victims of abuse (64%). Results failed to support the hypothesis, since most police officers (76%) endorsed 'feared for life.' The researcher determined that primary trauma reactions may overshadow a vicarious trauma response as determined by the Rorschach and different measures may be required to separate primary trauma. It was further hypothesized that there would be a higher number of Aggressive and Morbid Specialty Scores amongst police officers. Only two subjects (10%) provided a single Aggressive response and 11 subjects (55%) provided one or more Morbid responses. It was hypothesized that there would be a correlation between color responses and responses on The Trauma Symptom Check list (TSC). Data screening was conducted. Color responses appeared to conform to the ordinal level of data. The utilization of these assessment instruments may assist in identifying the presence of vicarious traumatization; however, further research is needed. Upon acknowledging the presence of vicarious trauma in for law enforcement officers there may be an increase in de-briefings, as well as the formation of group psychotherapy to address the emotional needs of the officers. Thus, making an attempt to decrease the police officer's rate of domestic violence, depression, alcoholism and improve officer's interpersonal relationships. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
