AB  - Throughout his writings, assertions of truth's "irresistible advance" in human society are attended by troubled assessments of the means of truth's apprehension, and thus its realization as human knowledge.2 When, for example, Godwin offers in Political Justice (1793) "an idea of knowledge as perpetually gaining ground, unaccompanied with peril in the means of its diffusion," the picture collides with a litany of warnings and qualifications concerning how such an ideal can be achieved in the here-and-now.3 It is the "peril" that looms large throughout this treatise: text, speech, and even cognitive mechanisms are untrustworthy vessels, while political, religious, and domestic environments distort their mediatory work. Godwin contends in both that book-reading and conversation, properly conceived, should work in harmony to balance the speed of intellectual exchange, giving them complementary temporal roles in the social pursuit of knowledge.7 My argument here is that, as Godwin propounded this communicative ideal, he exposed the crosscurrent of philosophical commitment that underlay his enduring preoccupation with the means of intellectual advance. A book's "efficacy" as a vector is undermined by its rigid and uncompromising "constitution," thus apparently counteracting the very mental flexibility (or "elasticity") which features in Political Justice as the gateway to social justice and human achievement. In this critique, which Godwin reiterated in his Considerations on Lord Grenville's and Mr Pitt's Bills (1795), heat accompanies rapidity as a hallmark of unreflective exchange-the "cauldron of civil contention simmers"-indicating that his ascription of "constitutional coldness" to the codex is in part a counter-discourse to the dangers of oratorical enthusiasm.9 This context explains his emphasis upon both the slowness of books and the merits of small-group conversation among friends (as opposed to that of larger meetings or formal associations).
