AB  - Compared the psychosocial functioning of an untreated, community sample of 24 Ss with Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders-III-Revised (DSM-III-R) dysthymia at screening (drawn from 96 19–73 yr olds who responded by mail to newspaper advertisements soliciting depressed Ss) to that of a matched sample of 18 community nondepressed volunteers with no lifetime or current history of axis I disorders. Interviews and testing procedures revealed that Ss with dysthymia were less sociable (introversion) and clinically high on neuroticism/instability, external in regard to their causal attributions, less stable for positive uncontrollable events and more stable and global for negative uncontrollable events, relied more on coping strategies such as wishful thinking and self-blame, were more interpersonally submissive and hostile, and had a poorer social support-resource network. In addition, Ss with dysthymia reported more family dysfunction and a higher rate of negative major life events. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2020 APA, all rights reserved)
