AB  - Introduction: Falls are the leading cause of injury‐related deaths in those ≥65 y/o. Fall risk is even greater for those with heart failure (HF) due to decreased exercise capacity, loss of skeletal muscle and medication side effects. Resistance training (RT) is effective for improving skeletal muscle. However, the effect on improving balance is only modest, since balance is comprised of peripheral sensory input central integration and motor output. A multi‐component intervention focusing on balance retraining and strengthening the muscles supporting static/dynamic balance and functional mobility is needed. Purpose: The purpose of this pilot study was to evaluate the effect of a 12 week multi‐component balance and RT intervention on physical function, balance, and falls in older [≥65 years of age] HF patients. Methods: A randomized, two‐group with wait list control (WLC), repeated measures experimental design. Participants were recruited from a medical center HF clinic; supervised group sessions were conducted in the center's health and wellness facility. Those who met study criteria and passed the Mini Mental Status Exam ≥23 were tested at baseline, randomized to intervention (N = 15) or WLC group (N = 18), and re‐tested at 12 and 24 weeks. Focus groups were conducted pre/post intervention. The intervention involved once weekly supervised group sessions and twice weekly home sessions. Outcomes were the 30 Second Sit‐to‐Stand (STS), Modified Clinical Test of Sensory Interaction on Balance (CTSIBM), Activity Specific Balance Confidence Scale (ABC), Timed Up and Go (TUG), Dynamic Gait Index (DGI). Results and Conclusions: Participants' (N= 35, 2 lost to follow up; M = 79.36 years; 45.5% female; NYHAII 48.5%; NYHA III 51.5%) fall rate declined 38% during the study. Independent t‐tests indicated significant change in DGI between groups during the first 12 weeks (intervention M = 20.9, SD 1.8; WLC M = 18.9, SD 4.4; t (25) = 2.356, p =.029; effect size =.92). A medium effect was found for both CTSIBM time (effect size =.63) and sway velocity (effect size =.40). When the groups were combined for pre‐post analysis, DGI was the only outcome approaching significance (P =.068) and had a medium effect size (.38). Testing for maintenance in the intervention group at 24 weeks revealed essentially no change in the TUG and CTSIBM sway velocity. Other outcomes declined, showing small to medium effects (ABC effect size=.18; STS effect size=.38; CTSIBM time effect size=.40). Falls decreased during the Balance Activities and Strengthening to Improve Condition (BASIC): Training for Elders with HF pilot study. Further study should be undertaken to gain a more complete understanding of the effects of this intervention.
