AB  - Presents the findings of professionals who developed substance abuse services in a state psychiatric hospital. Substance abuse is defined as disorders characterized by problematic use of drugs, alcohol, or both, as outlined in the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders (DSM-III) categories. Those patients with this dual diagnosis of mental illness and substance abuse have historically been treated by either drug abuse or mental health systems. These patients need to have substance abuse and psychiatric issues addressed concomitantly rather than trying to determine cause and effect type relationships. By videotaping group counseling sessions it is possible to take advantage of consultations with other professionals. Case studies are used to illustrate the complicated nature and treatment of these dual diagnosis patients, who need a supportive nonjudgmental environment in which to work through the sometimes long and difficult therapeutic process. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2019 APA, all rights reserved)
