AB  - This study sought to determine whether individuals who are suffering from problems with alcohol also meet the criteria of dysthymia. Dysthymia is a chronic, low grade depression and is viewed as a nonspecific consequence of a variety of chronic life stressors and physical and mental disorders, particularly personality disorders. In this study, dysthymia was systematically evaluated within the context of other known predictors or correlates of depressive symptomatology: perceived stress, perceived social support, and religiosity (i.e., religious attitudes and behavior). Employing a naturalistic design, a sample of two hundred outpatient African American volunteers (126 males and 74 females, 21 to 67 years of age) diagnosed as alcoholics, were recruited from a clinic facility in Detroit, Michigan. The participants completed a questionnaire which included the Eysenck Personality Inventory (EPI) and several other self-report instruments: Khavari Alcohol Test (KAT), Perceived Stress Scale (PSS), Multidimensional Scale of Perceived Social Support (MSPSS), Religious Attitudes and Behavior (RABI), and the Wisconsin Substance Use Inventory (WSUI). The Eysenck Personality Inventory measured dysthymia as high neuroticism concomitant with low extraversion. The WSUI was utilized to differentiate polydrug users from primarily alcohol users (the focus of this research), but it was not used in the statistical analyses. The basic question to be answered was whether the diagnosed alcoholics in this study also met the criteria of dysthymia. Statistical procedures included correlational analysis and multiple regression analysis. These analyses were supplemented by analysis of variance and t-tests when appropriate. The findings of this study indicated that the participants presented evidence of dysthymic symptomatology. The presence of dysthymia in these participants was further substantiated by the significant associations of dysthymia with perceived stress (positive), perceived 