AB  - Chapter five chronicles the work of A.R. Kaufman and the birth control campaign of central Canada: "[t]he importance of the Ontario birth controllers was that they in effect made the advocacy of family planning respectable. Many of the first Canadian defenders of contraception had associated it... with sexual and political radicalism; the new birth controllers succeeded in presenting family planning as a force that would support rather than subvert existing social, political, and sexual relationships" (93). A lot of this history supports contemporary policy theory about morality politics, and one vivid illustration was the trial of Dorothea Palmer, who was acquitted of criminal charges of door-to-door canvassing for birth control in poor neighborhoods of the Ottawa Valley. It "was compared by some at the time as being to the birth controllers what the Scopes monkey trial had been to the evolutionists" and, when the acquittal was upheld by the Ontario appeal court, it "marked a breakthrough in the legitimation of birth control in Canada" (92). Palmer was a nurse who worked for Kaufman (a factory owner), so Kaufman was heavily involved in her litigation as well as bankrolling the advocacy work of A.H. Tyrer, author of the best-selling Sex, Marriage and Birth Control. Tyrer became the propagandist of the birth control movement in Canada, but his benefactor exacted a price insofar as Tyrer was urged to incorporate eugenics as a defense of contraception. As to the collective impact of the leading birth controllers, "[t]he conservative-minded were only willing to rally to the birth control cause in the depression when Tyrer, [Mary] Hawkins [who started a birth control clinic in Ontario], and Kaufman played up the idea that contraception would bring down welfare costs. Birth control ideology was embraced by the middle classes once they understood how it could be turned to the control of the reproduction of the lower orders rather than to their liberation" (123). Chapter six looks at Quebec. The birth of the Dionne quintuplets in 1934 was "for the English the 'symbol of a great fear'" (126), and Kaufman began publicizing birth control in Quebec in 1935 to counteract the high French birthrate. Of course the dramatic fall in its birthrate twenty years later had nothing to do with Kaufman or others and, in fact, "the openly anti-Catholic diatribes of Kaufman and Tyrer" provided ammunition for Catholic writers who saw an Anglophone plot against them. Quebec offers an intriguing story for the 1960s: Catholic bishops realized that church teaching held little sway with lay members; reform of the Criminal Code was led by Quebec politician Pierre Trudeau (then Justice Minister); and Dr. Morgentaler publicized his abortion clinic in Montreal.
