AB  - Examined the effects of pretreatment psychiatric status and treatment duration on improvement following drug abuse rehabilitation. The effects were measured in terms of whether patients who received longer treatment showed greater improvement at a 6-mo follow-up and whether treatment duration and amount of improvement were similar for outcome criteria (drug use, employment, and crime), psychiatric severity subgroups (low, mid, and high), and treatment modalities (therapeutic communities [TCs] and methadone maintenance [MM]). 118 veterans (mean age 29 yrs) were in the TC, and 154 Ss (mean age 32 yrs) were in the MM program. Ss were administered a battery of tests, including the Beck Depression Inventory and the Addiction Severity Index. All measures showed a positive relation between treatment duration and amount of improvement. Low-severity Ss in both programs showed the greatest levels of improvement, more improvement at shorter treatment durations, and less additional improvement at longer durations. Mid-severity Ss in both programs showed the most dramatic effects of treatment duration, with large improvements on all criteria at greater treatment lengths. High-severity MM Ss showed low levels of improvement, but longer treatment durations were associated with modest improvements. High-severity TC Ss also showed low levels of improvement, but they showed worsened status with greater treatment duration. (23 ref) (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2019 APA, all rights reserved)
