AB  - The maladaptive expression of anger has been associated with substance abuse and aggressive behaviors and tendencies. Substance abuse patients who express anger frequently and intensely may be at great risk for relapse. Demographic trends and addiction profile studies show that substance use among older Americans will be a major health concern in the near future. Unfortunately, substance abusing seniors receive fewer treatment services than any other age group. This study examined the effects of anger management treatment on senior substance abusers.The pretest-posttest, wait-list control group research design applied a twelve week cognitive-behavioral intervention for thirty senior (age 65 and older) male substance abusers. The randomly assigned experimental group, which consisted of fifteen participants, received the treatment while the control group, also consisting of fifteen participants, did not. All participants were assessed before, during and after the intervention for levels of anger measured as by The State-Trait Anger Expression Inventory, assaultive behavior as measured by the Assaultive Behavior Survey, and substance use as measured by The Quantitative Drug Use Inventory and corroborated by third party reports of weekly urine toxicology screens.Findings show that each participant entered this study with a high level of anger. There was statistically significant improvements on all reported measures by the experimental group. No relapses were reported for either the experimental or the control group. Between group analyses revealed significant treatment effects. The findings from this study suggest that mental health practitioners formally address the management of clients' anger in the treatment of substance abuse. By showing that severely angry participants can increase their control over anger while maintaining abstinence, this study sounds a note of cautious optimism.
