AB  - Background: Opioid abuse is epidemic in the United States and the demand for more providers offering medication-assisted treatment (MAT) options for opioid dependence is high. Standing alongside methadone maintenance, buprenorphine/naloxone treatment has continued to gain popularity for patients who are deemed appropriate for MAT since its FDA approval in October 2002. However, longitudinal outcome studies of buprenorphine/naloxone treatment have been meager at best, having focused on the simplistic results of treatment retention and urine drug screens. Although informative, such results give no broad measure of a patient's overall treatment success, or lack thereof, in terms of global improvement or restoration of function. At present, there remains limited data to guide providers on the optimal length of therapy or adjunctive interventions to help increase outcome success. The Drug Abuse Reporting Program (DARP) Outcome Criteria, an underrecog-nized rating scale first published in 1975, remains a viable and comprehensive measurement of the global outcome of drug treatment. Using the modified DARP criteria, the purpose of this study was to evaluate if the length of buprenorphine/naloxone therapy and patient participation in a peer-support group increased the likeliness of treatment success. Method(s): A retrospective chart review was completed on patients who had received at least 6 consecutive months of buprenorphine/naloxone therapy through the Minneapolis Veterans Affairs Health Care System (MVAHCS). Modified DARP criteria were collected on each patient by means of either chart review or patient interview. For patients who were currently receiving therapy, 2 modified DARP scores were obtained: (1) at treatment initiation and (2) current score within the past 3 months. For veterans who were no longer receiving therapy, 3 modified DARP scores were obtained: (1) at treatment initiation, (2) treatment completion and (3) a current score within the past 3 months. Result(s): A total of 85 patients who had undergone buprenorphine/naloxone therapy (43 who were still on therapy and 42 who had completed therapy) were included in this analysis. Treatment success, as determined by decreased DARP scores by at least 7 points, was seen significantly more often in those patients who were still on buprenorphine/naloxone therapy when compared with those no longer on therapy (100% vs 20%, respectively). Although continued buprenorphine/naloxone therapy demonstrated a much higher incidence of outcome success, no clear correlation was identified between the degree of outcome success and length of therapy or participation in peer-support group therapy. Conclusion(s): Although this analysis did not detect a correlation between outcome success and length of buprenorphine/naloxone therapy or participation in a peer-support group, it did provide important insight into the treatment of opioid dependence with this medication. Additionally, this study highlights the utility of the DARP in measuring broader definitions of treatment success and global function over time. Outcomes support the current guideline recommendations that length of buprenorphine/naloxone therapy should not be rigidly predetermined and should be individualized based on patient progress and tolerability of the treatment.
