AB  - Traditional treatment approaches have stressed the need to separate the mentally ill person from the presumedly pathological family, or to carry out conjoint therapy to understand & alter conditions such as "double-binds" & "psuedomutuality." In recent years, there has been a recognition of the need to reconceptualize the nature of individual & family pathology. More recent assessments of the fundamental concepts of family systems theory, as well as research findings from both biological & psychological studies suggest that more clinical success could be achieved with approaches that are less therapeutic & more educational or didactic. Reviewed are selected past & current concepts from family systems theory as these apply to treatment models. The implications of models of biochemical causation, crisis management, & parent training are also discussed as these apply to more supportive (& less confrontive & accusatory) & more didactic models for ongoing care for the chronically mentally ill. Such a focus is also a major step toward validating the painful experiences of families with a disabled member & toward empowering them as consumers of service. It also encourages formation of supportive groups of families who are struggling with similar concerns of stigma, daily management of problematic behavior, & responses to repeated crises. The rethinking of the models, & the alterations in practice, also have significant implications for the training of social workers, especially those enrolled in more clinically focused programs. 23 References. HA
