AB  - The study tested goal setting theory which predicts that people who set clear, specific goals will have more positive outcomes than those who do not. The purpose of this study was twofold: first, to examine outcomes for patients admitted to a dual diagnosis unit compared to a general psychiatric treatment unit and second, to determine if goals mediate the relationship between receiving inpatient treatment and sobriety measures two months and six months after treatment. It is hypothesized that goals would mediate the relationship between receiving specialized inpatient treatment and sobriety six months after discharge. Patients were randomly assigned to specialized dual diagnosis inpatient treatment designated as the experimental group and general treatment designated as the control group. Four hundred and sixty-seven patients admitted to a state psychiatric hospital participated in the study. Approximately 75% of the participants are African-American and 25% are Caucasian. A longitudinal analysis of admission, discharge, and six-month post-discharge interviews used descriptive statistics, Pearson correlation, and chi-square analyses. Patients who received treatment on the dual diagnosis ward had lower Addiction Severity Index (ASI) Alcohol Composite Scores and were less likely to use alcohol thirty days before the second follow-up interview than patients on the usual psychiatric treatment ward. There was no difference between the two treatment groups regarding the ASI Drug Composite scores and likelihood for drug use thirty days before the second follow-up interview. Having specific goals or sobriety related goals did not relate to six-month alcohol and drug sobriety scores for either treatment group. Although the participants were optimistic about their goals and their chances of achieving them, goals did not mediate the relationship between inpatient treatment and six-month sobriety outcomes. The research findings suggest that goal specificity was not a valid predictor of sobriety outcomes six months after discharge from inpatient treatment. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2019 APA, all rights reserved)
