AB  - Medicine and Culture: Varieties of Treatment in the United States, England, West Germany and France by Lynn Payer (New York: Henry Holt and Co., 1988), 204 pp., $18.95 The Illness Narratives: Suffering, Healing and the Human Condition by Arthur Kleinman (New York: Basic Books, 1988), 284 pp., $19.95_ Reviewed by David Mechanic As the United States increasingly applies sophisticated strategies and tools to contain the rising costs of medical care, we readily delude ourselves that the health challenge is simply one of appropriately allocating the fruits of biomedical advance. In the social sciences, the study of national character is a discredited activity criticized for its failure to deal with complex variations characteristic of all countries, its dependence on ex post facto interpretations, and the common use of tautology (for example, American doctors are aggressive because Americans have aggressive characters). The healing relationship has many potential sources of instability and is typically characterized by uncertainty; beliefs influence much that takes place and help explain many of the good treatment results physicians achieve-in more technical terms, the "placebo effect." Kleinman argues that the physician's capacity to interpret the patient's illness as well as diagnose the disease is particularly pertinent to chronic disorders where effective clinicians seek to contain disability, enhance function, and maintain a coherent and meaningful view of the future.
