AB  - Studied the effectiveness and long-term outcomes of Morita therapy in the treatment of obsessive–compulsive disorder. 64 inpatients with obsessive–compulsive disorder in Suzhou, China, were assigned into a pharmacological therapy group (31 Ss, mean age: 34.48 yrs) and a combined Morita and pharmacological therapy group (33 Ss, mean age: 31.67 yrs). Ss were assessed with the Yale-Brown Obsessive Compulsive Scale and the Hamilton Anxiety Rating Scale before treatment and at 6 wks, and then at 6 mo after treatment, and again at 1 yr after hospital discharge. The results of clinical effectiveness of the therapies were compared between groups among therapy periods. Ss' social functions were assessed with the Social Disability Screening Schedule 1 yr after Ss' hospital discharge, and the results were studied between groups. The lower scores of obsessive thoughts, anxiety symptoms, and social disability in the combined therapy group Ss were discussed, indicating the long-term effectiveness of Morita therapy in improving social functions and living quality. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2020 APA, all rights reserved)
