AB  - The concepts of social support and stigma are widely known within mental health literature. Social support has been found to positively impact physical health, mental health and reduce stress. Conversely, stigma can have debilitating effects on individuals living with mental illness. While research on these concepts is plentiful, few studies have specifically examined the complex relationship between them. Drawing upon an anti-oppressive practice framework, the relationship between social support and stigma experiences was investigated and issues faced within social support relationships were explored. This thesis used data from a large-scale participatory-action research program examining issues of poverty and social inclusion for individuals with lived experience of mental illness. A secondary analysis using mixed methodology was conducted. A total of 380 individuals with a mental health diagnosis participated in the quantitative interviews and a subset of the quantitative sample participated in subsequent focus group sessions (n=46). Quantitative instruments included the Personal Resource Questionnaire and the Stigma Scale and analyses were conducted to determine the relationship between social support, demographic characteristics and stigma experiences. Qualitative methods involved a descriptive analysis of focus group data to explore the process of navigating social support relationships as described by participants. The findings indicate that social support predicts variations in stigma experiences. Further, participants described unique experiences within social support networks and identified a number of barriers in the development of supportive relationships. The findings contribute to mental health knowledge and practice by outlining various individual, community and policy issues in need of redress to assist individuals with lived experience of mental illness in developing supportive networks. Copies of dissertations may be obtained by addressing your request to ProQuest, 789 E. Eisenhower Parkway, P.O. Box 1346, Ann Arbor, MI 48106-1346. Telephone 1-800-521-3042; e-mail: disspub@umi.com
