AB  - Examined reasons for the low rates of voluntary entry into aftercare from prison-based therapeutic community (TC) programs. This was based on a theoretical formulation of the TC process involving the effects on aftercare entry of the interaction of clients' motivation and participation in the activities of the TC. Ss were 110 adult volunteers with drug problems in a prison-based TC for whom there were client and staff ratings of 3-mo participation and 12-mo follow-up data on (drug use) relapse and recidivism. Results of path analyses support a model in which the interaction of motivation and 3-mo participation ratings have a direct effect on the selection of aftercare, and aftercare has a direct effect on relapse and recidivism. The use of a combination of enhanced motivation and early program participation as a means of increasing the utilization and effectiveness of aftercare is discussed. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
