AB  - Measures of psychopathology among mentally ill male chemical abusers (aged 21 yrs and older) were examined as predictors of levels of functioning in 2 types of community-based, residential programs: therapeutic community (TC) and community residence (CR). The TC features well-defined rules, group therapies, and expectations for a global change in lifestyle, while CR aims to provide the least restrictive alternative to inpatient care. Six measurements of psychopathology and 5 measurements of interpersonal functioning were obtained at the beginning and end of a 12 mo treatment. Non-significant associations were generally observed between scales of psychiatric symptoms (e.g., depression, psychotic ideation, cognitive disorientation, and hostility) and counselors' ratings of the residents' capacity to meet the social and interpersonal expectations of the programs (e.g., personal care, involvement in interpersonal relationships, and development of work skills). The study suggests that individuals with moderately severe psychopathology can be successfully engaged in residential treatment, even in programs with relatively high expectations for interpersonal involvement and functioning, such as the therapeutic community. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2017 APA, all rights reserved)
