AB  - Assessed the training needs of 205 community mental health residence workers who regularly had to deal with mentally ill chemical abusing (MICA) clients. A focus group, key informant interviews, and a forced-choice questionnaire were used to identify practice issues that Ss found difficult, their preparation to address care issues, preferred training topics, and work methods for intervention with MICA clients. Results indicate Ss found MICA clients difficult to assist; Ss were also inadequately trained or supervised to support these residents. Ss had limited education, and few had received training about MICA treatment or had the required skills. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
