AB  - The prevalence of medical problems is high among those with alcohol and drug use disorders. Little is known about how patterns of remission and relapse in these individuals are related to morbidity andmortality. Although medical problems are often worsened by alcohol or drug use, many individuals remit or reduce use in response tomedical problems. In the current study we examine how trajectories of remission from alcohol and drug use are related to mortality by 13 years after index treatment in a sample of 1204 individuals with intakes into outpatient alcohol and drug treatment in a private, integrated health care system.We also compare mortality among these individuals to that among 5060 demographically matched controls fromthe health plan without alcohol or drug use disorders. Data included interviews at baseline and follow-up at 1-year and every two years beginning at 5-years. Mortality records data were extracted from both public agencies and the health plan. We used a group-based modeling approach to identify trajectory groups based on repeated measures of remission and identified three distinct groups. These groups were labeled "High" (i.e., high rates of remission over time), "Declining" (i.e., moderate rates of remission in early years and declining over time) and "Low" (i.e., low rates of remission and high rates of alcohol and drug use).We found that dierences in raw mortality rates did not significantly dier by trajectory group.We also examine dierences in Standardized Mortality Ratios. Themortality rates among those treated for alcohol and drug use disorders was higher (7.1%) than in demographically matched controls (2.8%). This increased mortality risk for alcohol and drug use disorder patients versus matched controls was pronounced in women and older adults whose mortality ratio compared to controls was strikingly high. Most common cause of deaths in cases were heart disease (25%), digestive system disorders including liver cirrhosis (17%), cancer (14%), suicide (10%) and infectious disease (5%). The study highlights the ongoing poorer health prognosis for those with alcohol and drug use disorders.
