AB  - As part of Phase II of the World Health Organization (WHO) Injecting Drug Use Multi-City Study, rapid assessment & response methodology was used in Sao Vicente (Sao Paulo State, Brazil) to analyze the situation regarding injecting drug use & HIV (human immunodeficiency virus) infection. Over a period of 8 weeks, existing information was analyzed & 4 focus groups & 45 depth interviews were conducted; qualitative data analysis was undertaken during a further 12 weeks. This work was conducted by a team of six researchers, & the research process was overseen by an advisory community group. The general drug use situation; HIV/AIDS (acquired immune deficiency syndrome) & drugs; & harm reduction were addressed. Analyses reveal a negative moral status associated with drug use & drug users; prejudices against this group; & a lack of information about health services & HIV prevention programs within Sao Vicente. Injecting drug users reported awareness of risks & methods of prevention; however, safer sex was considered difficult by all participants. Interventions were proposed to minimize problems & gaps in provision, & some of these have already been implemented. A review of existing quantitative data found that some key institutions do not collect systematic data on drug use. 2 Tables, 15 References. Adapted from the source document.
