AB  - Examined the effects of therapeutic community (TC) treatment on drug-addicted individuals with antisocial personality disorder (APD). 275 individuals (aged 20-54 yrs) participating in 2 TCs were diagnosed with APD according to Millon Clinical Multiaxial Inventory-II (MCMI-II) criteria. Ss completed follow-up interviews and provided urine samples. Results show that 187 Ss met MCMI-II criteria for APD. An MCMI-II diagnosis of APD was unrelated to all examined treatment outcomes, including treatment completion, drug use at follow-up, and postdischarge arrest. Treatment completion was the most important factor in reducing recent drug use and postdischarge arrests. It is concluded that individuals diagnosed with APD, with histories of substantial drug abuse and criminality, can benefit from TC treatment with aftercare in the community or at the very least, do as well as those with no APD. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2019 APA, all rights reserved)
