AB  - Identifying and accurately treating psychiatric disorders is persons with developmental disabilities are particularly difficult due to the fact that many of these individuals are nonverbal or unable to communicate internal states. Information necessary for understanding problems being experienced by people with mental retardation is often communicated to mental health professionals by secondary sources called Direct Care Personnel. Direct care personnel are those who are employed to work daily with individuals with developmental disabilities in order to help them achieve the tasks of daily living and maintain personal safety. Training in dual diagnosis, or the coexistence of a mental retardation and mental illness, is particularly necessary for direct care staff in order to ensure that what they report to mental health professionals on behalf of persons with mental retardation is accurate, objective, and complete. Providing quantity and quality information about dual diagnosis to direct care staff has previously been compromised by a number of variables currently operating within the field of developmental disabilities, including a lack of adequate training materials, an exorbitant rate of staff turnover, and a lack of professionals and paraprofessionals who are trained/educated in mental retardation, mental illness, and dual diagnosis. In response to these variables, the curriculum entitled 'Psychiatric Disorders in Individuals with Developmental Disabilities: A Curriculum for Direct Service Personnel' has been written. This curriculum seeks to provide direct care personnel with the knowledge and abilities needed to ensure that the identification, assessment, diagnosis, and treatment of persons with mental retardation who may be suffering from a mental illness are conducted and carried out according to the most appropriate means possible. Using a Posttest-Only Control Group design, the usefulness of this curriculum was assessed, and the forty-two randomly assigned direct care personnel who participated in the two-day training on Dual Diagnosis performed significantly better (p <.05) on a test designed to measure knowledge and problem-solving skills related to dual diagnosis than the forty-two randomly assigned direct care personnel who did not receive the two-day training on Dual Diagnosis. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
