AB  - Of all the experiments now being launched under the Affordable Care Act of 2010, none carries human and financial stakes as great as the effort to transform care for the 9.2 million individuals enrolled in both Medicare and Medicaid. These "dual eligibles" are many of the nation's sickest and most vulnerable adults; their care costs totaled an estimated $319.5 billion in 2011. Dual eligibles thus represent a formidable challenge. To help states design better ways to coordinate care and to reduce incentives for shuttling patients between nursing homes or other long-term care settings and hospitals, Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services has shared real-time Medicare claims data with the states so they can see how individual dual eligibles are using services across both Medicare and Medicaid. A major piece of an overall solution, most experts agree, is to nudge duals into integrated care.
