AB  - Introduction: There is a nationwide heroin epidemic in the US, and unfortunately the greater Cincinnati area is # 6 on the list of the 10 cities in the United States with the highest overdose rate. Deaths involving heroin nearly quadrupled in the USA since 2000, with the greatest increase seen in the Midwest. Cincinnati had 264 deaths from overdose in 2016. The CF population is not immune from this epidemic. Literature suggests a correlation with depression and substance abuse. It's estimated that up to one-third of people with a serious medical condition have symptoms of depression. Depression is one of the most common complications of chronic illness, including cystic fibrosis. Depression and substance abuse can feed into each other, and one condition will often make the other worse. Depression and heroin use is a significant issue in our CF center. We have now begun screening our population for depression utilizing the guidelines from the Cystic Fibrosis Foundation to see if we can correlate depression scores and substance abuse. Method(s): We reviewed our patient population for known substance abuse first, and then reviewed our A&D screening. A PubMed search was performed for articles emphasizing the subject headings "chronic disease and depression" and "substance abuse and depression." We made surveys of the addiction treatment centers in the greater Cincinnati area, and reviewed admission criteria and availability. Result(s): The data results from our depression/anxiety surveys (PHQ & GAD) show 128 patients screened (94%). Of the patients screened, 29 patients (23%) scored mild; 25 patients (20%) scored moderate or higher. Mild scoring is 5-10 indicating need for increased monitoring. Ten or greater is moderate to high indicating that intervention is likely needed. To date our CF center has had 5 cases of heroin overdose resulting in 2 deaths. Depression screening was only available on one of the 5 patients with a heroin overdose. On 1/5/17 this patient scored PHQ-9: 22 (range for severe depression) and GAD-7: 21 (range for severe anxiety). Of the 7 Addiction/Rehab Centers in Cincinnati, the majority have a wait list ranging from a 3- 12 months. Many do not accept patients without private insurance or chronic medical conditions such as CF. Conclusion(s): Treatment programs for substance abuse are limited and can have long waiting lists as well as insurance constraints. Most programs are not equipped to treat those with chronic medical issues such as CF. Increasing early diagnosis of and response to depressive symptoms may help reduce substance abuse. Consistent screenings for depression within our CF care centers are crucial. Resources such as a Mental Health Coordinator as part of the CF team are essential for counseling, referrals, and help in navigating mental health care and treatment.
