AB  - Background: 'Touch points' - the crucial moments in a patient's journey that make a difference (positive or negative) to a service user's, family member's or service provider's experience of a care process - must be handled well in a patient-centred health system, particularly during periods of vulnerability (e.g., transitions between providers) and among the most vulnerable (e.g., youth with mental disorder). Aims of Study: To identify the key touch points and underlying values that powerfully shape experiences of transitioning from youth to adult mental health services in from the perspectives of young people, family members and service providers. Method(s): Secondary analysis of interview data from youth (16 participants) aged 16 to 24 receiving mental health services, their family members (9 participants) and services providers (10 participants) during the transition from child to adult mental health services gathered in Ontario over the period August 2014 to December 2015. Employing interpretive phenomenological analysis, emergent themes were coded and analyzed to identify key touch points and values from the three perspectives. Three focus groups - one for each participant type - were hosted to validate and prioritize the most important touch points to address. At each of the identified touch points we explored associated underlying values as a basis to develop 'design principles' for youth mental health services across the province. Result(s): Three key touch points were heard consistently from all three participant types: the value of a positive patient-provider relationship, frustration with arbitrary age cut-offs (typically at age 16 or 18) for youth services, and frustration with the lack of knowledge about services available to transition age youth. Underlying values for youth included have the right to participate in decision making processes about their own care, for family members to be informed about medication changes that affect youth living under their roof, and for service providers to be able to be able to recommend when age cuts off should be interpreted more flexibly for the youth in their care. Discussion/Limitation: Many jurisdictions prioritize patient-centredness and these findings offer a tangible way to harness the experiences of service users, families and service providers in identifying priorities for service improvement and transitions more broadly. The focus on a single geographic jurisdiction in validating touch points is a limitation of this study. Implications for Healthcare Provision: This research facilitates a shared understanding of the different experiences across the participant perspectives. Implications for Health Policies: Findings of this research support the patient-centered approach to policy making and facilitate prioritizing negative touch points as and area for quality improvement. Implications for Future Research: Future research could explore co-design as a method for developing interventions to improve experiences at negative touch points and develop 'design principles' that could improve transitions more broadly.
