AB  - Background: Mental disorders are highly prevalent and associated with considerable disease burden and health expenditure both internationally and in Australia. Suicide is an important sequel of mental disorders, though not all suicides are associated with mental disorders. While there is an increasing emphasis on the prevention of chronic physical diseases such as cancer, heart disease and diabetes, this is not as evident for mental disorders. The reasons for this are numerous and include a perception that mental disorders are not preventable since there is no easily identifiable and modifiable constellation of risk factors. However there is an emerging and sizable literature demonstrating that there are a number of interventions, largely psychological in nature, which can be effective in preventing the incidence of some mental disorders, particularly major depression in adults and children. Unsurprisingly, the cost-effectiveness of these interventions is largely unknown. Aims of the Study: The current study assessed the population costeffectiveness of 12 preventive interventions for mental disorders, including adult major depressive disorder, post-natal depression, suicide, psychotic disorders, childhood depression and childhood anxiety. Method(s): The study used standard methods modelling outcomes in Disability-Adjusted Life Years [DALY]) as part of a larger study evaluating the cost-effectiveness of preventive interventions for non-communicable disease within Australia called the 'ACE-Prevention study'. Result(s): Preventive psychological strategies for adult, childhood depression and post-natal depression; a parenting intervention for the prevention of anxiety disorders in young children; early interventions for psychotic disorders; and problem-solving therapy for people who deliberately self-harm can provide very good value for money when a threshold of AUD $50,000 per DALY averted is used. Legislation covering the ownership and storage of firearms along with a gun-buyback scheme was not cost-effective and emergency contact cards for people who deliberately self-harm was found not to be effective. Discussion(s): (with limitations of the study) Issues such as work force availability and financing mechanisms need to be addressed before such interventions can become routinely available. The application of a consistent methodology to mental disorders proved to be challenging as there are important data gaps, particularly associated with the epidemiology of such disorders. Implications for Health Care Provision and Use: Mental health service providers need to consider offering interventions designed to prevent mental disorders as such interventions are as cost-effective, if not more cost-effective than many treatments for mental disorders. Implications for Health Policies: Health policies should include reference to preventive interventions for mental disorders, particularly interventions for depression, anxiety and psychosis as effective and cost-effective interventions. Implications for Further Research: Future research is required to fill some important epidemiological data gaps for mental disorders as well as evaluate efficacious preventive interventions under routine health service conditions.
