AB  - Fibromyalgia syndrome (FM) is a chronic pain illness that is often very difficult to treat and is poorly understood by physicians, patients, and the general public. The underlying cause or causes, as well as the pathophysiology of fibromyalgia continue to remain a mystery to physicians and researchers alike. In addition, current treatments focus more on relieving symptoms than curing the disorder. Pharmacological, nonpharmacological, cognitive-behavioral, exercise, multi-modal, and complementary and alternative medicine (CAM) treatments have all been used with varying success. Yoga is one treatment that combines mindfulness and awareness components found in many CAM treatments with the gentle movement found in exercise. Additionally, yoga is easily accessible and relatively inexpensive when compared to the cost of taking multiple trips to the doctor's office or to treatments such as group therapy. To date, only two trials have been conducted using yoga as a treatment for FM and both have shown promising results in reduction of pain, improved sleep quality, and improved pain coping skills. The purpose of this study aims to further test the effectiveness of gentle yoga compared to cognitive behavioral therapy (another non-pharmacological form of treatment that has been found to have varying success in treating FM) by looking at changes in general symptoms of FM, psychosocial adjustment, and physiology. Participants with physician diagnosed FM were recruited from the community. 55 participants were randomly assigned to either gentle yoga group or CBT group. The groups meet for a total of eight weeks, with the yoga group meeting twice a week for an hour and fifteen minutes and the CBT group meeting once a week for two hours and fifteen minutes. Primary outcome measures were taken pre and post treatment and they include FM symptoms (pain, fatigue, sleep quality, disability, anxiety, depression, helplessness, coping, self-efficacy), neurocognitive functioning (short and long term memory, recall vs. recognition, and auditory vs. visual modalities), and physiology (heart rate, heart rate variability, and breath rate). Final data analyzed included 47 participants (24 yoga and 23 CBT) ages 18-65 who been diagnosed with FM for .30-24 years (M = 7.96). Yoga participants reported significant improvements in pain, fatigue, sleep, depression, helpless, coping, self-efficacy, memory, certain measurements of HRV, and respiration. CBT participants reported many of the same improvements. Results suggest that yoga may another non-pharmacological treatment approach for managing the symptoms of fibromyalgia. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2019 APA, all rights reserved)
