AB  - Evaluated whether interventions that promote guideline-concordant treatments for depression among young, predominantly minority women would improve rates of appropriate care and clinical and functional outcomes. After screening, 427 women completed a structured psychiatric diagnostic telephone interview and met criteria for the study. 267 Ss completed a subsequent clinical interview, consented to treatment, and were randomized into the intervention trials. Ss were randomized as follows: antidepressant medications (ADs; paroxetine, with a switch to buproprion in cases of nonresponse) administered by a primary care nurse practitioner in consultation with a psychiatrist (n=88), cognitive behavioral therapy (CBT) conducted by a psychologist (n=90), or referral to community mental health services (n=89). Clinical outcomes were determined through a mixed-effects repeated-measures analysis comparing mean depression symptom and functioning scores across treatment groups over successive time periods. Results show that ADs and CBT were both significantly more effective than no treatment in decreasing depressive symptoms. ADs were superior in improving instrumental role functioning, and ADs and CBT resulted in improved social functioning compared to no treatment. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2020 APA, all rights reserved)
