AB  - The purpose of this study was to investigate treatment conditions, psychosocial variables, and physical diagnoses in order to determine trends for chronic temporomandibular disorder participants' response to treatment. Ninety-four patients with chronic temporomandibular disorder were assigned to either a biofeedback treatment group, a cognitive-behavioral skills training (CBST) treatment group, a combined (combination of biofeedback/CBST) treatment group, or a no-treatment control group. Pain scores were analyzed pre-treatment and post-treatment to determine group and within-subjects treatment effects. With regards to a self-reported pain score, participants in the biofeedback group were significantly improved compared to the no-treatment group. Moreover, all three treatment groups had significantly decreased pain scores from pre-treatment to post-treatment, while the no-treatment group did not. Furthermore, participants in the three treatment groups displayed significant improvement in mood states. Patients were also classified as Adaptive Copers, Interpersonally Distressed, or Dysfunctional, according to Turk and Rudy's system (1988). Results showed that those classified as Adaptive Copers and Dysfunctional responded best to treatment according to a pain and disability score, while the Interpersonally Distressed patients did not. However, the Dysfunctionals did not improve on a depression score, whereas the Adaptive Coper and Interpersonally Distressed groups did. Current Axis I disorder, current Axis I and subthreshold Axis I disorder, and past or current major depressive disorder or subthreshold major depressive disorder were examined to determine whether those diagnosed with these disorders responded less favorably to treatment compared to those without these diagnoses. No significant results were found. Moreover, no significant differences in response to treatment were found between those diagnosed with personality disorders and those without a personality disorder. SCID Axis I diagnoses, SCID Axis II diagnoses, and RDC Axis I diagnoses were examined to determine if any significant relationships existed. Significant relationships were found among: past anxiety disorder, personality disorder, and disk displacement; past anxiety disorder, personality disorder, and muscle disorder; current Axis I disorder, personality disorder, and disk displacement; current mood disorder, personality disorder, and disk displacement; current mood disorder, personality disorder, and muscle disorder. It was also found that the participants with a high acceptance of treatment goals were more likely to be responders to treatment relative to those with a low acceptance of treatment goals. Finally, psychological diagnoses and several psychosocial variables were analyzed to determine if they predicted good response to treatment. High acceptance of treatment goals predicted good response to treatment according to a pain measure, and the absence of a Cluster C personality disorder predicted good response to treatment according to a pain measure which takes disability into account. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
