AB  - Inhibition reflects the cognitions and actions that encompass the ability to effectively develop goals and consider the consequences of one's behaviors in response to environmental demands. Difficulties with inhibition are common among a wide spectrum of psychiatric disorders ranging from neurodevelopmental disorders in childhood to major neurocognitive disorders, commonly known as dementia, in late adulthood. Additionally, impulsive actions are often associated with substance use, traumatic brain injuries, as well as mood and trauma related disorders. One population uniquely affected by the aforementioned conditions is that of veterans. The goal of this study was to examine how the presence of a co-occurring disorder such as a mild traumatic brain injury or posttraumatic stress disorder influenced inhibitory control among veterans from the Operation Enduring Freedom and Operation Iraqi Freedom conflicts with alcohol use disorder. Additionally, any differences between an endorsed pre-deployment mental health condition and inhibitory control were explored. Inhibition was measured by examining the omission, commission, and variability rates from the Connors Performance Test as well as the rate of delay discounting as measured from the Money Choice Questionnaire. Data were abstracted from 29 participants enrolled in a larger study conducted at Edward Hines Jr., Veterans Affairs Medical Center. Participant data were separated into two groups based on presence of co-occurring condition and compared via the use of independent t tests. No significant differences were found between groups based on co-occurring conditions or presence of a pre-deployment condition. However, there was wide variability in inhibition scores among participants in both groups indicating that there may be additional factors that influence performance in veterans with alcohol use disorder. Implications for future directions, current recommended treatments and complimentary alternative treatments are discussed. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2020 APA, all rights reserved)
