AB  - The joint Research Committee of the National Adult Protective Services Association and the National Committee for the Prevention of Elder Abuse, which began in the 1990s, is an inter-disciplinary group of individuals trained in gerontology, medicine, mental health, social work, sociology, public health, and related fields. The committee's goals are to promote high-quality research in the area of elder and vulnerable adult abuse, and support the application of research findings by Adult Protective Services (APS) to inform and strengthen their practice of investigating abuse reports and providing effective interventions to victims. The group engages in an exchange of ideas during monthly conference calls, and holds an annual, in-person meeting; these activities help to promote the committee's goals. Another major committee undertaking has been the creation of "Guiding Principles for Research in APS" and "Guidelines for Evaluating and Applying Research in Adult Protective Services." These are posted on the NAPSA Web site and the principles have also been published. Adapted from the source document.
