AB  - The article discusses how historically, mentally ill patients have been treated in seemingly contradictory ways. It reports that the social movement for mentally ill patients' rights and protections had its greatest impetus in the 1960s as part of the larger social reform and civil rights movements of that time. Advocacy at that time had two occasionally conflicting foci, which are the right to access services and the right to protection and involvement in decision-making once engaged in treatment. Advocacy groups for the latter focused primarily on people who were involuntarily committed to psychiatric facilities, but soon broadened their efforts to include those with mental retardation and those committed to long-term care institutions. Groups advocating for the right to access services concentrated on calling for a range of comprehensive and continuous professional services, stall and facilities and sought legislation and funding to ensure that the government assumed responsibility for implementing the right to treatment.
