AB  - Background: The ACoS Commission on Cancer has recommended national standards for cancer centers to meet that emphasize patient-centered care including distress screening and survivorship treatment plans (Standard 4.7). However, there are few roadmaps in which to guide Survivorship Program planning among underserved, ethnic minority men with prostate cancer (PC), a population at risk for medical nonadherence. We report findings of a psychosocial needs assessment among a convenience sample of 37 PC patients relative to 240 men with other cancers at a major cancer center in Bronx, NY. Method(s): Patients were approached in oncology waiting rooms or by telephone via referral to psychosocial services and administered a brief assessment that included the Distress Thermometer (DT), a QoL symptom checklist, and screening of preferences for various psychosocial interventions. Result(s): The sample was comprised of 47% African American, 31% Hispanic, 22% Caucasian, 19% Spanish-speaking, M age = 68+-10.43 (48-91) years, and M years since diagnosis = 3+-3.74 (.04-14.80). In comparison to men with other cancers, PC patients reported more sadness (44% vs 29%; p < 0.05), more problems with urination; 39% vs 19% (p < 0.02), and a trend towards more sexual dysfunction (41% vs 27%; p < 0.06). PC patients were marginally more likely to endorse clinical distress; 53% vs 37% (p < 0.06), and were nearly twice as likely to accept a referral to counseling, 48% vs 25% (p < 0.01). Other interventions of interest included: mind-body programs (87%), health education/fitness (70%), support groups (53%), complementary medicine (44%), creative arts (39%), and smoking cessation (12%). Up to 16% wanted a "Treatment Buddy" and 24% reported an interest in volunteering to help other cancer patients. Conclusion(s): This pilot needs assessment study suggests that underserved PC patients may be more emotionally vulnerable and receptive to psychosocial intervention than those with other cancers. Further this assessment highlights several types of interventions that should be considered in PC Survivorship treatment plans, including counseling, mind-body therapies and health and fitness promotion.
