AB  - Objectives: To investigate the durability and generalizability of Brain Injury Coping Skills (BICS) treatment group that was previously found to improve perceived self‐efficacy (PSE) in persons with brain injury (BI) and their caregivers (Backhaus et al, 2010). Design: Randomized controlled study with measurements at baseline, postintervention, and 3‐month follow‐up. Setting: Outpatient rehabilitation setting at a large tertiary care medical center in the upper‐Midwestern U.S. Participants: 8 survivors of BIs and 8 caregivers. Interventions: BICS is a 12‐session, manualized, cognitive‐behavioral treatment group that provides psychoeducation, support, and coping skills training with goals of increasing PSE and preventing emotional disorders. Interventions: Not applicable. Main Outcome Measures: Brief Symptom Inventory 18 (BSI‐18); Brain Injury Coping Skills Questionnaire (BICSQ). Results: Repeated measures analysis of variance showed a significant positive treatment effect for PSE on BICSQ (F=18.41, P=.001) but not on BSI‐18 indicators of emotional distress. Although not statistically significant (t=1.95, P=.07), PSE was lower for the BICS group (mean BICSQ=80.00) than for the control group (mean BICSQ=97.38) at baseline. The BICS group improved significantly on PSE (mean BICSQ=109.25) compared to the control group (mean BICSQ=92.88) immediately posttreatment (t=‐2.35, P=.044). Although PSE was similar for both groups at 3‐month follow‐up (BICS mean BICSQ=95.13; control mean BICSQ=98.5), PSE for the BICS group remained higher than at baseline. Both groups were generally within normal limits on BSI‐18 measures at each assessment period. Conclusions: Despite the small sample size, this study demonstrated a significant treatment effect for the BICS group in improving PSE, replicating previous findings for the BICS group treatment. This study was conducted in a separate setting from the original study using different facilitators, providing evidence of generalizability of the treatment effects and the usefulness of the BICS group as a durable treatment. Establishment of long‐term treatment effects awaits further research with larger samples.
