AB  - According to data from the most recent wave (2000) of the Health and Retirement Study, in a two-year period more than two million elderly Medicare beneficiaries did not adhere to drug treatment regimens because of cost. This poor adherence tended to be more common among beneficiaries with no or partial medication coverage and was associated with poorer health and higher rates of hospitalization. The risk for cost-related poor adherence was especially pronounced among lower-income beneficiaries with high out-of-pocket drug spending. This paper argues that this pattern of cost-related poor medication adherence should inform the design of Medicare prescription drug benefit legislation. Efficient benefit design should give priority to people who without such coverage would be at highest risk of delaying or forgoing the purchase of necessary medications. Thus, an efficient plan should include a sliding-scale copayment schedule, referenced to income and spending, that reduces copayment to zero for the lowest-income beneficiaries with high out-of-pocket spending.
