AB  - Purpose: Several change mechanisms accounting for AA-related benefit have been identified, including abstinence-based social support and gains in spiritual practices. This study investigated whether AA member age moderated the extent that AA meeting attendancemobilized spiritual practices and, in turn, if the age of AA members moderated the salutary effects of such spiritual gains on increased abstinence (PDA) and reductions in drinking intensity (DPDD). Method(s): Study participants (N = 210) were recruited for a single-group naturalistic study on AA processes (N = 253; R01-AA014197-01A1). Assessments for this secondary analysis included the CASAA demographic form, the Form 90 semi-structured interview, and the Religious Behaviors and Practices questionnaire. Participant age was dichotomized at 30 years of age to examine differences between younger and older participants. Result(s): Overall, follow-up rates were excellent, pre-post gains in PDA and reductions in DPDD were significant and a majority of the participants reported AA attendance throughout the 9-month follow-up interview. Age did not moderate the mediational effect of spiritual practice on the relationship between AA attendance and PDA (CI: -0.13 to 0.20) DPDD (CI: -6.56 to 3.38). More generally, gains in spiritual practice did notmediate the relationship between AA attendance and PDA (Under 30 CI: -3.82 to 5.98; Over 30 CI: -6.56 to 3.38) or DPDD (Under 30 CI: -3.82 to 5.98; Over 30 CI: -1.39 to 0.04) in the current sample. Discussion(s): Age did not moderate the mediational effect of spiritual practice on AA attendance and DPDD or PDA. However, consistent with previous research, AA attendance was positively associated with gains in spiritual practices and PDA as well as negatively associated with DPDD. Interestingly, an examination of individual paths showed that age may influence the extent to which AA attendance predicts increased engagement in spiritual practices. Specifically, this association appeared to be attenuated among older participants but not younger participants suggesting that certain aspects of AA meeting may foster increased engagement in spiritual practices among younger adults.
