AB  - Maltreated children suffer from serious short-term and long-term health and mental health consequences. With the accumulated knowledge of the impact of adverse childhood experiences on health, positive parenting is increasingly recognized as important preventive healthcare. Primary care has been identified as a potential home to disseminate evidence-informed positive parenting strategies at the population level. Trauma-informed integrated primary care provides a model for positive parenting integration into routine healthcare practice, is particularly valuable in rural communities with limited resources. The current study examined the process of rural primary care organizational change toward Trauma-Informed Integrated Primary Care associated with the integration of an evidence-informed behavioral parenting intervention, Behavior Checker, into routine care practice. The organizational change efforts were pursued through a community partnership comprised of a rural primary clinic, a non-profit organization, and an academic research and development center. Informed by the realist evaluation paradigm, this organizational case study was conducted to identify facilitating and interfering contexts and mechanisms in the organizational change efforts and thereby, contribute to building theories of integrated healthcare. Multiple methods of data collection were utilized including key informant interviews, focus groups, surveys, and direct observation. The data analysis process involved identifying initial program theories underlying Behavior Checker implementation strategies and refining them in the form of Context-Mechanism-Outcome configurations (CMOs) based on observational data. The core mechanisms found to facilitate organizational change efforts included 1) options to increase and maintain staff competence, 2) workflow efficiency that can contain complexity compression among frontline staff; 3) a systematic method of prompting intervention delivery and documentation; 4) capability for ongoing performance evaluation; 5) perception of fit, and 6) improved organizational capabilities through leveraging resources and sharing expertise. Additionally, multiple layers of contextual conditions at the organizational and community level that were antecedent to the organizational change initiatives influenced the change efforts. The results from the study informed the process of refining a theoretical framework of integrated healthcare, the Comprehensive Framework for Integrated Healthcare Development (CF-IHD). The introduction of Trauma-Informed Integrated Primary Care in rural communities presents a window of opportunity to advance efforts to systematically disseminate evidence-informed positive parenting strategies at the population level, particularly among the most underserved populations. Increased knowledge of the contexts and mechanisms that facilitate the process of rural primary care organizational change provides insights into efforts to build capacity for rural primary healthcare facilities, thereby maximizing the opportunity to promote child well-being and prevent maltreatment through the public dissemination of positive parenting strategies. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2020 APA, all rights reserved)
