AB  - Background: The transition from adolescence to adulthood is characterized by high rates of substance use and mental health disorders that are unsurpassed by any other age group. Transition-aged youth also face unique psychosocial stressors associated with the task of becoming an independent adult, which often exacerbate existing substance use and mental health problems. Access to integrated, developmentally appropriate services for Transition-Aged Youth (TAY; ages 16-25) with dual diagnoses remains limited in many healthcare jurisdictions. The TAY Service was established to address this gap in Eastern Ontario, Canada, a region with approximately 1.2 million urban and rural residents. Service model development was informed by a regional needs assessment, including a survey of 134 community programs (72% response rate), and focus groups with urban and rural community partners (n=22), and with youth and family members (n=8). The TAY Service provides long-term multidisciplinary services to youth with dualdiagnosis, who present with moderate to severe substance use and mental health symptoms that result in significant impairment in major life domains (eg, school and employment). The program is recovery-focused, with core services including medication management, individual and group therapy, and case management. Services are provided in a community outpatient setting, with access to inpatient bed as needed through an affiliated hospital-based program. Little is known about the clinical characteristics, engagement, and outcomes of TAY presenting to treatment with dual diagnoses. Those factors are examined in clients presenting to the TAY Service. Method(s): For ongoing program evaluation, clients (n = 113) completed self-report measures of demographics, substance use, and mental health upon intake and at multiple follow-up time points. Diagnostic and appointment information was collected via medical chart review. Pre-post differences in self-reported substance use and mental health were examined using paired sample t tests. Spearman's correlations and independent sample t tests were used to explore associations between ordinal and binary factors and appointment attendance. Tests were considered significant at P < 0.05. Result(s): Most clients were male (55%) and white (77%), with an average age of 19.9 years. Most clients were unemployed or on social assistance (53%) and had unstable housing (56%), and many presented with legal issues (19%). At intake, most clients reported substantial-severe drug use (72%) and hazardous/ harmful drinking (73%), and many reported intravenous (IV) drug use (19%). Over half had polysubstance-use disorders (59%). Past 30-day substance use was highest for cannabis (81%), alcohol (79%), and cocaine (37%), and over a quarter of clients reported opioid use (27%). Most clients also reported moderate to severe depression (89%) and anxiety (86%), as well as internalizing (99%) and externalizing symptoms (91%). The most common mental health diagnoses were anxiety disorders (50%), mood disorders (42%), and BPD/BPD traits (16%). Additionally, most clients presented with two or more mental health disorders (59%). Most clients were in the contemplation stage of change in terms of their substance use (76%) and mental health problems (80%). Over three-quarters of all scheduled appointments were attended (76%). Clients were significantly more likely to attend psychiatry appointments (80%) than psychology appointments (74%; P<0.05), while 78% of social work appointments were attended. Appointments scheduled for within 1 week of a previous appointment were more likely to be attended than those scheduled for after 1 week (P< 0.05). Clients were less likely to attend appointments if they were reported past 30-day cocaine use (P< 0.01), stimulant use (P<0.05), opioid use (P<0.05), lifetime IV drug use (P<0.05), or unstable housing (P< 0.05). Clients who ascribed a high degree of importance to their treatment goals were more likely to attend appointments. At 6- to 12-month follow-up, clients reported significant reductions in drug and alcohol use severity (Ps< 0.05), and in depression and anxiety (Ps<0.01). Client satisfaction was very positive. Conclusion(s): There is a high burden of substance use and mental health symptoms among TAY seeking treatment for dual diagnoses, complicated by significant psychosocial stressors. These results emphasize the importance of providing substance use and mental health services tailored to the unique needs of TAY. Treatment for TAY with concurrent substance use and mental health disorders should be integrated and address their unique psychosocial stressors, which is consistent with the TAY Service model. This model can be implemented in other healthcare jurisdictions. Summary: The transition from adolescence to adulthood is characterized by high rates of substance use and mental health disorders and unique psychosocial stressors associated with the task of becoming an independent adult. Access to integrated, developmentally appropriate services for TAY (ages 16-25) with dual diagnoses remains limited. The TAY Service was established to address this service gap in Eastern Ontario, Canada. Little is known about the clinical characteristics, engagement, and outcomes of TAY presenting to treatment with dual diagnoses. Those factors are examined in clients presenting to the TAY Service. Clients (n = 113) completed self-report measures of demographics, substance use and mental health upon intake and at multiple follow-up time points. Diagnostic and appointment information was collected via medical chart review. Most clients were unemployed or on social assistance (53%) and had unstable housing (56%), and many presented with legal issues (19%). Most clients reported substantial-severe drug use (72%) and hazardous/harmful drinking (73%), and many reported IV drug use (19%). Over half had polysubstance-use disorders (59%). Most clients also reported moderate to severe depression (89%) and anxiety (86%), and presented with two or more mental health disorders (59%). Clients were mostly in the contemplation stage of change with respect to their substance use (76%) and mental health problems (80%). Over three-quarters of all scheduled appointments were attended (76%). Attendance was associated with both client (Ps < 0.05) and appointment characteristics (Ps < 0.05). At 6- to 12-month follow-up, clients reported significant reductions in drug and alcohol use severity (Ps < 0.05), and in depression and anxiety (Ps < 0.01). There is a high burden of substance use and mental health symptoms among TAY seeking treatment for dual diagnoses, which are complicated by significant psychosocial stressors. Treatment for TAY with concurrent substance use and mental health disorders should be integrated and tailored to address their unique psychosocial stressors, which is consistent with the TAY Service model. This model can be implemented in other healthcare jurisdictions.
