A1  - Nelson T
A1  - Perrin PB
KW  - eppi-reviewer
PY  - 2020
DA  - 2020///
Y1  - 2020///
AB  - This study examines the relationships among English proficiency, Spanish proficiency, religious engagement. alcohol use, and drug use among Latinxs of Cuban, Mexican, Puerto Rican, and other Latinx origins. Results showed that increased English proficiency is associated with decreased alcohol and drug use as well as religious engagement for Latinxs of Mexican origin. English proficiency also predicted increased religious engagement for those of Cuban descent. There was a significant inverse relationship between religious engagement and alcohol use among Latinxs of Mexican origin, and religious engagement mediated the relationship between English proficiency and alcohol use for Latinxs of Mexican origin. Regarding Spanish proficiency, results indicated a significant and negative association between Spanish proficiency and alcohol use as well as a significant and negative association between Spanish proficiency and drug use for those of Mexican origin and other Latinx groups. Spanish proficiency was associated with increased religious engagement for Latinxs of Mexican and Puerto Rican background. Further, religious engagement mediated the relationship between Spanish proficiency and alcohol use for Latinxs of Mexican origin. Clinical implications for substance use treatments among the Latinx community are discussed.
