AB  - Background and Objective(s): Parent carers of disabled children are at increased risk of mental and physical health problems. The Healthy Parent Carers programme is a peer-led, groupbased intervention that encourages behaviours associated with health and wellbeing. The study aims to assess feasibility of delivering the programme in the community and the feasibility of evaluating effectiveness in a trial. Study Design: Feasibility parallel group randomised controlled trial. Study Participants and Settings: Parents of children with special educational needs and disabilities in UK. Material(s) and Method(s): Parents of children with special educational needs and disabilities are individually randomised, stratified by site, to take part in a group programme (intervention) or access online materials (control). Participants are assessed before randomisation (baseline), at 15 and 40 weeks after randomisation. Measures include mental health, wellbeing, health-related quality of life, health behaviours, patient activation, resilience, social connections, support, use of health care, social care and societal resources. Result(s): Six geographically diverse community-based sites were established successfully in collaboration with local parent carer forums, a county-wide adult learning service, children's hospice, and two special schools. Experienced lead facilitators were recruited through the Council for Disabled Children, and a local assistant facilitator for each group were recruited through local networks. Facilitators have been trained in delivering the intervention programme. Results being presented include recruitment, retention, and fidelity of intervention delivery. Conclusions or Significance: This study will assess whether programme delivery and evaluation are feasible, and will inform design of a definitive randomised controlled trial to test effectiveness and cost-effectiveness.
