AB  - Sought to improve understanding of the experiences of people with bipolar disorder (BD); to gain insight into the information received by patients and to explore whether there was a need for greater education of both patients and the general population; to gain a greater insight as to how medications and other treatments are perceived; and to disseminate the information in order to raise awareness of the burden of BD and other disorders. A detailed patient-based questionnaire, GAMIAN-Europe/BEAM survey, was completed by 1,632 patients (aged 18-83 yrs) in 11 European countries. 1,041 respondents had BD. The remainder suffered from depression, dysthymia or atypical depression. Findings indicate that BD patients wait for 5.7 years for a correct diagnosis from the 1st onset of symptoms, often have a family history of mood/anxiety disorders, and experience a high degree of stigmatization. Also, most patients receive combination therapy. Compliance problems resulting from adverse side-effects are less significant than in the past. Overall, the level of satisfaction with pharmacotherapy was high. Yet, patients had reservations about dependency issues and possible long-term side-effects. The need for more intervention education in patients and increased public awareness is concluded. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
