AB  - Three broad forms of mental health advocacy are discussed, designed to meet the needs of different groups of people & to operate in somewhat different ways, proposed by the Nottingham (England) Advocacy Group, which is committed to user empowerment & giving service users a voice. "Collective self-advocacy" offers meetings as a forum for users to comment on services received; "advocacy on behalf of service users" proposes a national network of advocates who represent individual patients; & "citizen advocacy" matches volunteer advocates with disabled users of the services. The essential role of effective user representation in the field of community care is discussed. 11 References. Adapted from the source document.
