AB  - A study to ascertain whether values stressed by members of middle class SS background differ from those stressed by lower class members. 16 closed AA meetings at Manteno State Hospital, Manteno, Ill., were attended and the speeches delivered recorded verbatim. SS evaluations of speakers were based on occupation, source of income, education, religion, and ethnicity. Thus 20 middle class and 21 lower class speakers were identified. Explicit value statements were noted by the R. K. White procedure. No significant difference in value emphasis was found. The patient group was divided into Middle and Lower SS groups and asked to select their preferred speakers. Those commonly selected by both groups were compared to those speakers not selected as preferred. The overall findings are: (1) Middle and Lower class leaders and speakers were equally represented at AA meetings; (2) 13 values constituted a 'group therapeutic program of character re-education'; (3) Middle and Lower class patients selected a common group of speakers as preferred consisting of Lower (N=8) and Middle (N=7) class leaders. Preferred speakers stressed attainment of common group objectives, non-preferred concentrated on past personal experience (p=.03). (4) AA group life is an effective agent for bridging social class differences in the collective activity of the problem-centered group. L. P. Chall.
