AB  - Background: Health worker (HW) stigma towards those who use tobacco, abuse alcohol, or have other substance use disorders (SUDs) is a service barrier associated with lower use of effective interventions, lesser identification and treatment of SUD complications and co-morbidities, and less HW advocacy for services and policies for these groups. This study sought to determine the impact of online training on HWs' stigma against those with SUDs in Kenya; it is nested in a research program called the computer-based Drug and Alcohol Training Assessment in Kenya (eDATA K). Through NextGenU.org, the Africa Mental Health Foundation, and the Annenberg Physician Training Program in Addiction Medicine, eDATA K offered two SUD clinical courses (on screening and on interventions in primary care), both including modules on stigma, non-judgmental communication, and health determinants related to SUDs. Method(s): The Opening Mind Scale for HWs (OMS), a validated mental health stigma tool, was adapted for SUD and administered to HWs interested in taking the courses in 15 primary care outpatient facilities participating in eDATA K (11 public and 3 private, in three counties). We compared pre vs. post scores (with a four month gap), for course completers and non-completers through paired t-tests, and conducted focus group discussions. Result(s): Of the 123 HWs who enrolled in the course, 82.1% (n = 101) completed both pre and post OMS surveys, with 57 survey completers (56.4%) who finished the training during the study period. For the three assessed categories of substances (alcohol, tobacco, and other psychoactive substances), course completers experienced a statistically significant (p < 0.05) decrease in mean stigma scores post-vs. pre-training, with no statistically significant difference between pre-and post-scores for course non-completers. Qualitatively, HWs who took the courses reported meaningful course impacts on their understanding of people with SUDs and their attitudes toward them. Workers in one large facility described being victim of stigma-by-proxy (stigma against those working with patients with stigmatizing conditions) from other HWs in departments not involved in the study. HWs asked that we intervene to teach all workers about SUDs, done through organizing an in-person Continuing Medical Education session open to all (but without the known presence of those with SUDs), with HWs involved in eDATA K reporting less stigma-by-proxy after that session. Conclusion(s): eDATA K online clinical courses were effective at reducing stigma toward those with tobacco, alcohol and other SUDs. The decrease in stigma was as large as with gold-standard stigma interventions done with in-person meetings with stigmatized patients. Additionally, experiences of stigma-by-proxy were successfully addressed, decreasing barriers to providing SUD services in Kenya.
