AB  - TYPE: Abstract Publication TOPIC: Tobacco Cessation and Prevention PURPOSE: Smoking is a chronic disease, despite the existing therapeutic resources,relapse is still high.The aim of this study was to evaluate the immediate treatment and a 1 year follow-up results for smokers' monitored at a University service. METHOD(S): 290 patients entered the Smoking Treatment Group Program of a University Hospital from January 2011 to December 2017.The study included patients who participated in at least four of the six cognitive behavioral therapy sessions. Sociodemographic characteristics, current and previous medical history, smoking history were analyzed. The Fagerstrom's test score characterized nicotine dependence degree. Patients who stopped smoking after 6 sessions were considered as immediate success. Long-term success was defined as abstinence for a period of one year. RESULT(S): Of the 290 patients studied, 194(67%) were female, mean age of 58 +/-5 years. Most patients (67.1% ) had a high or very high degree of nicotine dependence. The mean number of cigaretts smoked was 20.0 +/-5.0 cigaretts/day and the mean duration was 42.0 +/- 5.0 years. Of the 290 patients, 197 were evaluated for treatment success. At the end of the six sessions, 160 patients had ceased smoking (immediate abstinence rate of 81.21%). Long-term telephone contact follow-up was achieved with 148 patients, of whom 78 remained abstinent (long-term abstinence rate of 52.7%). CONCLUSION(S): the high immediate and long-term abstinence rates found in this study are possibly related to the greater knowledge of the patients' profile and to the multidisciplinary team's actions. CLINICAL IMPLICATIONS: Smoking treatment Services should offer long-term support, especially for patients with a high degree of dependence. DISCLOSURE: No significant relationships. KEYWORDS: Smoking, treatment, addictionCopyright © 2020 American College of Chest Physicians
