AB  - Background: Worldwide, mental disorders are a leading cause of disability. Workforce shortages pose major obstacles to delivering adequate mental health care and scaling up services to address the global treatment gap. There is mounting evidence demonstrating the clinical effectiveness of having community health workers deliver brief psychosocial treatments, such as the Healthy Activity Program, for common mental disorders in primary care settings. With rapidly increasing access to and use of digital technology, there may be new opportunities to leverage these emerging digital technologies to support community health workers and increase mental health workforce capacity. Objective(s): In this preliminary study, our goal was to explore the acceptability and feasibility of using digital technology for training community health workers to deliver evidence-based psychosocial treatments for common mental disorders in primary care settings in rural India. Method(s): From February to July 2018, we employed an iterative design process involving two sequential evaluations of a prototype of a digital training intervention. This involved obtaining qualitative feedback from community health workers through in-depth semistructured focus group interviews to inform modifications to the intervention content and design. We examined community health worker perspectives related to mental health care and the potential value of using digital technology to support these efforts. Qualitative data were examined using a directed content analysis approach focused on predetermined categories related to feasibility, acceptability, and utility. Result(s): In total, we recruited 32 community health workers across both phases of the study. All participants were female, between the ages of 28 and 40, and had a minimum of 8th grade education and between 1 to 13 years of work experience. Our content analysis of the qualitative data generated the following five overarching themes: (i) Importance and utility of depression training; (ii) Significance of the digital training program; (iii) Digital literacy and challenges; (iv) Content and language of the training program; and (v) Engagement in the training program content. Discussion(s): Our findings highlight the potential that digital technology holds for supporting community health workers in delivering evidence-based mental health care in low-resource settings in rural India. Importantly, our study demonstrates that not only is there interest in using technology for task sharing among community health workers, but that this workforce agrees that this is an urgent need within the communities they serve. Furthermore, the community health workers in this study highlighted several challenges to using digital technology to support the scale up of evidence-based mental health services. These valuable perspectives obtained from community health workers will be essential for guiding future efforts. These findings should be interpreted cautiously given the exploratory nature of this study. Implications for Future Research: This study will inform the development and evaluation of a digital training intervention for community health workers in rural India. Our findings highlight future opportunities to use digital technology for supporting the development of a trained, effective, and sustainable mental health workforce, and the potential to leverage novel technologies for integrating mental health care into existing health systems in lowresource settings.
