AB  - To conduct a randomized controlled trial comparing the efficacy of Meaning‐Centered Group Psychotherapy (MCGP) versus a standardized Supportive Group Psychotherapy (SGP) in reducing psychological distress (depression and anxiety), end‐of‐life despair (hopelessness, desire for hastened death, and suicidal ideation), and improving spiritual well‐being and overall quality of life in a sample patients with advanced cancer. To assess the relative impact of Meaning‐Centered Group Psychotherapy on different aspects of spiritual well‐being (e.g., a sense of meaning and purpose versus spirituality linked to religious faith). To examine clinical and demographic variables that may correspond to differential responses to Meaning‐Centered Group Psychotherapy (e.g., potential mediating and moderating influences such as illness severity, religion and religiosity, level of education, race/ethnicity, level of pre‐intervention social support, presence of pain and physical symptom burden).
