T1  - Awareness Development and Usage of mHealth Technology Among Hypertensive Patients in a Rural Community of Bangladesh
JF  - https://clinicaltrials.gov/show/NCT03614104
A1  - NCT03614104
KW  - eppi-reviewer
PY  - 2018
DA  - 2018///
Y1  - 2018///
AB  - Introduction: Hypertension is one of the foremost non‐communicable diseases (NCDs) in the world, which significantly contributes to the burden of cardiovascular diseases (CVDs), stroke, kidney failure, disability, and premature death. It is also recognized as a global public health problem and ranked third as a cause of disability‐adjusted life‐years (DALYs). According to the World Health Organization (WHO), about 17 million deaths occur worldwide due to CVDs, of which hypertension alone accounts for 9.4 million deaths and 80% of the CVD‐related deaths occurred in the developing countries. The global prevalence of hypertension is projected to increase from 26% in 2000 to 29% by 2025, which will be approximately 29% of the world's population. Its prevalence is increasing in the low and middle‐income countries (LMIC), especially Southeast Asia where individuals are having an increasing burden of hypertension including CVDs. According to WHO, hypertension has become a significant health concern in the Asian region, affecting more than 35% of the adult population. Bangladesh, a developing country in South Asia, has been experiencing an epidemiologic transition from communicable diseases to NCDs. In recent years, rapid urbanization, prolonged life expectancy, unhealthy diet, and lifestyle changes have led to an increase in the rate of CVD including hypertension in Bangladesh. Even though, many still think hypertension to be a disease of the elderly individuals, but now‐a‐days, the individuals with age above 25 years are also being affected by hypertension. In India, one in 4 individuals aged above 25 years has hypertension and it is increasing at an alarming rate. The prevalence of hypertension was first reported as 1.10 % in 1976 in Bangladesh. A systematic review and meta‐analysis of the prevalence of hypertension in the country among 6,430 adults for the period 1995 to 2009 was estimated to be 13.5% with a 95% confidence interval (CI) ranging from 12.7‐14.2%. In recent years, in Bangladesh, approximately 20% of adults and 40‐65% of elderly people suffer from HTN. Most of the common reasons are sedentary life style, changing food habit, smoking, and adulteration of food products, fruits, and vegetables. However, keeping in mind the changes in lifestyle and food habits among the young adults in Bangladesh, the possibilities of hypertension in young adults can't be ruled out among this age group. In this study, we aim to develop awareness by health education and usage of mHealth technology among hypertensive patients in a rural community of Bangladesh. Objectives: General objective: 1. To develop awareness, enhance knowledge and make behavioral changes by using health education and mHealth technology among hypertensive patients in a rural community of Bangladesh. Specific objectives: 2. To evaluate blood pressure change, the occurrence of the related complications, and quality of life (QOL) by intervention. 3. To identify the current perceptions, attitudes, and practices related to Hypertension. 4. To determine the socio‐demographic factors those are associated with the awareness of hypertension. 5. To assess the health care seeking behavior, life style pattern, drug and dietary use related to hypertension. 6. To evaluate the feasibility of this intervention strategies and mHealth technology. Hypothesis: Here investigator hypothesize that hypertensive patients because of mHealth technology applications (cell phone monitoring of BP and behavior change education ‐ drug adherence) and follow‐up will have decreased blood pressure (BP) relative to controls under medication but without any mHealth technology applications. Compared to controls, intervention patients at follow‐up will report quality of life, behavioral change, dietary habit as well as their greater satisfaction with care. Methodology: Study Design: Randomized Controlled Trial for 12 months. Study Place: Kumudini Women's Medical College and Hospital, Mirzapur, Bangladesh. Target Population: The target population is 155 in each group. Sample size calculation: With a 95% probability and a power of 90%, 155 in each group would comprise the sample size with an expected 15% increase in the proportion of hypertensive drug adherence, monitoring, and reporting, and 15% decrease in associated health problems. Investigator will conduct a randomized controlled effectiveness trial that will compare [i] standard‐of‐care (n=155) with [ii] standard‐of‐care [n=155] plus a mobile‐based SMS intervention for consecutive 12 months. Significance level will be 0.05, Equivalence limit will be 0.10, Allocation ratio will be 1, and Margin of error will be 7.86. Enrollment of participants: The study will be implemented at Kumudini Women's Medical College and Hospital immediately after getting the IRB approval from Bangladesh Medical and Research Council (BMRC). Hypertensive patients living in rural Mirzapur will be mostly identified from Mirzapur Kumudini Hospital. Additionally, study participants will be selected from the community by a door to door visits. Diagnosis of hypertension will be based on the prescription and Kumudini hospital database records. Investigator limits data collection to this single large health facility where the hypertensive participants from surrounding rural community seek care. Consecutive patients from this facility or community who will be meeting the eligibility criteria will be asked to participate. After giving written informed consent, participants will be randomly allocated to the intervention or usual care control group based on a computer‐generated series of numbers. At baseline, all patients will receive written information about hypertension and its management produced in the native language. Study Activities and Data Collection Procedure: Investigator will do a face to face interview to develop the awareness among hypertensive people to provide improved health care services. The face to face interview will take place at Kumudini Hospital or in a rural community around the facility. Through the face to face interview, investigator will understand the individuals' perceptions, attitude, and practices associated with compliance to treatment and behavior change. Additionally, the study will identify major barriers related to hypertension associated health care seeking behaviours. Investigator expected that face to face interview would produce very precise and specific answers as well as share an exhaustive and varied knowledge about individual's experience, perceptions, opinions, and motive related to health care seeking for hypertension. Subjects are diagnosed cases of hypertension. The selection criteria would be: individuals aged 35 years and more, have 1‐5 years of schooling, who are open and can exchange their views freely as well as who can be asked in‐depth and probing questions, who can share information about the nature and extent of hypertension and related problems and suggest measures for solutions. Immediately before the implementation of the research, a structured questionnaire will be developed based on past experience in rural Bangladesh and elsewhere, which will be discussed thoroughly with field staff members and extensive field‐testing will be done for necessary modifications of the questionnaire. Using a standardized pre‐tested questionnaire, the socio‐economic status and demography, health status, health care seeking behavior, food consumption practices, life styles such as smoking, level of physical activity and other relevant information will be obtained. Moreover, blood and urine specimens will be collected and laboratory tests like serum total cholesterol, random blood sugar, urinary salinity, and urinary protein will be performed. Measurements of nutritional indices such as height in meters, weight in kg, MUAC in millimetre, hip and waist ratio will be carried out from the enrollment day till the end of follow up. Follow up and physical examination will take place at Kumudini Hospital or in the nearest Health and Family Welfare Center. In this study, investigator will use a portable health clinic device which is recommended by Grameen health program and by using this device investigator can save time, as this device has all the test and measurement facilities which will do in this study. Six follow up visits will be undertaken during the study period. Initially follow up visits will be twice a month then once a month up to 4 consecutive months. Moreover, staff members will be trained on use of all hardware. Usability evaluation of the staff members will be performed to document (a) user performance and understand common errors that are made by them while using the devices, (b) understand user perceptions and their suggestions in better usage of the devices. Similar usability evaluation of the 10‐15 hypertensive individuals will be conducted. The evaluation will be conducted in a quiet environment with no distractions. Five qualitative questions will be asked to understand the practical benefits and drawbacks of the devices. SMS messages will be developed in native language by the members of the research team representing different professional background; these messages will be sent to 10‐15 people (randomly) for their comprehension and acceptability as part of field‐testing. The wired intervention individuals will receive short messaging service (SMS) and they will have the liberty to participate additionally in a two‐way communication system (between wired individuals and research team members). After receiving feedback, messages will be finalized. The SMS will provide simple health education and BP measurement reminders to encourage adherence to medical advice. Research team members will send a text message to a wired person having registered cell phone number throughout the study period. All the enrolled patients will receive health education booklets created by the researchers as well as a self‐management notebook will provide to participants in which participant will record blood pressure, body weight and life style improvement goals. The education booklets will be selected for that it will suitable for the individual participant. Any abnormalities in the monitored values or data or exacerbation of participant symptoms will be reported immediately to the primary care physician. Data management and analysis: Data will be entered twice and stored in a relational database. A form, with point‐of‐entry data checks and validation programs, will be prepared to check the consistency of data entry to form a working dataset. The data will be securely stored in a locked room. Data will be entered, processed and analyzed by using the statistical software packages SPSS for Windows version 21.0 and Epi Info version 6.0. Statistical analyses will include descriptive as well as analytical methods. The descriptive statistics will be expressed as means, medians, cross tabulation, standard deviation with range or percentages with 95% confidence intervals where appropriate. For analytic statistics: when the variable of interest is a categorical one, the significance of differences will be evaluated by Chi‐square test. When the main outcome measures are continuous variables, the statistical significance of group mean comparisons will be determined by Student's t‐test. In case of non‐normal distribution of measurements, equivalent non‐parametric tests will be used. Statistical significance will be defined as p<0.05. Outcome Variables: Primary outcomes: 1. Increase in awareness by health education and evaluated by Likert scale. 2. Increase in knowledge and attitude by health education (evaluated by the researchers who developed a questionnaire and evaluation tools). 3. Modify behavioral changes by health education (evaluated by the researcher who developed a questionnaire). Secondary outcomes: 1. To decrease the level of blood pressure (SBP <140 mm of Hg, DBP < 90 mm of Hg) 2. To measure health status by Euro‐5D QALY (Mobility, self‐care, usual activities, pain/discomfort, anxiety/depression) 3. To decrease dietary salt intake (Measured by urinary salinity test) 4. To measure blood glucose level (Random Blood Sugar) 5. To measure Blood Lipid Profile (S. Cholesterol) 6. Urinary Protein test 7. Check the feasibility of smartphone in hypertensive individuals by field testing questionnaires which will be developed by the researchers.
