AB  - Background: A prerequisite for sound healthcare of patients with mental illnesses is close and efficient collaboration between all sectors. However, cooperation between the different sectors in the German healthcare system does not run frictionless. In addition, the financing of the psychiatric health care system is splintered. Since 2012, psychiatric hospitals have been offered the opportunity to take care of mentally ill patients through model projects that are multi-professional, work across treatment periods and sectors and implement new forms of financing. A common characteristic of all model projects is the establishment of a common budget from inpatient hospital budget and the revenues of the Psychiatric Institutional Outpatients' Departments (PIA). The hospital can opt for their own treatment form and adapt their treatment to patients' needs. Aims of the Study: Effectivity, costs and cost-effectiveness of described model projects are evaluated and compared to routine care. Method(s): A prospective and retrospective secondary data-based non-randomized parallel group study design is used. Data from 97 statutory health insurance companies on inpatient and outpatient healthcare utilization, on therapeutic products and drugs and on sick leave will be analyzed. All patients who were insured by any of the participating health insurance companies and who were treated in any of the model hospitals due to any of 16 defined mental illnesses within the evaluation period will be compared with patients in routine care. Comparable hospitals were identified through a new algorithm based on a systematic analysis of structured hospital quality reports and regional aggregated data from governmental institutions. Result(s): Costs and effectiveness between model and routine care will be investigated using cost-effectiveness analyses. Effects will be analyzed as frequency and duration of inpatient care, frequency and intensity of outpatient care, cross-sectoral continuity, discontinuation of contacts to psychiatric health care, physician and hospital hopping, sick leave, re-admission rate, comorbidity, mortality, disease progression and guideline adherence. Costs will be considered from the perspective of health insurance companies through direct inpatient psychiatric healthcare costs, direct psychiatric healthcare costs, costs of outpatient care and transparency of healthcare services. Sixteen model hospitals are included in this evaluation so far. For each model hospital, up to ten control hospitals were selected from which control patients for matching will be selected. The evaluation period encompasses the first four years of each model hospital and one further year of follow-up together with two additional patient-individual years before first treatment in either model or control hospital. Altogether, the scientific evaluation will continue for eleven years including several reports for each model hospital and joint analyses including a meta-analysis over all model projects. Discussion and Implication for Health Policies: Evaluations of complex interventions in healthcare is one of the main tasks of health services research and is the basis for an evidence-based development of the healthcare system. The treatment needs to be adapted flexibly to the needs of the patient. If the model projects in psychiatric care will be estimated to be efficient and cost-effective compared to routine care, this evaluation will provide arguments for a new structuring of routine care for patients with mental illnesses in Germany.
