PY  - 2008
DA  - 2008/02//
Y1  - 2008/02//
AB  - BACKGROUND The current treatments widely recommended by general practitioners for subacute low back pain, advice and exercise, have not been proven effective. OBJECTIVE To investigate the efficacy of exercise, advice, or both, for treatment of subacute low back pain. DESIGN AND INTERVENTION This randomized, placebo-controlled trial was conducted between January 2001 and June 2003 at seven physiotherapy clinics in Australia and New Zealand, and enrolled individuals aged 18-81 years with nonspecific back pain that had lasted for 6-12 weeks. Patients were randomly assigned to receive 6 weeks of one of following treatments: exercise and advice, exercise and sham advice, sham exercise and advice, or sham exercise and sham advice. The exercise and sham exercise treatments were delivered by a physiotherapist in 12 sessions and comprised the design and execution of an individualized, progressive, submaximal home exercise program, and 5 minutes of sham pulsed ultrasonography and 20 minutes of sham pulsed short-wave therapy. Advice and sham advice were provided in three physiotherapist-directed sessions that were designed to either encourage progressive return to normal activities or to reassure, but provide no advice on low back pain. Patients were assessed for pain, global perceived effect, and functional ability at baseline and at 6 weeks, 3 months and 12 months. Disability and depression were also assessed using the Roland-Morris Disability questionnaire and the 21-item Depression Anxiety Stress Scales, respectively. OUTCOME MEASURES The primary outcome measures were pain, global perceived effect and functional ability at 6 weeks and 12 months. RESULTS A total of 259 of the 693 individuals screened participated in this study; approximately 60 patients were in each treatment group and ≥89% attended each of the three follow-up sessions. Exercise and advice both had a significant effect on pain score at 6 weeks (relative change -0.8 and -0.7; P=0.004 and P=0.011, respectively), but these effects were not significant at 3 months and 12 months. Exercise had a significantly greater perceived global effect than the sham treatment at both 6 weeks and 3 months (P~0.017 and P~0.030, respectively), but did not have a significant effect at 12 months, while advice had a more significant perceived global effect than exercise at 6 weeks (P< 0.001) but no significant effect at 3 months and 12 months. Exercise did not have a significant effect on function at any point, whereas advice had an effect at 6 weeks (relative change 0.7, P=0.014) and 12 months (relative change 0.6, P=0.023). The combined effect of exercise and advice on function was statistically significant at all time points (P≤0.05 for all), but was only significant at 6 weeks and 3 months for pain and global perceived effect. Exercise, advice, or exercise and advice combined had no effect on disability or depression scores. CONCLUSION Treatment of subacute low back pain with physiotherapist-guided exercise and advice is better than either therapy alone, and provides short-term improvement in function and pain. [ABSTRACT FROM AUTHOR] Copyright of Nature Clinical Practice Rheumatology is the property of Springer Nature and its content may not be copied or emailed to multiple sites or posted to a listserv without the copyright holder's express written permission. However, users may print, download, or email articles for individual use. This abstract may be abridged. No warranty is given about the accuracy of the copy. Users should refer to the original published version of the material for the full abstract. (Copyright applies to all Abstracts.)
