AB  - Depression and Chronic Diseases: Primary Co-morbid Associations: Depressive symptoms and major depression frequently co-exist in the same patients who have major chronic diseases such as stroke, COPD, hip fractures, etc. The significance of this comorbid association remains poorly understood and under-appreciated. Is depression a predisposing risk factor for chronic medical diseases? Conversely, do underlying biological and/or psychological processes in chronic medical diseases precipitate or perpetuate depressive symptoms and depression? Or do they give rise to both psychiatric and physical illnesses at the same time? A clear understanding of these relationships is important for both preventive and curative treatment, since co-morbid depression may significantly impact medical and behavioural outcomes of care such as functional disability, quality of life, mortality, hospitalization, medication and lifestyle adherence. A number of studies conducted on elderly persons in the Singapore Longitudinal Ageing Studies explored the primary relationships between depression or depressive symptoms and chronic medical conditions. In cross-sectional studies, depressive symptoms assessed by Geriatric Depression Scale (GDS) increased with the number of chronic illnesses and ADL disability. In particular, they were much more frequently found in elderly with asthma or COPD (both by self-report and spirometry), gastro-intestinal problems, arthritis and heart failure, and were not explained by psychosocial, behavioural and physical functional risk factors. On the other hand, although depressive symptoms were particularly found to be more frequent in stroke patients, the association was almost entirely explained by accompanying physical functional disability. Post-stroke (6 month) depression was also explained by concurrent ADL disability, but also by stroke recurrence. Interestingly, low blood pressure (hypotension) was found to be significantly associated with depressive symptoms, both in elderly with and without anti-hypertensive treatment. As expected, the use of multiple and inappropriate drugs, as well as systemic corticosteroids and H2 blockers were associated with increased likelihood of depressive symptoms, but interestingly, statins use was associated with a decreased likelihood of depressive symptoms. The comorbid association of depressive symptoms with major chronic illnesses is complex. It is large parts explained by accompanying poor psychosocial, health and functional status, but a direct psychobiological basis plausibly involving chronic inflammatory mediators is likely, and should be investigated in-depth. The Effect of Early-Life Psychological Stress on Bronchial Asthma: Early-life psychological stress has long been hypothesized to be closely related to the development and exacerbation of bronchial asthma. An injurious effect on asthma of stressful experiences during early life to a variety of psychological stressors such as maternal separation, caregiver stress, negative life events has been observed. And previous studies have suggested that this effect is mediated through several systems involving the autonomic nervous system, hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis, immune system, oxidative stress pathways and modified genetic expression. But these results mainly came from retrospective investigation of hospitalized patients which avoided the inherent difficulty of long-term observation and there is much controversy concerning the underlying pathophysiologic mechanism. Recent pilot studies have employed animal modeds to provide experimental evidence of psychological stress' long-lasting impact on asthma. We used ovalbumin (OVA) for sensitization and airway challenge and restraint as stressor to establish asthma and psychological stress models and explored the effects on immune function and HPA axis responses to adult asthmatic rats who experienced earlylife or adulthood stress. Our results suggested that both childhood and adulthood stress resulted in exacerbation of asthma by modulating immune functions, including airway inflammation and cytokine levels. But different HPA axis responsiveness was induced. HPA axis hyperactivity was involved in adulthood psychological stress' impact, while long-lasting suppressive effect was found in early-life stress. The time of stress exposure significantly affects the mechanism of the effect of stress on asthma. Future research may address the nature and the extent to which early-life psychological stress predisposes the whole lifespan as well as the potential mechanism. In addition, understanding the interactive effects of early-life stress and other factors such as age is also of significance to identify the full health impact of stress and to develop effective interventions. Impact of depression on chronic disease outcomes: research findings and implications for care of elderly patients: Depressive symptoms and major depression occur frequently in elderly patients with chronic diseases such as stroke, COPD, hip fractures, etc. Recent clinical studies in elderly patientswith various chronic medical conditions have shown that depression may significantly impactmedical and behavioural outcomes of care such as functional disability, quality of life, mortality, hospitalization, medication and lifestyle adherence. These associations are demonstrated by clinical research findings of elderly populations of patients with COPD, stroke, hip fractures, and chronic kidney disease in Singapore. The presence of depressive symptoms and depression (with dementia) were associated with more all-causes hospitalizations. In patients hospitalized for COPD, we demonstrated that comorbid depressive symptoms were associated with poorer survival, longer hospitalization stay, increased symptom burden, poorer physical and social functioning, and poor smoking quit rates. Negative impacts of depressive symptoms on survival and quality of life were also recently observed in elderly persons with Chronic Kidney Disease (CKD). In hip fracture patients who underwent post surgical rehabilitation, co-morbid depressive symptoms independently of cognitive impairment predicted functional disability and reduced quality of life. In stroke patients, depressive symptoms at the beginning of rehabilitation were observed to predict 6month post-stroke cognitive impairment and physical functional recovery. Depressive symptoms andmajor depression are often under-recognized and under-treated in elderly patients with chronic medical diseases. These findings highlight an important role for depression screening and treatment in elderly patients with chronic illnesses. Depressive symptoms in Chinese older persons in Hong Kong: Relationship to chronic physical illnesses, level of functioning, social support and subjective health-related quality of life: Aim: This study aimed to explore the relationship among depressed symptoms, number of chronic physical illnesses, level of functional independence, instrumental activities of daily living, social support and subjective health-related quality of life in Hong Kong Chinese older persons. Method(s): This was a community based study. A convenience sample of 577 older people was recruited from community activity centres in the Territory. Data were collected by face-to-face structured interview. Measures included Physical Health Problems Checklist, Geriatric Depression Scale, Modified Barthel Index, Instrumental Activities of Daily Living scale, Social Support Questionnaire and the Hong Kong Chinese World Health Organization Quality of Life Scale-Brief Version. Conclusion(s): There were 139 (24.1%) male and 438 (75.9%) female participants. The results showed that about 9.8% (n = 56) of participants had depressive symptoms. There was no significant difference between the gender groups in the scores of depressive symptoms. The most common chronic physical illnesses were joint pain, hypertension, diabetes and heart diseases. Depressive symptoms were positively correlated with the persons' number of chronic physical illnesses and negatively correlated with their level of functional independence, instrumental activities of daily living, number and satisfaction with social support and subjective health-related quality of life. This study provides insights for healthcare professionals to plan innovative and co-ordinated services that meet the various health care needs of older people in the community.
