AB  - Background. Depression is an emerging public health crisis, affecting approximately 50% of American older adults in institutionalized settings and costing $43 billion each year (AAGP, 2002). Depressive symptoms in older adults often coexist with aging, dementia, physical limitations, and medical comorbidities, which are psychologically challenging in regards to differential diagnoses and treatments. However, it is hypothesized that depressive symptoms can be managed by psychotherapy and exercise, especially when both methods are applied simultaneously. Purpose. The purpose of this study was to investigate whether the simultaneous technique of exercise and psychotherapy (STEP) affects the level of depression in acute inpatient psychiatric older adults with depressive symptoms. Method. The study employed a true experimental design with 78 adult patients 50 years or older, who were randomly assigned to either the comparison group, which received standard treatment including medications and group therapy, the experimental Group 1 (Walk and Talk), which received simultaneous technique of exercise and psychotherapy, or the experimental Group 2 (Talk), which received individual psychotherapy, in addition to the standard treatment. Because this was an inpatient unit, length of treatment depended on patients' length of hospital stay, which was approximately 2 weeks. The study utilized well established questionnaires, including the Behavioral and Symptom Identification Scale (BASIS-32) and the long version of the Geriatric Depression Scale (GDS) to assess patients' pre and post depressive symptoms. Pre assessments were conducted at the time of admission and post assessments were conducted when patients were discharged. Individual psychotherapy was conducted in a 30-minute private session within the senior unit at the inpatient facility. Simultaneous technique of exercise (facility-based walking) and psychotherapy and were implemented as a dynamic method of intervention, which involved an interactive 30-minute walk and talk between the clinical therapist and each individual patient. A one-way ANCOVA statistical method via SPSS was applied to determine if there were differences across the three study groups controlling for baseline values, followed by post-hoc tests to determine where the differences were between groups. Results. At posttest, the difference in GDS scores between Groups 1 (M = 4.24, SE = 0.62) and 2 (M = 11.34, SE = 0.62) with the Walk and Talk treatment was more superior to the Talk alone (p < .001). The mean in GDS scores between Group 2 (M = 11.34, SE = 0.62) and Group 3 (M = 14.84, SE = 0.62) was also significant (p < .001), with individual Talk psychotherapy superior to the control group. In addition, the difference in GDS scores between Group 1 (M = 4.24, SE = 0.62) and Group 3 (M = 14.84, SE = 0.62) was also statistically significant (p < .001). The results from the BASIS-32 total and subscales were similar to the GDS. Overall, these results indicate that patients' depression scores improve significantly when receiving STEP treatment compared to individual psychotherapy alone and especially compared to the standard care control. Conclusion. This study suggests that a short term exercise program consisting of 30 minutes of walking in conjunction with simultaneous individual psychotherapy is an effective intervention for prevention and alleviation of depressive symptoms among inpatients. However, future research is needed to investigate whether the effect persists beyond the limited 2-week intervention and study period. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
