AB  - LEARNING OBJECTIVE 1: Recognize learning disability (LD) as a cause of nonadherence in the elderly LEARNING OBJECTIVE 2: Acknowledge the role of neuropsychiatric testing in directing interventions to improve risk factor management in patients with LDs CASE: A 65 y/o male with a history of poorly controlled DM Type II (HBA1C 12.3%), diffuse CAD, HTN and hyperlipidemia, and a long history of nonadherence leading to 3-vessel CABG, PVD with severe claudication, and persistently elevated BP and blood glucose with CKD-stage 3 and peripheral neuropathy had undergone multiple outpatient interventions to improve his compliance including: close primary care follow-up, ongoing psychiatric visits, and group therapy. Concerned about continued poor risk factor management, his frustrated providers enrolled him in theVA Home-Based Primary Care program (HBPC) for in-home assistance with medication management and lifestyle modification. Extensive efforts by the HBPC interdisciplinary team were temporarily effective but resulted in little longterm adherence. Despite sincere motivation and demonstrable knowledge, he failed to follow through with the plan of care; his response was often to become angry and have self-defeating thoughts. The pt recounted a childhood history of poor school performance described as getting "average grades," until he "started failing" in the 9th grade and dropped out of high school. He enjoyed learning but "wasn't good at it" especially reading. He cannot "hold onto" what is read; he must see things in order to process it. As a young adult he developed a rage disorder and diagnosed with impulse control disorder resulting in a felony conviction for domestic violence, subsequent divorce, inability to hold a job due to threatening co-workers, and alienation from his family. Medications and counseling were marginally effective. Concerned over his lifelong learning impairment and the possibility that his "rage" was a response to frustration with processing information, he was referred for neuropsychiatric assessment which revealed a verbal learning disorder: he scored high average to superior in knowledge of word definitions, verbal intelligence and visual abstraction; average in performance-based intelligence, verbal abstraction, arithmetic skills; mild to moderately impaired in spelling, novel problem solving, verbal learning; and severely impaired on short and long cued recall of word lists. Based on these findings the team implemented the following interventions: Non-verbal and non-telephonic instructions with use of signs and placing medications where they are most visually accessible in open shelves; medications were simplified with clonidine patch applied by weekly nurse visit; once daily administration of insulin; pt-created log of glucose and BP readings; and specific meal menus and shopping list rather than general dietary guidelines. DISCUSSION: This case illustrates the grave consequences of nonadherence related to undiagnosed LD in an elderly patient with serious medical conditions. While the LD certainly contributed to his nonadherence, it likely also led to his rage disorder and a lifetime of adverse consequences from it. LDs are not often first diagnosed in late life; there is a dearth of information regarding newly diagnosed LD in the elderly but are not likely to be directly reversible at this late age. Neuropsychiatric testing is helpful in directing the team to the most likely in-home interventions to improve the likelihood of meeting risk factor management goals. LDs should be considered in the differential diagnosis of patients with an inexplicable failure to adhere to a plan of care.
