AB  - The COVID-19 crisis has affected not only the health care system but also the economy and many other aspects of life. Children are the most vulnerable and affected groups and so are children with autism spectrum disorder (ASD). In many countries, social distancing measures have caused disruption on school- and center-based autism intervention, for example, Italy, the Philippines, and Vietnam. Some types of remote interventions have been proposed to provide continuous intervention for this group of children. Besides the negative impacts, we notice an unexpected positive effect during this period of time in Vietnam. The halting of the operation of nonessential businesses, the limitation of unnecessary travel, and teleworking allow parents to spend more time with their children with ASD. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2022 APA, all rights reserved)
