AB  - Frequent emergency department (ED) users-those using the ED four or more times during a given year- account for only 4.5-8.0 percent of all ED patients but account for 21.0-28.0 percent of ED visits, which makes them a focus for policy makers and providers. Compared to nonfrequent ED users, frequent users have multiple medical needs and high rates of concomitant mental health and substance use disorders, which results in frequent hospitalizations that are a marker for severe disease. Many interventions are focused on preventing potentially adverse outcomes such as ED revisits and hospital admissions. However, little is known about frequent ED users' risk of mortality. We undertook the first study of mortality among frequent ED users, accounting for deaths both in and out of the hospital by linking multiple years of state health services utilization and death data. Our goal was to estimate the association between past (up to six years) frequent ED use and death within seven days, ninety days, and two years (exhibit 1). We found that nonelderly patients' frequent ED use in the past year was predictive of mortality in the short term (within seven days [odds ratio: 1.25; 95% confidence interval: 1.07, 1.45]) and the longer term (within two years [OR: 2.48; 95% CI: 2.39, 2.56]), even when observable factors such as patient demographic characteristics, insurance status, and Elixhauser comorbidities were controlled for.
