AB  - Compares the drinking patterns & problems of Cubans, Mexican Americans, Puerto Ricans, & other Hispanics in the US, using interview data from the 1993 National Household Survey on Drug Abuse (total N = 4,462 adults, ages 18+). Analyses using SUDAAN reveal significant differences across the Hispanic groups in their drinking patterns & problems. Among men, Mexican Americans report the most frequent & heavy drinking, the greatest prevalence of drunkenness & alcohol-related problems. Cubans report the lowest %s, & Puerto Ricans & other Hispanics are in between the other two groups. For women, fewer ethnic differences are evidenced than for men. In general, Puerto Ricans & Mexican Americans drink more often & heavily & experience more problems than the other groups. Even after controlling for predictors of alcohol use, ethnic differences in drinking persist. 5 Tables, 42 References. Adapted from the source document.
