AB  - In trying to examine how food deprivation influences health behaviors, this research study was carried out in Eshowe, a town set within a rural environment in KwaZulu Natal, South Africa. Ten focus groups were conducted in ten different tribal districts in Eshowe. A hunger scale questionnaire was administered to determine the level of food security among the 95 families represented in the focus groups. Focus group discussions concentrated on topics dealing with food production strategies, constraints, and challenges facing families in acquiring adequate food commodities. Questions asked at the focus groups were consistent with the tenets of the health belief model, which is based on the premise that individuals alter their behaviors when they perceive risks associated with a particular cause of action. The study revealed the drudgery facing rural families as they struggle to construct their livelihoods and produce food for themselves, the dull ache of deprivation, the ennui which saps their energies, despair, and hopelessness. Many families in Eshowe lack the resources necessary to enable them to acquire food commodities and they experienced chronic food shortages. Arduous conditions including drought and chronic poverty undermine the ability of families to access adequate food. The lingering and persistent hunger robs rural livelihoods of good health, and the hope to invest in a better future. About 85% of the households were food insecure, 6% were experiencing hunger, and 4% were food secure. In contrast to the premise of the health belief model, participants adopted harmful behaviors even though they clearly understood health risks associated with certain behaviors. The study also showed that even when drugs became readily available, hunger and starvation deterred participants from completing the course of medication for serious illness including tuberculosis. Thus, the removal of environmental constraints through comprehensive social and economic strategies which would enable families access to food and other necessary resources should be a priority in helping these families practice positive health behaviors. Intervention programs must consider the social structure and physical environment surrounding individuals that influence their health, and need to be tailored to influence the most relevant level, especially the family.
