AB  - A task-centered group approach for treating alcohol abuse among culturally marginal American Indians -- those who identify with elements of both tribal & non-Indian cultures -- is presented. It recognizes that Indians must often cope with conflicting values, views their alcohol use as means for short-term coping, & considers task-centered treatment more useful than a personality-focused approach, keeping in mind the dominant Indian values of: a strong sense of responsibility to family, kinship group, & tribe; belief in group & family reciprocity; perception of a holistic, interrelated world; & preference for pragmatic action. Stages of the group therapy include: (1) development of rapport with the leader; (2) discussion through storytelling; (3) specification of group tasks congruent with their values; & (4) identification of individual tasks linked to the group effort. This approach gives members considerable autonomy in stating the problems & solutions rather than having a social work intervention prescribed for them. Modified HA
