AB  - Objective: To describe the development and implementation of a community-based educational support group after TBI. Design(s): Descriptive narrative. Setting(s): Community. Participants (or Animals, Specimens, Cadavers): Adults with TBI. Intervention(s): Increasingly, brain health and mental well-being are recognized as factors contributing to recovery and community re-integration after TBI. Our team sought to design and implement an education-based support group, with a focus on brain health and mental well-being. Main Outcome Measure(s): Not applicable. Result(s): The Brain Health Group (BHG) is a 12-month, 22-session program in which adults with TBI meet in a group setting. Care Partners and loved ones are encouraged to attend. The focus of the BHG is to provide education regarding TBI and overall brain health through instructional sessions, group discussion, structured tasks and an app to send participants health tips and reminders. Topics discussed include: (1) understanding TBI and recovery, (2) setting achievable goals for recovery, (3) dealing with anxiety, depression and fatigue, (4) improving brain health, (5) cognitive changes, (6) return to work and community re-integration, (7) role changes, (8) gratitude, and (9) communication, intimacy, and relationships. Additionally, participants are encouraged to develop short and long-term goals and to make regular entries in a gratitude journal, which is provided by the BHG team. Conclusion(s): An education-based support group with an emphasis and focus on brain health and mental well-being can allow individuals with TBI to understand their brain anatomy and injury, develop strategies to cope with the stresses of life after TBI, and recover their sense of purpose. Future research should examine the impact of participation on self-efficacy, depression, and satisfaction with life. Author Disclosure(s): The contents of this abstract were developed under a grant from the National Institute on Disability, Independent Living, and Rehabilitation Research (NIDILRR grant number 90DPTB0013). NIDILRR is a center within the Administration for Community Living (ACL), Department of Health and Human Services (HHS). The contents of this abstract do not necessarily represent the policy of NIDILRR, ACL, HHS, and you should not assume endorsement by the Federal Government. Key Words: Brain Health, Mental Well-Being, Traumatic Brain Injury, Support GroupCopyright © 2019
