AB  - Surveyed the attitudes of four specific user groups toward the quality and effectiveness of the presentence investigation report (PSI) in the state of Utah. These four groups were judges, prosecuting attorneys, public defenders, and probation/parole officers who supervise offenders (all aged 31–50 yrs). 227 questionnaires concerning attitudes toward the PSI (response rate 60%) were returned. Nearly half the respondents did not read the entire report. Respondents favored offense-based sections over those involving offender characteristics such as physical and mental health). The sections ranked highest by users included factors related to the current offense, the offender's culpability in the crime, harm done to the victim, and risk assessment characteristics. In significantly large numbers, users expressed concerns about the accuracy of the presentence report. Recommendations to improve the quality and usability of the PSI are included. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
