AB  - Describes the model of treatment used at a British hospital, run as a therapeutic community for 29 adults with personality disorders, most of whom are criminal offenders. Treatment is voluntary, and problems center on meaningful engagement and maintenance in therapy. Admission is accomplished via a selection interview in a group setting with residents and staff. The model includes a formal patient hierarchy, a rigid timetable for the weekly routine, general rule enforcement by residents, a daily community meeting, procedures for response to serious incidents, and a disengaging process. Components of the model that are generalizable to other settings include patient involvement in the treatment process and staff structures that are sufficiently flexible and that avoid overly hierarchical and authoritarian styles. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
