AB  - Therapeutic Case Report. Objective(s): A unique case report to demonstrate addiction in a pharmacist through the use of buprenorphine/ naloxone film for the self-prescribed treatment of migraine headaches. Case Summary: A 35 year old female hospital pharmacist is admitted to treatment for opioid use disorder for using buprenorphine/ naloxone film to self-medicate her migraine headaches. After daily use of sublingual buprenorphine/naloxone, and several failed attempts to discontinue use, the pharmacist was admitted to a partial hospitalization treatment program. She was prescribed sumatriptan subcutaneous injection for her migraines while maintaining buprenorphine/naloxone abstinence. Upon completion, the pharmacist transitioned to the aftercare program where she maintains sobriety and uses her story to help aid in other patients' recoveries at the treatment center. Discussion(s): Addiction and substance abuse affect a substantial number of healthcare professionals. Pharmacists are particularly vulnerable to prescription drug misuse and addiction as a result of their direct access and vast pharmacologic knowledge. . This case is a story of rehabilitation and recovery of a pharmacist who has a desire to return to the practice of pharmacy through the use of effective pharmacological and behavioral interventions. Review of Literature: MEDLINE and GALILEO searches revealed no published case reports of Buprenorphine/Naloxone addiction. Cases may be underreported, leading to a lack of existing data. Conclusion(s): In conclusion, the patient in this case successfully completed six weeks of outpatient treatment in summer of 2010. After being discharged from primary treatment, the patient transitioned to the aftercare program where she participated in mirror imaging - a method where the patient played the role of counselor to combat the shame and embarrassment of overcoming addiction. The patient reported that mirror imaging served as reminder of where she used to be and saw similarities in others' experiences, thus prompting her to provide insight and advice to current patients. She contributed to group therapy sessions as if she was still a patient and was cognizant of modeling behavior that set a reachable example for other patients. She hoped that soon she would have her license reinstated and returned to the practice of pharmacy.
